      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CRY FROM MACEDONIA. Sacred Concert in the Parr Hall. RESOLUTION OF PROTEST.</head>
        <p>THE CRY FROM MACEDONIA. Sacred Concert in the Parr Hall. RESOLUTION OF PROTEST.</p>
        <p>An enjoyable sacred concert, the proceeds of which were in aid of the Macedonia Relief Fund, was held in the Parr Hall on Sunday evening. There was a fair attendance, Councillor James Dodds occupying the chair. An admirable programme was submitted and met with cordial appreciation. Mr. Caradog Roberts, F.R.C.0., A.R.C.M., L.R.A.M., contributed a number of organ solos in masterly style. He showed a perfect command over the instrument, and his playing was marked by brilliance of execution and full attention to expression. Miss Gwladys Roberts, R.AIM. is the possessor of a rich contralto voice of splendid volume andshe gave feeling interpretations of "I will give you rest" (Cowen), and "Light" (Clement Tocknane). Mr. Seth Hughes, R.C.M. (tenor) was heard to distinct advantage in "Be thou faithful" (Mendelssohn), and "Total eclipse" (Handel); and Miss Annie Hughes, R.A.M.. played harp solos with taste and skill. The concluding item was the duet "Children pray" (Sphor), in which the voices of Miss Gwladys Roberts and Mr. Seth Hughes blended with fine effect. During an interval, The CHAIRMAN said he neednot tell them thatlhe was in entire sympathy with the object of the gathering. He did not think many of them had come solely to hear the/music. Their main object,he thought was to express sympathy with the poor Macedonians—such of them as were left—in the position inhvhich they found themselves. He had been contrasting the position of those who were present that night with that of the poor refugees— children, women and old men—among the hills and mountains of Macedonia. Councillor Dodds proceeded to read an article by a "Daily News" correspondent describing the shocking state of affairs and, continuing, observed that he should say the tamest heart in Christen- dom would be anxious that the Turk should no longer be allowed to carry on his terrible work. They were, he considered, also presentlthat night to protest against the Christian:countries of Europe allowing the massacres to go'on anyronger. (Applause.) If England had doneher duty in 1876,7-8 at Berlin,whenLordßosebery could not lead us to do it, and if England did her duty now the oppression might easily be stopped. In the last great speech made by the great man whose life had just been issued—Mr. Gladstone—at Liverpool when he was 86 years of age he. said that England ought to take up the work herself. If England would take up the work of clearing the Turk out of Europe, he said, even if other nations were jealous of us and tried to stop us, we ought at any rate to make a pro- test even if we had to go back when we had started a war. He thought Mr. Gladstone was right when he said that if England had taken up that position the other nations of Europe would have been shamed into taking the matter up, and long since the Turk would have been driven out of Europe, and such scenes as he had been reading of could not have occurred. It was a sad state of things that in this 20th Christian century humanity should treat humanity in that way. But these Turks never learnt; they never got away from their brutality. It seemed to him that the government ought at once to take such steps as might be necessary to rid Europe of this pest which had for many centuries been the greatest opponent of freedom in our own continent and throughout the world. He hoped that apait from party and other differences the English people would come to a decision that once for all this kind of thing must be stopped and that the time they were all longing for "when man and man the world o'er shall brother's be " would come as please God it might, if they but did their duty. (Applause). The Rev. J. S: HUGHES pointed out that that was the first time he .had appeared on the platform at a sacred concert on Sunday but he was very pleased to be present not simply because the object was a noble one, but also because the singing and playing that night had been in perfect harmony with the high truths which some of them had been endeavouring to enforce during the day. If they spent the remainder of their Sunday evenings oftener in that way,—with some object for good in view—he thought they would be serving their country and their God as well. He moved "That this meeting tenders to our fellow Christians in Macedonia its sympathy with them in -their sufferings and calls upon his Majesty's Govern- ment to take immediate steps to put an end to the um- Christian and cruel Government of the Turkish empire and to insist upon reforms that will be in harmony with civilized Government." He observed that a similar resolution was passed at Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel and forwarded to Mr. Balfour. He receivedan acknowledg- ment enclosing the letter which the Prime Minister had forwarded to the papers recently. It would seem from that letter that the Government did not intend to take any action. He felt very grieved and he believed that if meetings of that character could be multiplied and the sentiments of the nation given free expression to, and a thorough protest made against those cruelties, there would be a change in the attitude of the Govern- ment. It was that change which they desired and prayed for. When he had read of the horrible things that had been done he had exclaimed in the quietude of his study :4" Oh! for an hour of Gladstone,!" to go like a flaming torch throughout this country again as he did during the Bulgarian atrocities and as he protested during the Armenian massacres. The least they could do was to express their sympathy with their fellow Christians in Macedonia and to knock at the door of Downing-street,until the Prime Minister would listen to the cry of Christians in England appealing for their outraged brethren in Macedonia. (Applause.) The Rev. J. L. MILNE seconded. He laid a great part of the blame for the massacres on ourselves. He believed that if we as a Christian people in years gone by had done our duty these atrocities would never have occurred. It was a shame to our country and to the Government that such -a resolution should have to be put, and he trusted that many other resolutions passed throughout the country would waken the Government to its duty. (Applause.) The resolution was unanimously carried. The artistes who kindly gave their services, were cordially thanked.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Stretton's Contribution to the Fund.</head>
        <p>Stretton's Contribution to the Fund.</p>
        <p>With the laudable object of assisting the Macedonia Relief fund two threatrical performances were given in Stretton schoolroom on Monday afternoon before a large audience. The plays presented were a one-act drama entitled" Our bitterest foe "and an original farcical comedy in one act entitled "Browne with an E." In the first named the characters were :—General von Rosenberg (a Prusian commander), Viscount Tiver- ton ; Henri de In Fere (an officer in the French army), Mr. Leicester Warren; and Blanche d'Evran (his 49onsin), Miss Dorothy Lyon. The farce was taken part in by the following :—Graf von Donnerwetter- Tjeberunterhinterberg, Mr. Mainwaring ; Mr. Robert Brown, Mr. J. Egerton-Warburton; Monsieur Vinfran Parjour, Colonel Alured Brooke; Mrs. Gushington Nervesby, Miss Cicely Thorpe; Mrs. Robert Browne, Miss Florence Lyon ; and Miss Excelsiora J. Peak, Miss Honor Vansittart. Each play was admirably presented and reflected great credit upon the performers. Colonel Brooke discharged the duties of stage manager. During the afternoon the orchestra gave selections, Lady Mary Pepys recited, and ITESS Dorothy Cross rendered a slave song. A violin solo was contributed by Miss Allan,and Viscount Tiverton appearedin a capital character sketch. It is hoped to hand over about £2O to the Balkans Committee in aid of the survivors of the massacres in Macedonia.</p>
        <p>In two inquests held on Tuesday by the City Coroner (Mr. E. A. Gibson) the cause of death was set clown as excessive drinking of alcoholic 'liquor. One case was that of a married woman named Simmonds, forty years old, who lived in Red Bank. The other was a pensioner named Butler, aged sixty-five. It was said that when he drew his pension he spent it all in drink. The Coroner said he did not know whether the jury ought not to return a verdict of suicide when people drank themselves to death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The King is paying one or two country house visits i before the return of the Queen who s not expected back from Denmark until the end of the month. His Majesty's visit to Lord and Lady Londonderry at Wyn- ward Park is not the first time he has been the guest of one of the most charming of hostesses, who has on several occasions entertained Royalty. The hospitality offered to the Emperor and Empress of Germany in 1891, at Londonderry House was one of the great events of the yearitheir present Majesties—then the Prince and Princess of Wales, being amongst the guests. A Stately Home.—Wynward with it 2,000 acres, and the still more valuable property the Londonderry collieries, were brought into the family by the wife of the third Marquis. The house is a very large one, and the gardens, and the Home Farm are the especial care of Lady Londonderry. She is a firm believer in the doctrine that "if you want anything well done, you must do it yourself,' and she has effected much good in the colliery districts of Durham by establishing institutions and taking an active interest in works of public utility. The King's Youngest-Niece.—Princess Ena of Batten- berg, the youngest of the Royal Princesses of her own generation, will be sixteen on the 24th of this month, and is already looking forward to making her formal entry into society. As an only daughter, she his spent most of her time in the company of her mother, Princess Henry of Battenberg, who has given the greatest care and attention to her education. Princess Henry was the only Royal lady in the family circle of the late Queen Victoria, who had any liking for athletics, but her daughter is more inclined to drawing-room accom- plishments than to outdoor games. .Music, acting, singing, and dancing, are her special attractions, and she is a great favourite with her godmother, the Empress Eugenie, from whom she takes her second name. Dancing.—With the advent of winter, dancing once more becomes one of themost agreeable forms of social enjoyment. It is a favourite diversion, with all classes, from the palace to the village and within proper limits, it is a rational and fascinating form of amusement. Many hostesses complain that men are not so keen on dancing as they used to be, and that in recent years dances have suffered from rival attractions, such as the crazes for ping pong and bridge.—There does not seem this winter to be any particular drawing-room game of dominating interest. Ping pong has gone out, and the attempts to introduce fresh table ball games do not promise to be very successful. Dancing in any case is not likely to go out of fashion. It has survived for many centuries, and is likely to be as popular as ever this winter. Lady Rosslyn, whose financial difficulties were recently made public, has always been -looked upon as a clever woman of business, and is understood to have made a good deal of money by her dealings in house property in London. She would buy up houses that had fallen into a neglected and tenantless condition, and, by fitting them up in a modern and artistic manner, was generally able to 'dispose of them at a profit. Of late, however, Lady Rosslyn has been prevented by illness from attending to her affairs, and her eldest son' the present Lord Rosslyn, has not been altogether a finan- cial success. She was twice married, first to Mr. Maynard, and secondly to the late Lord Rosslyn, and her daughters, Lady Warwick, the Duchess of Suther- land, Lady Algernon Gordon-Lennox, Lady Westmor- land, and Lady Angela Forbes, are all well married. Winter Materials.—Dress materials this winter are all of the more substantial and heavier variety. Velvet and velveteen will be very generally worn, the latter fabric being now produced to such perfection as to leave noth- ing more to be desired. Corduroy velveteen is much used for " trot-about" costumes, but whether or not it is from old plebian associations, this fabric always seems to lack a refined appearance. Brown—not too dark—is usually the color chosen for these velveteen outdoor suits, the basqued coat having one or two small shoulder capes. Hats and Toques.—Most of the new millinery tends towards heaviness both as regards weight and style. There is the Rembrandt chapean, and again the all- round picture hat, built of velvet, and shewing as trim- ming sweeping plumes. Such headgear, together with the large fiat capelines of beaver, with full beefeater crowns of panne, are among the most difficult to wear gracefully. However diligently one may endeavour to fix such hats to the head, their tendency is to wobble, and reach an unbecoming angle, when they give to their wearers anything but a modish appearance.—The latest toques are smaller, but like hats, are heavy and distinctly over-trimmed. Marabout pompons, birds, wings, and richly coloured velvet foliage, are the favourite forms of trimming, the latter being little if any less expensive than the . huge ostrich plumes, and most certainly more fragile wear. Triumphs in Furs.—There would appear to be little the furrier cannot do with anything in the shape of a skin, be it even that only of the common or garden cat. Rabbit skin, once upon a time employed solely for linings, is now by the process of shaving, converted into a beautiful white fur, not unlike ermine. Squirrel skin he converts into sable or blue fox, or rather a very good semblance of these furs, whilst musgnash become by dyeing mink, sable, or skunk, by shaving moleskin, and by the double process of shaving and dyeing, beaver or sealskin. And decidedly the up-to-date furrier is as economical as he is clever, for out of the tiny pieces of fur taken from the heads or very small cuttings he forms what one may call, large patchwork lengths, from which coats are being cut. The appearance of such garments is particularly good, and needless to say they are by no means as costly as coats cut from the whole skins. The Sternutatory Warning.—Sneezing is nearly always one of the first warnings of a cold. That simple fact is sufficiently well known to put to ridicule the theory of an eminent doctor who once said that sneez- ing was a sign of robust health. No person in poor health, he said, was ever known to sneeze. One would be more inclined to doubt if there was ever a person who lived to grow up, who was not in some degree familiar with the act of sneezing. It has, in all ages, been regarded as of evil portent, and was looked upon as one of the signs of the plague. We do not regard a sneeze so seriously, though there are some people who look upon a bad cold as only one degree better than the 'Plague itself, and in any case one cannot be tea careful of coughs and colds which so often come with the October rains and gales. Autumn Leaves.—Flowers of some or other kinds can generally be obtained all the year round for table and drawing room decoration; but just now there is nothing better to take the pla,cd of summer blooms than the beautiful "autumn leaves" with their wealth of br-illiant and flaming colours. The creepers this year are unusually bright andl gay with autumn tints, and the leaves make a charming decoration deftly arranged as a table centre and garnitures for dessert dishes. They can also be utilised for the decoration of rooms, enabling one to eke out with comparatively few flowers, now fast disappearing from our gardens and becoming more expensive to buy. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NARROW ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>A NARROW ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>The Marsh, Wellington, near Hereford, furnishes a story which will be of unusual interest to many. Mr. William Pritchard, was very low with Dropsy and Rheumatism and was cured at the last moment. He says : 'Three years ago7l suffered with Dropsy and Rheu- matism. My body swelled and filled with water; my breath was short ; I had cramps in my muscles and limbs, dark circles under my eyes, bloated face in the morning, fitful sleep and bad dreams. "I tried all sorts of medicines, but they did me no good. I had to give up my work and golunder a doctor's care. He advised me to go into an hospital, which I did, and there underwent seventeen weeks treatment,and was tapped seven times. "When I came out I was as bad as ever, and then my wife persuaded me to try Dodd's ;Kidney Pills. After the first few doses I began to feel the water leaving mo. It was such a relief that I kept on with the Pills. Now there is no swelling. I have no aches or pains and I feel like a different man. Dodd's Kidney Pills saved my life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>it eA, okb kko4 "*VEAKADINE .0- • THING – soto • 1</head>
        <p>it eA, okb kko4 "*VEAKADINE .0- • THING - soto • 1</p>
        <p>BY HARRIS BUR</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXVIII.</p>
        <p>JAMES HEATHERBUTr.</p>
        <p>"WATER " repeated the wretched man trying to scramble over the rock, and falling back in his weakness. " Water !"</p>
        <p>His voice was harsh and broken, and hi tongue lolled slightly out of his mouth, like the tongue of a thirsty dog. Tredegar looked at him as though he had been some wild anftnal suddenly endowed with the gift of speech. Two brown hairy arms were thrust out through the opening, as if in supplication 4 Then the shaggy head of hair sank down between them, and the fingers were clasped together, and Tredegar heard a long moan of pain.</p>
        <p>- He was so astounded at the reappearance of the man he had flung out to his death that for two or three minutes he was quite unable to clearly think out a proper course of action. His first instinct was to kill, to climb over the barrier and throttle the miserable wreck of humanity to death, to drag the body back into the recesses of the tunnel, where it would never be found, and to close, once and for all, as he thought he had already closed, the life-book of James HeatherbuttJ</p>
        <p>But as he saw the clasped hands, and heard the piteous appeal for water, he knew that such a course had now been rendered im- possible. The horror of the whole situation lay now in the fact that the man's reason had been restored to him, and that he had once more spoken in a human voice, This moan- ing object before him was, no wild beast, but a man calling out to one of his fellows for a draught of water. It seemed possible to Tredegar that the violence of the combat had accomplished this extraordinary change, and that when he had beaten the mad brain into insensibility he had also broken down the crust of darkness that lay upon it, and let in the light of reason. The whole position was too horrible to con- template. He only knew one thing, that he could not kilt the wretched creature before him. He could not even let it die behind the grey barrier of rock. It would have been simple to do this. Heatherbutt was now in the last stages of exhaustion. He could not even climb out on to the shore. In another twenty-four hours he would, in all prob- ability, ' perish of thirst and hunger. But Tredegar could not even do this. He had not hesitated to murder the wild beast" in cold blood. But he could not even leave the " man " to die a natural death. The human voice, the human hands clasped in supplication, forbade all thought of such a thing. Tredegar resolved to help Heather- butt and leave the consideration of the future till a time when he could think more clearly, and find some possible solution to the nume- rous difficulties that confronted him.</p>
        <p>"I will get you water," he said, and he almost started at the sound of his own voice. It seemed so strange to be speaking to the thing before him. It was like talking to a wolf that had just been trying to devour</p>
        <p>He made his way out into the open, and finding an old tin can on the track, filled it with water, and brought it back to Heather- butt. The wretched , man drank copiously, nd then closed his eyes with a sigh of con- tent, and once more his head sank down on his arms:</p>
        <p>"Are you hungry ? " Tredegar said, abruptly: For answer Heatherbutt stretched out one hand. Tredegar shuddered. The fingers still looked like the claws of a wild beast. He drew out a large piece of bread from his knapsack and placed it in the outstretched palm. Heatherbutt devoured it ravenously, biting out large pieces with his yellow, broken teeth, Tredegar had knocked half of them down his throat.</p>
        <p>When he had finished, Tredegar helped him over the barrier of rocks; and the two men sat down a couple of yards from each other on tbe shingle inside the cave. Neither of them spoke. Tredegar filled his pipe. Heatherbutt blinked his eyes at the daylight, which was reflected from the sunlit beach out- side. He seemed half dazed. He looked more horrible than ever in the light of day, and the intelligence which had returned to his eyes only accentuated the brutality of his whole appearance. He had been terribly mauled in the combat with Tredegar. His great mouth was almost toothless. The hair on his face was caked with dried blood. The back of his head was one dark red patch. Is arms and legs were scarred with gashes, and his bare throat marked with the prints of the Welshman's fingers. His clothes were still wet, and resembled the fur of a drowned animal.</p>
        <p>Tredegar lit his pipe, and the fragrant smoke curled across the cave to Heatherbutt's nostrils.</p>
        <p>"Tobacco r he said, in a grating voice</p>
        <p>"Yes," fredegar 'answered, holding out his pouch. Then he remembered that the man had no pipe. Heatherbutt, however, snatched the pouch, and, taking out half an ounce of tobacco, crammed it into his mouth and chewed it.</p>
        <p>"I'll lend you my pipe," Tredegar said. "This'll do," the man answered; and he rolled the bitter morsel round his mouth as a boy might do who wished to get the full flavour of a chocolate. "Wrecked. eh ?" said Heatherbutt, after a long pause. What ship? And what the hell is this place? I wonder what has hap- pened to those devils."</p>
        <p>What devils ?" asked Tredegar. He saw that Heatherbutt imagined he was still on the ,island of spiders, and that he (Tredegar) had been also c :Ist away on its inhospitable shores.</p>
        <p>"The Walroye.s," continued Heatherbutt. "May God curse their souls! I trust they are deep at the bottom of the sea !" ";They are dead 1" Tredegar answered. He knew now that he had much to tell this man, and that it would be very hard to tell.</p>
        <p>"How do you know they are dead ? " growled Heatherbutt, clenching his hairy fists. "Have they returned to the island ?'</p>
        <p>Tredegar hesitated for a minute. Then he made up his mind to face the situation. • "This is no island," he replied, "but the coast of Cardiganshire, in Wales." "The coast of Wales," repeated Heather- butt, slowly, and looking round with a dazed expression in his eyes. '" Why, the treaure ! Let me see !" _ .</p>
        <p>And, rising to his feet, he stumbled up the bank of shingle and crawled on to the beach. Then he saw the broad panorama of sea, and bay, and a single glance showed him that he was in no tropical climate. He passed his hand over his forehead and tried' to think. It seemed to him that he had seen all this before, but darkly as in a dream. Tredegar came out to his side. "You must oome back !" he said, sternly. "Your life is in danger! They are hunting for you." And, taking him by the arm, he forced him back into the cave. "I do not understand," Heatherbutt replied in a feeble rasping voice. "How did I get here, and why are they hunting for me ? " And yet as he asked this question he seemed to have the recollection of some horrible dream, in which he was for ever killing and escaping from death. Again Tredegar hesitated. "I would rather not tell you," he said after a pause. " I will assist you as far as lies in my power to escape. I may even be able to provide you with money. I would rather you take irts, simple word- that if you are eau-gilt) you will be hanged or shut up in a lunatic asylum for the rest of your life.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>LAND, AUTHOR OF .DA COBRA", ETC.</p>
        <p>"I have been mad then," replied Heather. butt. "I knew it. I knew I was going mad. I have—tell me all. I insist upon it—the whole truth," and then once more the light of madness began to blaze in his eyes. "The Walroyds are dead," Thdegar ,said significantly.</p>
        <p>-The man's brutish face was illuminated with a glow of triumph. "Have J killed them ? " he asked eagerly. _ _ _</p>
        <p>'Have I killed them " "You have killed them."</p>
        <p>"Thank God," he cried, "they deserved to die. Whatever I have done in my madness lies at their door. They tried to kill /tie, but they only succeeded in killing my reason. Their cruelty has recoiled on their own heads. I hope they died in agony." Tredegar shuddered. The brutality of the beast was rising once more in Heatherbutt's unbalanced mind.</p>
        <p>"You must leave thi country at once," he said sternly. "If you do not promise to do so, I will kill you here with my own hands. If you knew all, you would kill yourself." "It is upon their heads," replied Heather- butt, "the blood is upon their heads. Tell me everything. I shall feel no pangs of conscience. It is good to know that one has killed murderers. I have been an instrument in the hands of God—in the hands of God," and he sank down to the shingle, laughing wildly. Tredegar saw that he was still far from sane, and feared another outbreak of madness. However, he resolved to tell him all.</p>
        <p>He told the story as briefly and simply as possible, omitting all the horrible details, but impressing strongly on his hearer that the latter was a hunted man, and must leave the country immediately.</p>
        <p>When he had finished, Heatherbutt burst into a long peal of laughter.</p>
        <p>"So it is you that have suffered," he cried, "and yet you want to get me out of the country." "What do you mean ? " said Tredegar sharply. "Why, when I have gone, you will have no chance of proving your innocence. I shall be regarded as a myth, as an invention of your own brain. An imaginary scapegoat for your own sins."</p>
        <p>" Yet you must go," Tredegar replied. He was thinking of Cynthia. At any cost she must not meet this man. He owed that at least to her. It would be a part payment of his debt.</p>
        <p>"I shall be only too glad to go," Heather- butt replied. "I shall not take a lot of persuasion. But you ? " Tredegar shrugged his shoulders. He was prepared by now to take what blows fate might deal him. She bad dealt him many hard buffets during the last few months, but none so hard as the loss of Mavanwy's love. Now that the first joy of his return to light and air was over he began to realise that all the future' was darkness.</p>
        <p>"I take my chance," he replied grimlk: But you must go." "I am as innocent as you," Heatherbutt said hoarsely. "I am innocent. It is they who are guilty—those two dead men. They made me what I am. The blood is on their heads. You do not know. You cannot understand what I have suffered."</p>
        <p>"I know part of your story," Tredegar replied; "I had it all from Walroyd's own lips—on oath. I know all the history of the treasure."</p>
        <p>"Have they found it? Have they found it ? " Heatherbutt broke in eagerly.</p>
        <p>"Yes," replied Tredegar: ." They found it. Did I not tell yo. so just now. They were rich men and--" "Part of it is mine," Heatherbutt cried fiercely. "I am a rich man." "You had better stay and claim it," Tredegar said drily. "I have no doubt the Government-"</p>
        <p>"No, no. only give me enough to live on. It is yours, and you have earned it. I will go. But Walroyd ? How much did he tell you ? "</p>
        <p>"All the story of the treasure, and how you and he and his brother were the sole survivors of the Wisahickon," and Tredegar briefly sketched out Walroyd's story. When he had finished, Heatherbutt crawled over the shingle and thrust his shaggy face close to Tredegar's ear.</p>
        <p>"Went mad, did I ? "he snarled. "Threw myself over the cliff, did I ? Ain't that a sweet fairy tale? And you believed him, eh ? "</p>
        <p>"I was sure he was lying," Tredegar replied "Lying," cried Ileatherbutt, dashing his fist on the shingle. "Aye.. indeed, and hir portion is with the liars in hell. Listen Pc me. I will tell you the truth." "Up to a certain point," the man com- menced, " Walroyd did not lie to ybu, and it is perfectly true that from the very first 1 had my suspicion of the two brothers. Ido not think, however, that this was the outcome of insanity. On the contrary, as events turned out, I ought to have been more wary in my dealings with them. The life on the island, as you can guess, was intolerable. Quarrels were of every day occurrence, and I carried my life in my hands from day to day. It was fortunate for me that the Walroyds were, like myself, unarmed, and that physi- cally I was more than a match for the two of them.</p>
        <p>"One day the sullen jealousy and petty spite broke out into an open quarrel. I was determined to have part of the disc in my own keeping, and ensure the Walroyds' co- operation till .the treasure was -actually divided. I knew that they believed the words inscribed on it, and that they would do all in, their power to avoid the prophecy of Sorrow and death. I demanded half as my just right, and on being refused, did my best to secure it. The disc broke in the struggle, but I was victorious, and concealed my half in the sand, lest it should be taken from me while I slept. "I did not, however, size up the Walroyds correctly, or guess thp,t avarice might easily get the better of superstitious dread.</p>
        <p>" One morning a ship appeared on the hori7on and all day we sat round a great pile of smoking brushwood, and laughed and sang, as we saw it draw nearer to the shores of the island. In the delirium of joy, I was off my guard. I did not conceive it possible that anything could then come between me and freedom. It was clear that the ship had seen our signal and that it was coming to rescue us from the horrors of the island. I was frank, jovial, unsuspicious. We ate our last meal round the crackling blaze, and saw the white sails come out of the sunset like the wings of an angel from the glorious depths of heaven. Night came on, and the pyramid of fire leapt up and crackled ,in the darkness. We sat round and talked of the wealth that was to be ours. Then I fell asleep. "When I awoke it was broad daylight, my hands and feet were bound, a shallow flood of water was surging round me, and I saw :he hull of the ship low down upon the horizon In an instant I realised my situation. I was left to perish on the island, and the rising tide was even then half a foot deep about me. had been drugged, and consigned to a miser- able death. By some miscalculation on the part of the two brothers, I had awoke too soon. They had evidently intended that I should never wake again. I raised my wrists to my face, and bit savagely at the creepers which bAund them. A flood of salt water poured over me, and receded in a whirl of foam. I rolled over and over up the beach until I was out of reach of the tide. Then I gnawed through my bonds till I released my hands, and cut the bands off my feet with a broken piece .of shella A year afterwards I would have given much to have been fathoms deep under the waves I then so eagerly cheated of their prey.</p>
        <p>'No words ot mine can possibly describe to you the horrors of the newt three years: You yourself can imazine them, but you would probably find it difficult to describe them to _another person."</p>
        <p>" The woids carved in the cabin of the old ship ? " queried Tredeg-ai. "Were they yours ? "</p>
        <p>"They were mine. I carved them in my \ast few weeks of sanity. Every day I tried aloud to God, who had but little cause to love me. 'Who shall deliver me from the body of this death.' And I wrote the sentence large on the place where I dragged out my miserable existence. I unearthed the half disc from its hiding place and fastened it to my wrist. I had the consolation of thinking that if there was ought in the words inscribed an it, sorrow and death would pursue the Walroyds to whatever part of the world they had fled.</p>
        <p>"Day by day the inconceivable horrora of hat loathsome, island gathered round my brain like a cloud of poisonous vapours. I saw myself becoming as the beasts that perish. I -began to move about on all fours. I found myself one day fashioning a little web out of string, on another I crept about catching insects and putting them aimlessly in my mouth. Then the memory of these awful days fades into oblivion, or into a misty dream, of which I can remember nothing but the fact that I was filled with the lust of blood and that something whispered to me, 'Kill, kill, kill,' and that yet my whole soul desired to escape from the that en- compassed me. "I recollect nothing clearly till I found myself in darkness, with the water surging about me. I need tell you nothing of my fight for life, of the spar grasped blindly by a drowning man, of the escape through a hole in the roof, through which I floated into a tunnel in the rock, of the journey, sick and dizzy through the darkness, of the faint light of a lamp far ahead and the sound of human voices. You cannot comprehend what all this meant to one awaking from a long dream —the confusion, the despair, the fight for life, the ignorance of all that lay around me. But now yon know why I thank God that the \Valroyds are dead and that they have died by my hand." He ceased speaking. Tredegar looked at him in pity and wonder. The man's rude eloquence, wrung straight from his heart by the memory of a great' suffering and a great wrong, moved him as no story had ever moved him before. His own sufferings had been nothing to this. He at least had no blood upon his hands. He at any rate had no wife to share his misery, or to be dragged down into the last depths of shame. Heatherbutt's future would be as terrible as his past. Only death would release the victim of the most cruel wrong that had ever been perpetrated for the lust of gold. "You were married? " Tredegar said after a pause. Your wife ? "</p>
        <p>hea.therbutt 'buried his 'battered face in • his hairy hands. "My wife?" he said: "My wife ? " and there was almost a note of tenderness in his grating voice.</p>
        <p>"You deserted her in America," Tredeg.ar continued pitilessly. "It was for her own sake," Heatherbutt replied. " I saw that my very presence was degrading her to my own level. I have much on my conscience, but ,nothing blacker than my marriage with that loving and pure- minded woman. I made her life a hell: I left her, not because I had ceased to care for her, but because my conduct had made her cease to care for me, because my very touch was loathsome to her. Because she saw deep into my soul arid shrank from what she saw."_ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Then he lociked at his wrist and clenched his hands.</p>
        <p>" I always wore this bracelet she gave me," he continued. "Even on that island it was a companion to me—my sole link with the love that might have raised my vile nature from the slime in which it wallowed, if it had not been so heavy and sunk so deep. I always loved her. I love her still. I hope that she has found peace ancl happiness. Perhaps she is dead."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps," replied Tredegar slowly, "as you say, she is dead."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIX. HUSBAND AND WIFE.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIX. HUSBAND AND WIFE.</p>
        <p>MAVANWY made her way to Llanfihangel and took the first train to Trethol Junction. She returned among her own people as one who had risen from the dead. From the moment she arrived at the station till the time when she reached the gates of Llynglas, the was besieged with eager questions, and tier hand was wrung a hundred times by excited villagers. She refused, however, to give any information as to her escape from leath, beyor.d the bare fact that she had been :arried off by some terrible monster, and had managed to elude his clutches in the depths )f the old Tredegar lead mine. She only gave this much information in order to clear Emrys Tredegar of any connection with the affair, and to set the local tongues wagging in the right direction. She learnt that her lather had been buried the day before, and that a place had been left by his side to receive aer own body if ever it should be found.</p>
        <p>Directly he arrived at Llynglas she sent for the village constable, and persuaded him to send a telegram to the police sergeant who had assisted at the capture of Tredegar. The worthy man slightly resented this step as an insult to his own intelligence, but Mavanwy absolutely declined to make any full state- ment to him without the advice of his superior. fie had, however, the consolation of taking the news of Walroyd's- death to Garth, and of becoming himself a planet of considerable brilliance by virtue of reflected light.' When the sergeant arrived, Mava.nwy told him the whole story, only keeping back thu fact that Tredegar was still hiding in the place where they had escaped into the day- light. She wished to be quite sure that het lover was acquitted of the atrocious crimes laid to his charge before revealing his where- abouts. Sergeant Jones, however, a shrewd and kindly Welshman, guessed at the little secret that she hugged so close to her heart, and hastened to re-assure her on this point. He told her that Walroydt's statement about the murder of Dennis Riley had already con- firmed the suspicion that the police had formed ever since the death of David James—namely, that Tredegar was not responsible for any of the crimes which the newspapers and popular opinions had laid at his door. He told her. that even when Tredegar was arrested on the beach, they had in their possession clear evidence that he could not have been the murderer of David James, and that he was only arrested on the strong representation of John Walroyd, who deposed an oath that Tredegar had attempted his life, and had slain the detective who had followed him from London. The sergeant added that her own story would be confirmed at the earliest opportunity, and that probably the body of the real murderer would be recovered and brought forward as evidence in Tredegar's favour. He finally assured her that the latter could now deliver himself into the hands of the law without any fear of the consequences, and that it was in the interests of justice that he should do so without further delay.</p>
        <p>When he had gone, Mavanwy vient upstairs and put on the only black dress she possessed. Since Tredegar found her in the mine her thoughts had been all for her lover, and the loss of her father had been almost thrust out of her mind. But in the lonely house at Llynglas the past came back to her in a fior,d of memories. John Morgan had been a hard man, but in his own rough way he had loved his only child. Mavanwy went up to his empty bedroom and burst into tears. Then she fell on her knees beside the bed and prayed. In that hour she only remembered the best and truest part of her father's life, she only recalled the kindly words; the rest was blotted out from her mind; like the sundial, she only marked the sunny hours. • She rose to her feet and clasped her hands. Then she crossed over to the window, and drawing up the blinds, let in the sunshine. Then he gave a last look at the empty bed, and going softly from the room, closed the door and locked it.</p>
        <p>(To be concluded,)</p>
