      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>GARDEN CITY FROM BALDOCK,</head>
        <p>THE GARDEN CITY FROM BALDOCK,</p>
        <p>however, is the 'practical side of the enterprise, and this, as far as it has been possible to determine up to the present, was shown in speeches delivered by Earl Grey, Viscount Peel, Mr. W. H. Lever, of Port Sunlight, Mr. George Cadbury, of Bourneville, and others. The population Will be limited to about 30,000 people, a provision which at once does away with any prospect of overcrowding. In the actual town area not more than ten houses to the acre are to be allowed. The Garden City Company will be the ground landlords, leasing houses and plots for a period of ninety-nine years. :The profits of shareholders in the company are limited to a cumulative dividend of 5 per cent. per annum. All profits beyond this will be applied for the benefit of the town and its inhabitants. These benefits will take the form of libraries and concert halls, the laying out of parks and gardens, the introduction of electric tram- ways and lighting, and other conveniences and attractions. Schools and churches, according to the requirements of the inhabi- tants, are to be built by the company on land purchased at agricultural prices. Provision is made for cheap provisions and reasonable railway rates, and the liquor traffic will be under public control. Until public-houses are required by the people they are not be built; but the profits on any that may sub- sequently be erected are to be devoted to beautifying the town. Rents will be low, and a plentiful supply of water and motive power for manufacture is a feature of the schemes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>JOHN MORLEY AND HIS LIFE'S WORK. 1</head>
        <p>JOHN MORLEY AND HIS LIFE'S WORK. 1</p>
        <p>Mr. John Morley's life of William Ewart Gladstone marks an epoch in national bio- graphy. It was a great work, and it has been done in a great fashion, worthy of it: Had Mr. Morley no other claim to famed this monumental work would make his name a household word, It is the ideal</p>
        <p>of what a great biography</p>
        <p>shourd be:</p>
        <p>is a huge book of three large closely printed</p>
        <p>volumes,</p>
        <p>taming</p>
        <p>thousand</p>
        <p>pages, but it is without a single superfluous lineJ</p>
        <p>The material</p>
        <p>which Mr. John Morley had at his disposal was</p>
        <p>enormous, and</p>
        <p>it was a con-</p>
        <p>tinuous process</p>
        <p>of boiling:. down.</p>
        <p>To begin with, there were over</p>
        <p>two hundred ' and fifty thousand letters and documents in Manuscript to be gone through, as well as the grea t statesman's own diaries, blue books, speeches, and many other things. Indeed, the writing of such a life involved to all intents and purposes the writing of a modern history of England for over half a century; and the biographer traces the life of Mr. Glad- stone in full detail from Eton to Westminster Abbey. Of course, particular attention is paid to the crises of Mr. Gladstone's life, which were not infrequent, and the book shows clearly the troubles and difficulties of a prominent politi- cian, especially a Premier! The whole tone of Mr. Morley's work is repression, coupled with ardent admiration. He saw eye to eye with Mr. Gladstone on every point; but he writes with the true instinct of the historian and biographer in a non-party spirit as far as possible, as the late Queen in a letter to the author enjoined upon him as particularly desirable. Mr. Gladstone was a personality and a politician who aroused strong partizans and equally bitter opponents, and this great book, wrongly written, would not only have lost a great deal of its interest as history, but would also 'have recalled afresh many points of conflict with bitterness. It could have been entrusted to no safer or more brilliant hands.</p>
        <p>, , it t .4111k4 .., . , ....-...-- . 1 . il'' 1. .1-=, • !., li\r- f . "1</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN M ORLEY:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ROYAL HONEYMOON.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL HONEYMOON.</p>
        <p>- Prince and Princess Andrew of Greece are spending their honeymoon at Heiligenberg Castle, that magnificent pile which lies among the most delightful scenery the whole country can boast. The happy couple may be said to be doubly wedded, for, according to cus- tom. when the consistorial ceremony in the Lutheran Church had been. concluded, the Royal party drove in state to the Russian Church, and the marriage was solemnised</p>
