      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HEILIGENBERG CASTLE.</head>
        <p>HEILIGENBERG CASTLE.</p>
        <p>according to the Greek Catholic Church: Queen Alexandra and the Tsar were present at the ceremonies, and at the family dinner given afterwards there was a large gathering of Royalty. By a coincidence the ceremony took place at the time of arrival of Queen Alexandra at Darmstadt, and her Majesty was welcomed at the station by a large number of distinguished persons who had gathered for the Royal wedding. One of the first to greet the Queen was the Tsar, who kissed her Majesty's hand. Among others on the platform were the bride and bridegroom, Prince Louis of Battenberg, and Prince Henry of Prussia. The wedding cake of this historic union was befittingly a wonderful piece ot the confectioner's arts It measured nearly six feet high, and was constructed in three tiers, covered with pure white sugar ivy leaves, with a wreath of ivy round the base. At . the top was a cluster of .'orange blossom, while long trails of ivy hung gracefully clowna</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>HOCKBY.—It one firm of manufacturers more than another keeps abreast of the times in the matter of Implements for dport. that firm is Geo. G. Bassey &amp; Co.. of London, who are leading the way again with their Hockey Clubs and Balls which bear their well-known trade mark, Ask the local Sports Dealer for G. G. B. &amp; Co.'. Special Hockey List. If not procurable apply to 36 &amp; 38 Queen Victoria Street, London. Factory -Peckham, S.B. (64).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>COUNTY TRIAL MATCH AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PROBABLES v. POSSIBLES.</p>
        <p>In order to select the team to represent Lancashire in their county match against Cheshire, a trial mateh took place on Monday on the Wileers- pool enclosure between teams representing the Probables and Possibles. The 12-aside game was played. There was only one alteration in the sides originally selected, J. Baxter (Rochdale Hornets) taking the place of S. James (Broughton Rangers) at half-back among the Probables, while for Baxter, who should have played for the Possibles, Lally (Widnes) was substituted. Prior to the match a heavy deluge of rain fell, and this no doubt affected the attendance which did not exceed 3,000. The going was a trifle heavy, and with the ball greasy slips were very frequent, and it was with diffi- culty that the players maintained a footing. The Possibles started towards the pavilion goal, and ' a sharp forward movement gained the Probables' quarters. Baxter cleared slightly, and assisted by Smith, a complete raid was made on the railway goal. Lomas opened out the game, andthe ball being speedily transferred to Fish, that player tricked Leytham ana scored two minutes after the start, the time player also adding the goal. The Probables again asserted ' themselves, and Fish got away on the left, but facing Thomas he essayed to punt over the Oldham man's head, but kicked into his hands. The kick did not avail the Possibles much, for Baxter got going, and passing to Wilson, that player transferred to Hogg,who had an easy journey. Fish landed the extra points. Baxter worked the scrimmages exceptionally well, and serv- ing Lomas well, the Salford skipper tricked Leytham, and made another easy opening for Fish,who also added the goal. Further tries were scored by Heath, Wilson, and Lomas, and at half-time the soore was: Probables, 5 goals 6 tries; Possibles, nil. The game had been so one-sided that the winning backs were placed behind the losing forwards for the second half and a closer contest followed. Lomas was early to the fore with a couple of spirited runs whilst a fine dribbling movement overcame Thomas, but the final touch was just a shade too hard, and only a minor accrued. Dickenson in tackling Hogg, was badly hurt, and had to retire, Frater taking the vacant place behind A desperate forward rush ended in Boardman (Leigh) completing the movement but Molyneux missed a somewhat easy place kick. A tangible record was soon forthcoming for the Possibles' forwards got fairly un- der weigh, and Lee rushed over near the posts for Lomas to miss the goal kick. Both goals were menaced, and ia quick succession each set of forwards displayed smart work. Lomas, Wilson, and Fish led up to the Warring- tonian scoring his third try, the scorer also adding his fourth goal. A really clever piece of work by Thomas negatived a fine effort by Wilson. The game ruled even after this, and nothing tangible accrued, and the final score reading as follows :—Probables, five goals seven tries (31 points) ; Possibles, one goal two tries (eight points). LANCASHIRE v. CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Following the trial match the County Committee met in the evening at the Patten Arms Hotel and selec- ted the following team to represent Lancashire against Cheshire at Broughton on October 28 :—Dan Smith (Salford), back; J. Fish (Warrington), R. Wilson and A. Hogg (Broughton Rangers), three-quarters ; J, Baxter (Rochdale Hornets), and J. Lomas (Salford), half-backs; P. Tunney, J. Rhapps' and G. Heath (Salford), J. Ferguson, and G. Frater(Oldham), and G. Boardman (Leigh), forwards. Reserves: R. L. Thomas (Oldham), back; Thomas (Oldham), Cl. Dickenson (Warrington), and J. Leysham (Lancaster), three-quarter backs • S. James (Broughton Rangers), half-back; A. Boardman (War- rington), H. Buckler (Salford), and J. R. Hilton (Wigan), forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>Hunslet were the visitors to Warrington on Saturday and they brought a strong team. About 5,000 spectators assembled and the turf being in excellent order some fast play was witnessed, in the opening stages mostly. Mr. Robinson (Manningham) was the referee, and the teams were as follow:</p>
        <p>Warrington—Hallam, back; Forster Dickenson,lsher- wood, and Fish, three-quarter backs; Davies and Mahoney, half backs • Boardman, Swift, Naylor, Morri- son, Thomas, Harmer:Edmondson' and Lunt, forwards. Hunslet—Place, back; Hannah, A. E. Goldthorpe, Whiteley, and W. Goldthorpe, three-quarter backs; Jackson and Everson, half 'backs; Shooter Wilson, Glew, Walsh, Uttley, Brookes, Wray, and Ramage forwards.</p>
        <p>A. Goldthorpe started, and the opening exchanges went in favour of the visitors. Mahoney and Davies opened out the game, and Dickenson looked a likely scorer until pulled up by W. Goldthorpe. The Hunslet forwards, ably led by Shooter and Glew, again invaded the Warrington quarters, and a series of quick transfers, between Jackson and Everson had the home backs in difficulties. Hunslet maintained the pressure and Jack- son outwitting Davies, he punted to Forster, Who mulled, and Jackson scored a soft try near the corner. A. Gold- thorpe failed at. goal. Following this success, the Hunslet forwards made strong play on the Warrington goal, only to find a substantial defence set up by Hallam and Fish. Shortly after, Warrington were penalised, and from the half-way line W. Goldthorpe kicked a goal. Warrington rallied after this, and the game was carried into Hunslet quarters. A fine passing movement ended in Fish making a bold but futile effort to cross at the corner, Place making a fine tackle; but a moment later Jackson was penalised at the pack, and Fish landed a goal. Immediately after, fine judgment by Isherwood gave Fish a further opportunity, and the Warrington crack equalised, the teams crossing over with 1 goal and 1 try each. Turning round, Hunslet were the first aggressive, and Jackson and Hannah were each held up in turn. Capital footwork by Dickenson and Thomas changed the venue, and Fish tried a long shot at goal. Place responded, and Goldthorpe almost lowered Warrington's citadel with a fine kick from beyond the half-way flag. Safety play was the order for a considerable time both sides making full use of touch. Near the close Hunslet attacked strongly the forwards rushing the ball over, and a confident appeal was made for Shooter scoring. Fish took the game to mid-field, and the end arrived with the score :—Warrington, 1 goal and 1 try (5 points) ; Hunslet, 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). RUNCORN v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS. • -</p>
        <p>About 4,000 spectators assembled on the Irwell Lane Ground to witness the meeting of the above teams I Walker started for Runcorn, and a scrummage ensued] in midfield. Phipps got possession, and had only to I pass Houghton to score, but the veteran brought him; l down. A moment later, however, the Rovers were awarded a free kick, and Carmichael placed a goal Resuming Runcorn attacked and a neat round of pass- ing between Jolley, Price, and Jones ended in the latter scoring. Jolley failed at goal. Guy,Barry,and Gordon got away with passing, but the latter was stopped when , well in the Runcorn half. From a penalty Carmichael almost landed another goal. Runcorn were' now forced to defend. The ball was kicked across the field to Gordon, who easily scored,the same player also improv- ing. Half-time score :—Hull Kingston Rovers 2 goals and 1 try (7 points), Runcorn 1 try (3 points). ' After the interval, Moran had a fine chance, butfailed to holdTthe ball. Butterworth, Price, and Jones gave a fine exhibition of passing, but Sinclair and Gordon saved. Jolley .got possession, but was stopped by several of the visitors, and he had to be carried off the field badly injured, and did not again resume. From a penalty kick Houghton added a goal for Runcorn. Guy then got away with a smart run, and scrummaging followed on the Runcorn line, but Walker relieved. just:before the finishiStarks got possession and scored a try for Hull, Carmichael failing at goal. The game was finely' contested throughout. Result --Hull Kings- ton Rovers, 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points) ; Runcorn, 1 goal and 1 try (5 points). Teams: Runcorn—Houghton, back ;• Jones Butterworth, Price, and Moran, three-quarter backs; Jolley and Richardson, half backs ; S. Walker Woods, Moulton, A Richardson, J. Walker, Tomlinson, and Williams, forwards. Hull Kingston Rovers—Carmichael, back; Phipps , Sinclair, Gordon, and Read, three-quarter backs; Guy land Barry, half backs; Starks, Kemp, Hotham, Windle, Taylor, 6olding, Pickering, and Townsley, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Renton (Hunslet).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LEIGH v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>LEIGH v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Played at Leigh, before 2,000 spectators. Widnes had the best of the opening play, their forwards proving stronger than Leigh in the scrummage. Pearson, hpw- ever, by a series of telling kicks into touch removed</p>
        <p>play to the Widnes end. Eventually the ball was passed to S. Lees, and from near the half-way he dropped a goal. Widnes were kept on the defensive, and after Molyneux and Lees had each had dropsat goal charged down, Molyneux dropped a goal, and Leigh crossed over with the useful lead of 4 points. In the second half Leigh went off with a rush. Eventually Bennett broke clean away and on reaching the full-back threw to Neville, who apparently had a certain try before him when he lost his footing and slid into touch. Tilley effected slight relief, but after another round of passing, started by Molyneux and taken up by Hilton and S. Lees, Neville scored an easy try, Molyneux failing at goal. Leigh missed another chance just afterwards, and then Widnes took play to Leigh quarter, where the Leigh backs foolishly started passing, with the result that Taylor cleverly intercepted and though challenged by Pearson got over near the posts,Tilley kicking a goal. Leigh eventually removed play to the centre, where Bennett got hold, and running right away from the Widnes backs scored a grand try near the corner.Molyneux failed at goal. Result :—Leigh,2 goals 2 tries (10 points), Widnes, 1 goal 1 try (5 point's). Leigh—Pearson, back; S. Johnson. H. Bennett (cap- tain), S. Lees, and R. Neville, three-quarter backs; J. Molyneaux and J. Hilton, half backs; G. Boardman. W. Roberts, J. A. Blackburn, J. Kelly, P. O'Neill, T.Arrow- smith, J. Riley, and E. Clare, forwards. Widnes—J. H.Tilley, back; R. Wailing, I. Taylor, G. Aspey (captain), and J. Hughes, three-quarter backs; J. Lalley and G. Kennedy, half backs; J. Leyshon, S. Aspey, H. Curtis, T. Lynch, M. McDonald, J. Power, W. Rigby, and M. Webster, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. McQutcheon (Oldham.) HULL v. KEIGHLEY.</p>
        <p>At Hull :—Hull two goals and five tries (19 points) Keighley, one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>LEEDS v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>These clubs met at Leeds, before 12,000 spectators: Once only was the bell got through successfully. Hewlett was the scorer but the point was not converted and so a capital game ended in the success of Leeds by three points to none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>"I WILL HAVE VI-COCOA."</head>
        <p>"I WILL HAVE VI-COCOA."</p>
        <p>Your grocer and every other grocer, too, can tell you of men and women who week after week regularly use Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and who, to use a common term, "swear by it" as a nourishing and stimulating Food Beverage.</p>
        <p>Are you a regular drinker of Vi-Cocoa? If not, why not?</p>
        <p>Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, by taking the place of stimu- lants of an ardent nature, has rpade happy many a home which otherwise to-clay would have been wretched, and the ultimate effects of which it is impossible to foresee. For breakfast, dinner, supper—indeed at any hour of the twenty-four—Dr. Tibbles Vi-Cocoa will be found light and refreshing; it leads the way among all beverages, and nothing can equal it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WIGAN v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>WIGAN v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>At Wigan. Halifax put on pressure, but, as was the case with the home team in the initial half, they could not cross the line. Brown endeavoured to bring relief but Little caught the ball and made his mark, from which he landed a splendid goal. Result :—Halifas, one goal (two points) ; Wigan nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>BRADFORD v. HUDDERSFIELD.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>At Bradford. before 2,000 spectators. Broadley started and the forwards kept play in the visitors' half a drop- kick by Gnnn missing. Bradford continued to press,and Gaunt followed up a long kick, Rees getting over between the posts, but Mosby failed in his kick at goal. Just before half-time Deere scoredas the result of clever passing, Muxworthy failingat goal. Sutcliffe restarted, and Thomas tried to save, but the ball hit a Bradford player and bounced back, Laidlow scoring. The visitors three-quarters passed well. Surnaan directly afterwards scored, Mosby converting, Result :—Bradford, one goal and three tries (11 points) ; Huddersfield, one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SALFORD v. SWL'ITON.</head>
        <p>SALFORD v. SWL'ITON.</p>
        <p>There was a very large crowd of spectators on the Salford ground to see the first match this season between these rival clubs. From start to finish a vigor- ous game was played, in which Salford showed much superior form during the second half, and eventually won by eight points. At half-time neither side had scored. The second half opened in rather a sensational manner. From the first scrummage Preston got the ball and passed it to Lomas, who dashed through the Swinton three-quarters and then gave Heath a fine opening to score a try. Lomas failed to kick a goal. After this success Salford kept up a vigorous attack, and eventu- ally Buckler crossed the Swinton line close to the posts, Lomas easily placing a goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BATLEY v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</head>
        <p>BATLEY v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Batley before a fair attend- ance of spectators. Both clubs were strongly repre- sented, and a well-contested game took place, which ended in a win for the Rangers by a margin of ten points. The Rangers made a determined attack, and W. James commenced a round of passing which ended in Hogg scoring a try. This was *quickly followed by another fine bout of passing, and Hogg scored again in a more favourable position. W. James placed a goal, and at half-time the Rangers led by eight points to two. The Rangers attache ii for the greater part of the game, and Poole dropped a goal. Final score :—Broughton Rangers, two goals and two tries (ten points); Batley, one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>TYE NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>TYE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Neetioi of the General Committee.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the General Committee was held en Tuesday night at the George Hotel, Hudders- field, Mr. R. Collinge,(Rochdale Homets,)presiding. The Wigan St. Thomas's Club being agreeable, Rad- cliffe hangers were allowed to withdraw from the qualifying round of the Cup-ties. In reply to a communication from the Askam P.C., it was decided to Anform them that the qualifying round of the Northern Union Cup-ties must be played 15 a-side.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Referees' Society called attention to the mode of putting the ball into the scrummage, and asked loran instruction thereon. The Committee deci- ded to instruct referees to see that theimlf-backs stand close to the scrummage, and put the ball in with a downward motion. In respect to a second question whether or not a referee had power to compel a half- back to carry out such instruction, the Committee decided that they had such power. The Wakefield and District Junior League and the Celts U.F.C. were elected to membership of the Union The matter of the Hull and District Rugby Unionand Goole Amateurs F.C. was again before the committee, and it was resolved to inform the first-named that the committee considered that they had acted in a proper manner towards the Goole A.F.C. in placing the names of the players of the club named in the defaulters' list. The Committee had under consideration the question of forwards packing four or five wide. It was decided that this is not in accordance with the spirit of the rules and it was unanimously resolved that all scrummages must be initially formed with three men in the front row; and, further, should the referee find that at the formation of a scrummage it is not formed in this manner he must penalise the offending side under Rule 11, clause 7. It having een reported that two players named Garreti (Dewsbury) and Green (Sharlston) had been convicted of a serious assault committed after the players had taken part in a football match, and sentenced to terms of imprisonment, the committee were unanim- ously of opinion that such players should not be per- mitted to take part in matches under the auspices of the Union, and it was resolved that the players named be suspended from taking any further part in matches accordingly. The York Club, for breach of Professional Law I,sub- section C, was fined ss. It was resolved that W. Norris, for breach of Professional Law 5, in having played for Somerville F.C. without the permission of the Pendle- buryClub, for whom he was registered as a professional, be suspended to the 17th October, he having already been suspended for a fortnight. The Somervffle Club was exonerated from blame. The Pendlebury F.C., for breach of Professional Law 3, in having no written agreement with their professional players, was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>" GUARDIAN " ATHLETIC V. SANKEY AMATEURS.</p>
        <p>Played ou the ground of the latter on Saturday. Result :—Sankey Amateurs, four goals; "Guardian" Athletic, nil.</p>
        <p>FROGHALL ALBIONS V. LOVELY-LANE ATHLETIC. On the tatter's ground on Saturday. Final :—Frog- hall Albions, six goals; Lovely-lane, three.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>GENERAL DEBILITY CURED BY DR. MORSE'S INDIAN ROOT PILLS.</head>
        <p>GENERAL DEBILITY CURED BY DR. MORSE'S INDIAN ROOT PILLS.</p>
        <p>Lost vitality and tone in the body, brought on by poor and weak blood, is what is known as General Debility. A tired, fagged, listless feeling creeps over the sufferer. Weary and worn, the victim soon drifts into worse dangers. It may be indigestion, headaches, or constipation. Often the whole system is completely shattered. With women it sometimes settles in that dread complaint, ANEMIA, which, as its name implies, means—want of blood. What is wanted is a medicine that will expel from the body the debilitating humours that have caused the trouble. Some natural agent that will cleanse and thoroughly tone up the blood; purify- ing and strengthening it, and so make it the body's most valuable asset. Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills will do this in a natural manner. Miss A. Brett, Lacey's-lane, Exning, Newmarket,had been reduced to a weak and low state. Her system had been debilitated and was open totals? disease. In fact, Atimmia had claimed her as its own. Shefound, however, a positive and safe cure in Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills. "At times," she says, "I felt most wretched and bad, for my illness had re duced me to a verypoor state. The doctor who attended me said that my complaint was Antemia, but he did little good towards improving my health. Had it not been for Dr. gorse's Indian Root. Pills, I should to this day have suffered hourlypain. The pills toned up,my blood, which rebuilt my system in such a marvellous manner that all traces of my troubles were removed, and I was made a perfectly healthy woman." Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills, a perfect blood purifier, cure liver and kidney complaints, biliousness, indigestion, constipation piles, dyspepsia, foul breath, sallow complexion, female ailments, eczema, pimples and blotches. Sold by lead- ing chemists and stores, price Is.l 1:141. per bottle. If unable to obtain where you reside, they may be had post free on receipt of price from The W. H. Comstock Co.. Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, g.c. FREE SAMPLE. Mention this paper and enclose id. stamp for postage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>GRAND LIBERAL BAZAAR.</head>
        <p>GRAND LIBERAL BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>Organising Meeting.</p>
        <p>The thousands of people who were interested in and helped forward to so grand a success, the Liberal Bazaar of eleven years ago, will doubtless be delighted to hear that a second big bazaar is to be held in October 1904, and with the object of making definite arrangements and organising committees, stall-holders, and the like, a meeting was held at the Liberal Club on Monday even- ing. There was an excellent attendance in response to the invitation of Mrs. Armitage and Mr. C.E.Parker,the two Bazaar Presidents, who were supported by Mrs' Parker,lVlr. ZibaArmita ge,Councillor and Mrs. Pierpoirft, Miss Crosfield.Cnpta, in G. R. Crosfield, and many other influential Liberals. Mr. PARKER said that as they all knew it had been decided that in connection with their Liberal Associa- tion they would have a bazaar to raise the necessary funds to help on the cause which, he took it for granted was dear to every one of them. Some people seemed to speak as if the Liberal pirty in Warrington was an extinct force. He felt sure they would agree with him when he saidithat so long as sectariamsra perilized their educational system ; so long as the religious difficulty was inherent with that system; so long as the privileges and rights which the people have had for I several hundred years of controlling the liquor Itraffic through the magistrates was called in question; so long as the landlords were like a great incubus on the prosperity and progress of the country; iso long as the great Free Trade policy of the country, !which, during the last 50 years, had done so much to TOO OLD AT FORTY-SIX. advance the prosperity of the people—(hear, hear)—so The Marine Engineers' Association of Glasgow in long as those great things were called in question and their monthly report, just issued, announce that an order such faults existed, the Liberal party of the country has been given by some of the largest engineering firms and in Warrington would have plenty of work, and on the Clydeside that in future no engineers over forty- work which was well worth all the enthusiasm force five years of age are to be employed. It is understood and power they could put into it. (Applause.) 'There that the order is an outcome of the Workmen's Com- , were some who seemed to think that the Liberal party pensation Act, which is telling heavily on employers, ought to hand on to some other organization the banner The Marine Engineers' Association declare that a man of progress, which they had upheld in this country at forty-five is worth two young men, and that the throughout the last century, but he felt sure they all masters will find this out should they insist upon their recognised that• there was a great work for the party order. still to do. He expressed regret that Mr. A. H. Cros- field was not present, but they had his younger brother with them and, better still, his sister. (Applause.) CONGREGATIONALISTS AND THE WAR OFFICE. Councillor PrEnporsT, in making a suggestion with The Congregational Union on Wednesday passed a reference to the bazaar, said he wanted to live long enough to see Warrington wen by a Liberal. He did resolution calling upon England to fulfil its obligations in regard to Macedonia, condemning the Education see it thus won once, and he as in hones of seeing the Acts G, and regarding with indignation the attempts of day dawn upon them again. (Applause). the overnment, and especially of the Prime Minister, Various suggestions were made, and it was decided to to limit magisterial authority in licensing matters. Dr. hold the bazaar about October of next year, and to have Horton, chairman of the Union, speaking on the war about twelve stalls. linquiry, said it revealed incapacity, carelessness, indol- Mr. OSBORNE, the secretary, said they would be glad ence, and almost criminal incapacity. Their fathers if something like £2,000 could be raised. Refreshments were afterwards partaken. of upstairs,and _brought Strafford, Laud, and the Royal Mead to the during the whole of 'the proceedings there was much block for offences which were not intrinsietilly worse than the offences committed by the Government, and enthusiasm displayed in regard to the Bazaar project. were they, the sons of those Ironsides, to be silent?</p>
        <p>FAILURES, BILLS Or SALE, AND DEBENTURES IN BRONCHITIS AND ASTHMA. ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to "Kemp's Aler- G. 11. SHARPE, F.C.S., Analyst, 11 &amp; 12, Great Tower cantile Gazette," the total number of failures re Street, London, says, in his Certificate of Analysis :—"I corded inEngland and Wales during the week endin am of opinion that VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE Friday, October 9th, was 153, viz.:—New bankruptc will prove a valuable remedy in the cure of coughs,colds, proceedings published in the "London Gazette," 79 asthma, and all bronchial complaints." deeds of arrangement registered, 74. The resp ectiv VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is the purest and numbers in the corresponding week of last year were most efficient remedy procurable for coughs, colds, bankruptcies, 74; deeds of arrangement, 78—total 152 b ronchitis,asthma, catarrh and children's coughs. Price being a net increase in bankruptcies of 6, and a net in-: 91d., is. 1.1., &amp; 2s. 9d. at chemiks and drug stores, crease in deeds of arrangement in 1903, to date, of 290 ,i • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>I</p>
