        <p>BUNS A</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>You can make 15 large, light, delicious Buns for a total cost of 30. from a id. packet of Eiffel Tower. BUN FLOUR</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Colborne-street.</p>
        <p>The Pastor took for his evening discourse on Sunday last the subject of "Faith." Restated that this was the keynote of all Christ's teaching and was the one thing which distinguished Christianity from other beliefs. Men at the present day were neglecting religion, yet found no solution to life's problems either in atheism or agnosticism. Man must be possessed by great over-ruling principle, and faith was the con- formity of the whole life to the spiritual ideal set forth and manifested in Jesus !Christ. It was not, however, necessary in order to commence the Christian life to believe all the doctrines of Christ. Belief in Christ himself and in His spiritual message was the one thing needed, and then the vision would grow larger," and though,' the preacher stated, "at first you only come to admire,by and by you will kneel down to adore."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Latchford Baptist Church.</head>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>The autumnal sermons were preached is the Baptist Church, Latchford, on Sunday, by the Rev. I. Nicholls of Nelson. There were large congregations the Church being crowded at the evening service. In the morning the preacher took for his text the passage "There is muchlfruit in the tillage of the poor and in the even- ing his sermon was based on the words "This one thing I do." His eloquent discourses were much appreciated In the afternoon a young people's service was held and Councillor W. Rrough delivered an interesting andhelp- ful address. Special hymns and anthems were rendered Mr. Peter Harrison presiding at the organ. The collec- tions, which were maid of the church funds, realized. between £ll and £l2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Legh Street Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>Legh Street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday the Christian Endeavour anniversary was celebrated, the Rev. W. L. Wardle, ALA.. 8.D., accupying the pulpit, morning and evening. The organ voluntaries contributed by the organist, Mr. Fred Podmore were :—" Andante Maestoro" (4th Organ Concerto, Handel)," Trumpet March " (Jude) ; evening, " Twilight Thoughts" (Montgomery, Prize Andante), "Intermezzo " t(Rheuiberger's Sonata in E minor). The choir ably contributed the anthem "Hark, hark my Soul" (Shelley),the blend of the voices being_excellent: The soloists were the Misses Alice Bridge, Eva Wood- land and Annie Bissell, each doing full justice to their respective parts. Mr. Albert Spruce conducted. Madame Christian, who was in excellent voice, rendered "Abide with me" with effect. On the following evening, a public meeting was held, the chair being taken by the Rev. J. G. Reed. The following addressed the gather- ing: Rev. W. L. Wardle, Pastor J. Grady (the newly- appointed missioner), and the Rev. J. G, Reed. Solos were given by Madame Christian and Mr. Barnes, Miss Eva Millet and Mr. Fred Podmore being the accom- panists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission Chapel, Bank Quay.</p>
        <p>At this mission on Sunday the harvest thanksgiving services were held. The preacher was Councillor James Evans. In the afternoon a service of song was given by the choir, the connective readings being taken by Councillor Tinnion. Councillor Evans preached in the evening from the words "Whatsoever a man soweth that shall he also reap." At the close of the servicethe preacher called the attention of the congregation to the atrocities in Macedonia, and a resolution was put before the meeting from the Free Church Councilandapproved of, calling on our Government to in some way intercede. The chapel had been very tastefully decorated, under the direction of-Miss Birch. On Monday evening a successful fruit banquet and social were held in the schoolroom, Thewlis-street. The collections and proceeds of the banquet were in aid of the building fund for the new chapel now in course of erection. The collections amounted to £6 12s. 6d.</p>
        <p>Daresbury. Thanksgiving servicesfor the in gathering of the harvest were held in the Parish Church on Friday and Sunday. As usual'a harvest tea was partaken of in large tents in a geld near the church on Friday afternoon, but owing to the inclemency of the weather the attendance was con- siderably reduced. During tea the Preston Brook Band played selections of music in one of the tents. Divine service was held in the church in the evening when the sermon was preached by the Rev. H. A. Lester, of Warrington Training College, the choir efficiently rendering the anthem "Thou crownest the year with Thy goodness" as well as special hymns and psalms suited to the occasion. The offertory was devoted to the Parish Nursing Fund and amounted to £3 is. 2d. The services were continued on Sunday. The Vicar preached in the morning and the Rev. G. W. Dickenson, of Warrington, in the evening. The collections for the day were devoted to the Warrington Infirmary, and realized the sum of £9 14s. The church, as in previous years, was !artistically decorated with corn, flowers, plants, fruit, Ste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Emmanuel Church, Bold-street.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Church, Bold-street.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Friday evening an ordination service was held when Mr. Joseph Callan was admitted into deacons' orders by the Right Rev. Philip X. Eldridge (Bishop of the Reformed Episcopal Church in England) assisted by the Rev. T. W. Bowman, M.A., and the Rev. J. Rowland Parker. Mr. Calhn was pre- sented by Dr. Bowman, and, after making the solemn required declarations, the ceremony of the laying on of hands took place. The Rev. J. R. Parker read the R3grtsheanwdorkr-ofire ni%nisptrreacy. ministry." tferrosmpeatlenwo words, duties and office of deacon in the church he referred to the special and unique circumstances of Mr. Callan's ordination, he having been brought up in the bosom of the church at Warrington and receiving instruction from his then pastor. He had prepared himself by an honourable and creditable college course at Cheshruit and was now taking a brief medical coarse at Living- stone College before going out as Reformed Church Missionary to the Punjab. The service was well attended.</p>
      </div>
