      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>A'ate hp 1 tat ton.</head>
        <p>A'ate hp 1 tat ton.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>OCTOBER 17th 1903.</p>
        <p>Perhaps Lord ROSE- , " HYPOTHESIS AND BERY was dreaming ASSERTION." of an unattainable ideal when he thought that the great fiscal issue should be treated from a non-political standpoint. It is difficult to imagine any co-:.ditions in which our party system would permit this desirable imparti- ality. For a short time Mr. GLADSTONE believed that the better government of Ireland could be made a non-party question ; so he proposed, in 1885, that Lord SALISBURY, then in office, should undertake this reform with the co-operation of the Opposition. That dream did not survive the first rough contact with realities. We cannot have a party system which can be put aside whenever the country is confronted by some proposal of funda- mental change. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S sudden abandonment of Free Trade was bound to stir the Liberal party to decisive 1 action in defence of a principle which is intimately associated with their strongest traditions. Moreover, the extraordinary situation, 1 in whicb a Ministry is avowedly inspired and directed by a statesman who has thrown off the , responsibilities of office, forces the oppositi@n to act more resolutely than ever on party lines. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN hopes to enjoy a free hand for agita- tion by a manoeuvre which is to keep the Unionist Free Traders tied in the bonds of allegiance to the Government. Nothing more dexterous has ever been seen in the history of party management. t would have thrown DISRAELI'S TAPER and TAD</p>
        <p>POLE in ecstasies of admiration, but it makes any- thing like a purely philosophical examination of the fiscal issue quite impossible. Liberals cannot be expected to approach this issue in serene obliv- ion of Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S strategy. They see it in the filial piety of the Chancellor of the Ex- chequer,and in the election address of the Colonial Secretary. "Preferential tariffs to secure closer commercial ties with the Colonies," says Mr. LYTTELTON, "involve a readjustment of taxes on food peculiarly affecting the poorer classes, who should have full opportunity minutely and deliber- ately to consider the matter. It !I:Lust, therefore be distinctly understood that there is no proposal to impose preferential tariffs for the Colonies with- out further reference to the people." This means that the sympathetic Mr. LYTTELTON Will SiG tight at the Colonial Office while Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is educating the people for a G 3aeral Election Neither the Colonial Secretary nor any other Minister will make any proposals whatever; but the business of the country will go on mechanically while the real initiative is left to the "missionary." The eminent cricketer who is wearing Mr. CHAM- BERLAIN'S official gloves and pads will not attempt to score a run. He will simply keep his bat down, II and not budge. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S supporters in the Press are not very happy in their comments on Lord ROSE- -1 BERY'S speech. He demanded proof that Free Trade is failing to ensure the prosperity of the country. The " Times " answers this by asserting that "the existing system of free imports is ceasing, owing to the great changes of conditions, either to maintain our prosperity or to give any prospect of arresting its decline." Here the decline is taken for granted,although a broad survey of the volume of our trade shows no real symptoms of it Unfavourable fluctuations there may have been; but, as it happens, the fluctuations are now dis- tinctly favourable, as anyone may see who examines the Board of Trade returns for the pre- sent year. The great changes of conditions are obvious. Our foreign competitors, largely by the applicatiou of scientific methods in which we are deficient, are able to chal- lenge the manufacturing supremacy we enjoyed for the greater part of a century. Does this prove that we should regain unquestioned supremacy by abandoning Free Trade, or that we should have maintained the supremacy if we had never given up Protection? Mr. LYTTELTON and the "Times" assume that the cheapness of food is irrelevant to the issue, because the working man is being.da- prived by foreign competition of the means to buy food at all. This is a pretty good illustration of the "hypothesis and assertion" on which Mr. CHAMBERLAIN relies. It is refuted by the general condition of our industries. We may be told that the conditions of a particular trade are not satis- factory; but is the German working man in every trade satisfied with the operation of his com- mercial system ? Does the Western farmer in America never complain of the price he has to pay the Eastern manufacturer of agricultural im- plements? We have to consider whether Free Trade is, on the whole, more suitable to our con- ditions than any manipulation of import duties. To that question Mr. CHAMBERLAIN has already had his answer.</p>
        <p>Alderman Monks has, we understand, written a letter to the Mayor, tendering his resignation of his position as an Alderman of the Borough and a member of the Education Committee. Such a step on Mr. Monks' part has been freely rumoured for some time, but, now that it has taken place we are sure there will DO a widespread feeling of regret in the town at the severance from public service of so able, hardworking and useful a man. Until Alderman Monks' reasons are made public we do not consider ourselves in a position to say more on the subject. The event is, however, one we personally deplore. Councillor John Evans, too, has intimated his decision not to seek re-election on November Ist, and he also will be much missed in many departments of municipal work. The Sanitary Works Committee is an especial sufferer, both gentlemen having very capably filled the position of chairman of that Committee.</p>
        <p>The Non-Traders held their fourteenth great meeting on Thursday evening when the Parr Hall was filled by enthusiastic backers of the Associa- tion.An interesting resume of"what we have done" was given by Councillor Dodds,and amplified by Mr Norbury Williams, the latter of whom gave a full explanation of the reasons actuating his confreres in regard to the inquiry. Mr. Williams' words on this question carry weight, and in his view he was supported by an audience which may be termed representative of the burgesses of the town. We presume that, after November 2nd the Council will be in the humour to order a resumption of the inquiry on Non-Trading lines eitlier by the present Commissioners or others. After all there is nothing very much to be at daggers drawn abolt. One can hardly imagine that there is any- thing in the course of the inquiry the Non-Traders wish to keep dark,—they have fought long enough for a complete investigation, and if they think a reasonable economy can be effected in the way they suggest they have just as much right to insist upon it as they had to insist upon the inquiry itself. It must not be forgotten that they nave the town behind them. The Corporation Com- missioners should bear this in mind when demand- ing an observance of the strict letter of the rule regarding a report of "the proceedings."</p>
        <p>There were two sentences uttered by Mr. Williams at the close of his speech which we desire to refer to. His remarks were : "We want the ratepayers to strengthen our hand to such an extent that we shall be able to practically dictate the policy of the Town Council. There is to be no more domination of political parties in municipal affairs." Omit the word "political," and the two sentences absolutely contradict, each other, and at the same time give point to a lesson which should be wholesome even to the Non- Traders. We do not deem it desirable—and we hope Councillor Dodds and Mr. Norbury Williams will agree with us—that any party, political or otherwise, should "dictate the policy of the Town Council "or "dominate municipal affairs." Such a thing cannot be in the best I interests of good government /land we trust the Non-Traders will enter upon a little self-examina- tion in this light. We are hoping to see the time, very soon, when there will be no Non-Trading Association or agitation. Its object was to stop all trading by members of the Council with the Corporation,—an object which we believe to be already achieved. There is the completion of the inquiry, as a desideratum, still to be worked for. This ought in our opinion, to be the last line in an unpleasant. though salutary and beneficial, chapter of local muhicipal history. The Town Council, and the town, might then be safely left to work out their own salvation, undis- turhed by the pinpricks of an agitation the pin- mance of which will only berve to lessen the usefulness of our public men by setting one against another. We make this suggestion in all serious- ness, without any feeling of antagonism to the Non-Traders, but with the one view of promoting the best interests of the town, and we hope it will be given full consideration by those to whom it is addressed.</p>
        <p>The most important subject to be submitted at the meeting of the Education Committee on Mon- day evening is that referring to school accommoda- tion. It can hardly come as a surprise to the rate- payers to learn that some new schools will have to be built in the near future. The Education</p>
        <p>Depapment ibade known their requirements in Warrington before the passing of the Education Act, and it was at the request of the Town Colncil that the matter was deferred until the new Act came into force. In the meantime the need for additional schools has become more urgent, and on Monday evening the Education Committee is to be asked to sanction the erection of a new school at L itchfor I, and another in the neighbour- hood of Brick-street. The ratepayers, of course will be called upon to provide the money, and as this is only the first instalment of building opera- tions, the prospect of an immediate reduction of the education rate is extremely remote. The new building programme is part of the price we have to pay for our laxity in regard to elementary education in the past and we must now prepare to face it.</p>
        <p>Captain G. R. Crosfield returns to the attack, in another column, on behalf of the National Ser vice League. We must admit that he states hi case very ably. So does Mr. Chamberlain. Ths comparison might be taken further,for both gentle° men base their arguments on assumptions which are not:borne out by a close inquiry into the facts. Captain Crosfield is possessed with the idea that this island oflours is in a most parlous andiperilous plight. Being" between the devil and the deep sea" is as nothing compared to the terrible situa- tion of the British Isles. In our correspondents' imagination every nation in Europe is seeking an opportunity to invade us and put our unpatriotic persons to the sword. Therefore we must all become soldiers and brandish a sword of our own. This is,? we think, a sad state of mind to be in. There is no evidence, that we are cognisant of, to show the anxiety of our European neighbours to annihilate us, and still less reason to believe that, in such case, we should be able to strike only the very feeblest and most ineffective of blows in our own defence. Captain Crosfield will insist on our helplessness. We are surely no more helpless than at any time during the last thousand years, in the course of which period there has been more than 1 one ineffectual attempt to invade out shores. We refuse to be frightened, and we must confess to 1 being incurably optimistic. DJ not, please, retort that we are blind or foolhardy. The spirit of militarism has not affected us to any great extent, beyond creating a desire to deprecate it. And we I "hare made a God of Freedom !" What Britisher has not ?</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield's arguments would, be most con- vincing were this country situated like France or Germany. But it is not, and the vital difference appears to be over-looked by our friends of the National Service League. We are not a military nation, we have no desire to be, and there is no more peed for us to become one than ever there was. It is on this foundation of belief that Cap- tain Crosfield and ourselves must and do plainly part company. Even supposing, for the sake of argument, that out and out. conscription was in force here we should not have an army at all Capable of maintaining for any length of time a conflict with the forces of a great European power or coalition of powers. Armihiliation would be ours, just as surply in the long run as it would be very shortly under present conditions were we once effectually invaded. Captain Crosfield in— stances Switzerland as a nation employing the defensive methods he advocates. Well, of what use would "universal training "be to the Swiss did one of the great military nations desire her conquest. Her army is too small numerically to give any serious resistance, and she would have to rely, as heretofore, on her mountain barriers. We rely, as heretofore,on our" wooden walls." It is the invasion we must prevent, and-it is the Navy which must prevent it. That has been our recog- nised defensive policy for hundreds of years, and it will have to remain so until the great disarma- ment takes place or the milleiaium arrives.</p>
        <p>There was a comical incident at a Primrose League meeting at Stretton on Monday evening, when the Alan de Tatton Eorton, M.P. delivered an address in the course of which he de- clared his intention of supporting Mr. Chamberlain in the" patriotic step "he had taken. Either the' declaration, or the speech, or the fiscal pro- posals did not go down very well with the Stretton villagers,and there was an ominous apathY displayed during the hon. gentleman's remarks. Such a flat reception was most distressing, so a gentleman on the platform wormed his way unob- trusively into the centre of the audience and implored those round about him to "cheer, boys cheer." At every available opportunity he clapped most vigorously, and the villagers were accordingly stimulated into something like applause. Per- haps, after all, the member for Kuutsford went away pleased with the good impression he appeared to have created. Whether or no, he owes a boic of cigars to the resourceful individual who "led the gallery."</p>
        <p>The Trades and Labour Council is an active body.and in Mr. Fred Stott it possesses a particu- larly active secretary. His annual report presented on Tuesday evening was brimful of interesting information and formed an admirable r6siorte, of the subjects which have engaged the attention of the Council during the pmt twelve months. Ths state of trade locally, it would appear, is not very bright. Depression, says Mr. Stott, seems to be very general at present, and Board of Trade ret.irns indicate that the coming winter is likely to be one of great distress owing to lack of employment. Of late, the industrial classes of Warrington have had reason to feel thankful that they have not had to suffer to anything like the same extent as the less fortunate workers in other Lancashire towns. This is doubtless due to the variety of industries, and we trust that the gloomy foreboding in regard to the winter will not be realized. Mr., J. PodmorFi is to be heartily congratulated upon his election to the presidency of the Trades Council. His zeal in the labour cause is known to all who have the interests of the movement at heart, and no one is more deserving of the honour than he.</p>
        <p>It has been so customary with us t) look upon the dense volumes of black smoke which are daily emitted from the chimneys of maufactories as an inevitable cincornAant of industrialism that we have resigned ourselves to it, with little demur- Measures have certainly been passed by the Legis- lature with the view of penalising those who are guilty of the offence, but local authorities are so chary in taking action against large ratepayers, that the Act is rarely put in force. This is the experience in Warrington at any rate, and although Alderman Bennett has raised his solemn protests, they have usually fallen upon deaf oars. At the meeting of the Literary and Philosophical Society, Mr. F. G. Ruddock, F.1.C., the new president, read a timely paper on "Fuel and its economic application.' He had little difficulty in showing that within recent years applied science has Ircought forth some remarkable developments in fuel economy. The mere fact that in the large majority of works only three per cent of the power the fuel contains is being utilized is sufficient evidence of waste to direct the attention of manufacturers to this im portant subject. . Already several local firms are conducting experiments with the view of obtain- ing the most economical methods of generating power and of heating furnaces. We believe that science has many secrets to reveal even on Buchan everyday subject as coal consumption, but it 15 only by encouraging scientific research that WO can hope for success, and s •)me manufacturers do not give that encouragement to chemists which they deserve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. ri.Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. ri.Society.</p>
        <p>There was a well attended meeting of this Societ9 on Sunday. Three new members were enrolled and the membership now stands at 529. The chair was occupied by Mr. Thomas Wallington who based an excellent address on the Words of St. Paul "As we have oppor- tunity let us do good unto all men." The world srag starving, he said, for men of St. Paul's stamp. Via needed leaders animated by his spirit and who put t.._he good of mankind before all other consideratiors. Du' Rimmer, of the City Mission, Liverpool, delivered an address under the title "Wanted a man." He pointed out the great need of men who would give their servic_V voluntarily in Christian work of various kinds. Mr. Ws H. Payton, Mns Bac., very efficiently contributed 512 organ solo of his own composition. Mr. Farrar and- Mr. W. Beswick jun., also took part in the service.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. At Golborne-street Baptist Church on Thursday the Rev. Arthur Mursell, of London will deliver his famous lecture on "Cobden and Bright and Free Trade." The visit of this veteran lecturer. is being looked forward to with much interest. Mr. Mursell has been . one of Mr. Chamberlain's most devoted advisers and will therefore be able to give an unbiassed opinion Councillor W. .Pierpoint, will . preside. A grand "Sunny South" Bazaar will be held in the Workingmen's Mission School. Thewl . street, on November sth, • 6th and 7th in aid of the new chapel building fund. A very attractive programme of competitions &amp;c., is being arranged. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS NTINERAL WATERS.—Advt. • Mrs. Porter, of Manchester, occupied the platform at the Spiritualist Hall, Bewsey-street, • on Sunday, Speaking in the evening ,very effectively on r" Truth," 'followed with a little clairvoyance. There was a large after-circle. On Monday evening there whs a good audience, when Mrs. 'Porter gave a number of clairvoyant and psychometric delineations. BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Warrington P.S.A. Society. The quarterly prize distribution took place on Sunday and was largely attended. Mr. W. Clegg occupied the chair. Miss L. Evans sang " Daddy ' and The last Milestone," for which she was heartily applanded, and Mr. A. Scriven gave an address on The Mystery of the Resurrection." A number of new members were enrolled at the close of the 3neeting. BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CLOCK.—Advt. Primrose League Meeting at Stretton. On Monday evening a meeting of the Appleton Habitation of the Primrose League was held in 'Stretton schools under the presidency of Mr. T. H. Lyon. An address was delivered by the Hon. Alan de Tatton Egerton,- M.P.' for,the Krnitsford Division, • who dealt at length with Mr. Chamberlain's tariff pro- posals. He declared his intention of supporting Mr. • Chamberlain in the" patriotic step" he had taken. LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRID GE-STREET.—Athq- 'The Latchford Baptist Literary and Debating • Society. At a meeting of this Society, Mr. T. H. Ford, of. Knutsford-road read a paper upon "Richard Cobden; • The man and his mission." He showed that the Pro- tection duty on corn raised the price of bread during the ten years from 10.14. to is. 6d. per 41b. loaf. The -following members took part in the debate :—Rev. J 'F. Matthews, and Messrs. Samuel .Albinson, William- Bamber' W. W. Drewett, Thomas Albin.son, and Walter Murby.—A. vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Ford for his able paper. You CANNOT DO BETTER! Buy your boots and shoes at ALT. TAYLOR'S. Cheapest and best town. it. John's Presbyterian Church. St John's Literary and Social Society held the open- 'ma social meeting of the present session in the Church Schoolroom, Winwickrstreet, on the evening of Tuesday the 13th inst. The Rev. J. L. Milne, M.A., 8.D., occupied the chair, and there was an attendance of 2nembers and their friends to the number of about sixty. The programme was opened with Musical -selections by the Misses Chesworth. Songs were rendered bv Miss Sumner and Messrs. Ewa u • and Hunter. Tea was provided during the evening, and fruit was served. Various social games were intro (bleed, all present thoroughly enjoying themselves. .An excellent syllabus has been arranged, and the members of the Society look forward to flying a most • successful session. GIVE THEM A TRIAL.—ALF. TAYLOR'S wonder- ful boots for everybody. .Accident in Church-street. A singular accident occurred, in Church-street on b Alondaymorning- elonolng At about ten o'clock a vehicle to -the American Oil Company, drawn by t NVO liOrses, was turning out of Fennel-street into Church-street, when one of the animals slipped-on the footpath and fell, the other horse dragging it along the roadway and twisting one of its legs so severely. 'that it was broken off. The animal, which was worth -about £4O or £5O, had to be slaughtered on the spot. - The driver had .a narrow escape. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC IS the KING OF . TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. 'Child's Sad Death. _ Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted an in- quest at the Police Station on Tuesday afternoon Concerning the death of Ann Caldwell, the eight-year- - 'old daughter of William Caldwell, general labourer, Hamilton-street.--The evidence showed that .-deceased was in the house with her sister on Friday. . During the morning the sister went into another room 'leaving deceased sitting on a chair in front of the , kitchen fire. Hearing screams she rushed into the kitchen and found deceased's clothing was on fire. With assistance the flames were extinguished and . -deceased was taken to the Infirmary where she died. The child told her father that she had got off the chair and was in the act of turning round when the back of her skirt became ignited.—A verdict of" Accidental death" was returned. , Humorous Competition in the Parr Hall. The Parr Hall resounded with laughter on. Monday evening when a large audience assembled to witness the seventhgannual humorous competition promoted by AressrsKilduff and Hardiman. For the time being 'hilarity held unquestioned sway and the most staid individual would have found it difficult to restrain mirth when between twenty • and thirty humorists 'were, in turn, doing their best to secure the prizes offered. The judge—Mr. Charles Darrell, of ltanchester—had_to :admit that his task was a difficult one but he ventured the state- ment that it Was quite evident that the people of Warrington' knew when good talent was placed before them. Their applause, he said, had helped him a great deal in judging the merits of the '°ompetitors. The prizes were awarded as follows :- Ist (14), Mr. Frank Lauri, Manchester ; 2nd (£3), Mr. Arthur Bearle, Manchester ; 3rd (0), Mr. Fred Hors- -fall, Leeds; 4th (£1), Mr. Jack Gordon, St. Armes- °n-Sea; sth (10s.), Mr.. All. B. Millard, Manchester. During the evening Mr. Matt Kilduff contributed a lanmorous'song in creditable style. Mr. William Ku- duff recited, recited, and Mr. .Fred Carlton skilfully played 'several banjo solos. Mr. Albert Holt, of Manchester, Was an efficient accompanist. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGII-CLISS MINERA. WATERS.—Advt. wi Warrington in the Days of Cromwell." This was the subject of a paper read by Mr. Joseph hawthorn before the members of the Wycliffe Young Ileople's Society on Monday evening. 'There was an .encouraging attendance, and the ensuing 'session Promises to be as successful as any of its predeces- The Rev. John YOnge presided. The ,essayist -Faye an outline of the distinguishing features of the lowa during the Cromwellian period. Reference was • .Inade to its streets, its market place, its houses, its , Principal residents and its local government. In deal- ing with the town as the scene of civil strife, the 'essayist -pointed out that Warrington's geographi- eal position and its possession of a., bridge ;cross the •Mersey made it the • key •of the orth country. The stirring scenes, in which t•rt,.l Earl of Derby held the town for the .vv'lng for two years against the Parliamentarians, re recounted, and the essayist proceeded to describe subsequent defeat and surrender of the Warrington arrison by Colonel Norris, after a memorable siege. nissing on to the entry of Cromwell- into the town .T...lter defeating the Scots army under the Duke of •-;leatinilton Winwick, the essayist read two historical ii.ters written by Cromwell at Warrington,and closed I°.ls paper with an appreciation of Cromwell :ussa_great Englishman and a great patriot. —ln a dis- wasii9n which followed, taken part in by Mr. George I.la, Mr. J. Gadd, Mr. C. Eccles, Mr. James r, g, and the Chairman, reference was made to the historical association of Warrington and a hope -b„u"-2„,tu ,expressed that at least a few of the historical teings in the town might be preserved for pos- till2tY.—A vote of thanks to Mr. Hawthorn concluded " Proceedings. t FIRF. GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR- nkr,NP, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, iuge -street, Warr ingi on.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. A. H. Crosfiehl was present at a dinner and semi-public meeting held at the Hotel Cecil, London, on Friday evening. The 3rd King's (Militia) whose headquarters are at Warrington have lost their chief, Colonel J. M. Batten, C. 8., who would have completed nine years' service as Lieutenant-Colonel to-day (Aaturday). He was given the Order of the Bath for the work in the late Boer war. _</p>
        <p>Canon Willink, vicar of St. Helens, has been offered the living of Great Yarmouth by the Dean and Chapter of Norwich. The parish has a population of 40,000, with a beautiful church, which has seating accommo- dation for 3,500 people. Attached to the parish church are six other churches and a large staff of clergy. Canon Willink is considering the matter. The Rev. W. Bracecamp, vicar of St. Paul's, War- rington, was the preacher at St. Barnabas' Church, Smithdown-road, Liverpool, on Sunday morning on the occasion of the harvest festival.</p>
        <p>Master Tom Barton, of Sunnymead, Lovely-lane, obtained the third prize in the violin solo competition at the Blackpool Musical Festival,being only:five marks behind the winner of the first prize. The adjudicator's (Dr- McNaughton, F.R.A.M.) remarks on the perform- ance were—" A fluent and scholarly rendering of the composition, but marred by playing one wrong note." UNDER the CLOCK Or Goon BOOTS.—Advt. Proposed New Jetty. The Board of Trade have issued an advertisement, which appears in another column, giving particulars of a jetty proposed to be constructed by Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., at Bank Quay. The firm intend to contruct a wooden jetty on the opposite side of the river to the existing wharf belonging to the company, and to connect the two by a wire cable or telpher line at a height of 64 feet above low water level.</p>
        <p>The Ship Ganal Company's River Proposal. At a meeting of the Rivers' Committee held yester- day afternoon, the Town Clerk submitted the report of Sir John Wolfe Barry on the proposed diversion of the river by the Manchester Ship Canal Company. It WftS decided that a joint conference be held on Tues- day next of the traders and members of the Rivers Preservation Society, the Chamber of Commerce and the members of the Rivers Committee. The report will then be thoroughly considered and some decision arrived at. _ _</p>
        <p>Liberalism in the Knutsford Division. Under the auspices of the Knutsford Division Liberal Association a public meeting will be held in the large room, Grappenhall on Wednesday evening under the presidency of Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. The speakers will include Mr. A. H. Crosfield, and Mr. A. J. King, President of the Association and prospective Liberal candidate. Lantern views of famous cartoons by Mr. F. C. Gould will be exhibited during the evening. Sale of Property. Mr. Thomas Tunstall offered for sale by auction at the Blue Bell Inn, on Thursday, several lots of pro- perty in the town. The only lot which found a pur- chaser was the one comprising the dwelling-house, 43, Church-street, with valuable land attached thereto. This lot was bought for £B5O by Mr. Thomas Pem- berton on behalf of Messrs. Thomas Locker and Company Limited. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BEST BOOTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Basket-makers' Dispute. At the meeting of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening, a deputation consisting of Messrs. Blease, Ackerley and Fletcher, attended to explain the details of a dispute between members of the local branch of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Skip and Basket-makers and Mr. D. John- son, basket and hamper manufacturer, Latchford. It was stated that the matter in dispute was the payment for certain extra work and. 24 men had refused to work on the terms offered by Mr. Johnson.—After hearing the statements of the deputation, the Qhair- man extended to the basket-makers the sympathy of the Council in their struggle. Should assistance be required he felt confident that the Council would be prepared to help them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>H. M. AsirroN's LIFE TONIC is the KING OF</head>
        <p>H. M. AsirroN's LIFE TONIC is the KING OF</p>
        <p>SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. A GOOD RESOLUTION.—Wear ALF TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.--Advt. Warrington Caledonian Association. - We would again draw our readers' attention to the Caledonian Association's notice in our advertising columns intimating a grand concert by the well-known Glasgow Select Choir, in Parr Hall, Warrington, on *Tuesday, December Ist. It will be recollected that the concert given by the choir in Warrington last year was a most successful one, all who attended being loud in their praise of the singing. This year there is to be a miscellaneous programme cf English, Scotch; Irish and Welsh part songs, glees and solos, and, as the choir stands unrivalled at part singing, those who attend may rely on hearing some of our grandest awl most stirring national songs sung in a manner that will not fail to delight them. As the entire proceeds of the concert, after, paying of expenses, are again to be devoted to the Warrington Infirmary and the Cale- donian Association's Benevolent Fund those who pur- chase tickets, while they will receive good value for their money in the entertainment provided, will have the satisfaction of knowing that they are supporting most deserving charities.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work at Wycliffe_ Memorial _Buildings.</p>
        <p>_ With the main object of reducing the debt on The Wycliffe Memorial Building, a sale of work was held on Thursday afternoon and evening. There was no formal opening, but amongst those present were The Rev. Johß Yonge, Mr. F. Monks, J.P., the Rev. W. Bracecamp (vicar of St. Paul's) and Mrs. Brace- camp. There was one large stall containing a variety of useful and fancy articles, in charge of Mrs. Ziba Armitage Mrs. Congreve (Moore), Mrs. Yonge, Mrs. Garland, Mr, E. S. Garland, Mrs. J. Walker, Miss C. Walker, Mrs. W. Wallington, Mrs. Corney, Mrs. Porritt, Mrs. Hawthorn. Mrs. Edelsten, Miss Ellison, Mrs. Bateman, Miss Pickavance, • Miss Knowles and Miss Harrisen, The refreshments were provided by the members of the choir under the superintend- ence .of Mrs. R. H. Pearson and Miss Perritt. A large number ,visited the sale, both afternoon and evening, and the proceeds amounted to about £65. At intervals, vocal and instrumental music was given. Miss Longdin sang several songs and Miss Bertha Bouchier played violin solos in her usually clever manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Loyal Order of Anclent_Shepherds.—Warrlng-</head>
        <p>Loyal Order of Anclent_Shepherds.—Warrlng-</p>
        <p>ton District.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the above district was held on Saturday at the. late Spring Rose Lodge, Barley Mow Inn, under the presidency of Bro: Josh Adams. He was supported by Bro. Joseph Ashton, Bro. James Pair, district treasurer ; Bro. P. Edgar, district secretary ; Bros. Eckersley and Hall,trustees, and delegates from Lodge. Bro John Haley was called upon to present Bro.. Joseph Williams with a hand- some silver lever watch in recognition of his services as P.O. Shepherd of the district for the year 1902-3. In making the presentation Bro. Haley remarked that Bro. Williams exemplified the precept " whatsoever thy hand findeth to do do it with all thy might." This he had found charact3riSed every act of his official duties, which combined with a courteous, kindly and obligi▪ ng nature made him welcome wherever he went. lie was brother care - . ful of the minor, details of hi▪ s ,office ready to spend and be spent in the service of his fellows during a long connection with shepherdry both in Lodge and District. During his term of office he had made many land lasting friends and no enemies, which was in it- self evidence of his worth, and wide esteem. He wished him many years of health and strength to look back upon his honourable term of service.— Bros. Parr, Weir, Adams; Miller, and manv other brethren spoke also in high praise. . Bro. Williams who was warmly received thanked one and all for their kind words and good wishes. He would look upon the' watch with pride, and wished that he should be spared to work amongst them for many years to come.</p>
        <p>, "B R A X " (Regd.)</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>Brax Biscuits are made from Brax Flour, and contain the most nutritious and most easily digested constituents of both Wheat and 7Alalt. The Prsprieters of C 'MD'S BISCUIT u, especially recommend them as a palatable and wholesome food.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>xaminer, Saturday October 17, 1903.</head>
        <p>The xaminer, Saturday October 17, 1903.</p>
        <p>have a consumption of 5,500 tons of raw material, the result of which we buy and pay for by our manufactured goods, which probably employ more labour and do not use up so much national capital in the form of coal and ore.</p>
        <p>It should be certainly one of our objects to safeguard as far as wise and practicable, the mineral wealth of our island, and anyone like Mr. Woods who buys iron upon which other countries have lavished their national re- sources, is a benefactor to the country, and does his countrymen no injustice. May he and his company prosper ! _ .</p>
        <p>- CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, October 14th, 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>TARIFF PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE TARIFF PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain's Illness.</p>
        <p>In course of a letter to Mr. H. P. Hughes, of Stock- ton Heath—fauther of "one-sided Free-Trade v. Pro- tection]—Mr. Chamberlain, under date 15th instant, states that he is improving; and hopes to be able to keep all his engagements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MORE CRITICISMS.</head>
        <p>MORE CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>Sir H. Campbell Bannerman M.P.</p>
        <p>In his speech at Bolton on Thursday night Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman condemned both Protection and Retaliation, and inaddition outlined a programme for the Liberal party when returned to power. With regard to the condition of the Liberal party, he said they would in a short time, if not immediately, see the party so united and so strong that the present dis- jointed and discredited Government would very soon be ejected from office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Mr. " Tommy " Bowles, M.P.</head>
        <p>Mr. " Tommy " Bowles, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Gibson Bowles, M.P., (Conservative) speaking in Glasgow, said, the ideal position for the British Isles was to be a free port to all merchandise. When they were asked to return to Protection they were asked to leave civilisation in order to return to primitive barbarism. ' Taking the battle of David and Goliath as a metaphor, Mr. Bowles looked for sling and stone to prevail against the brass of Birmingham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Mr. Winston Churchill, M.P.</head>
        <p>Mr. Winston Churchill, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Winston Churchill, M.P., (Conservative) in sup- porting the formation of an anti-food tax league, warns the working-classes against Mr. Chamberlain's "glitter- ing schemes." He acknowledges that the author is a tremendous power even by himself, and adds: "There is only too much reason to fear that Mr. Balfour is secretly aiding and abetting him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>" A Daniel Come to Judgment, Yea, a</head>
        <p>" A Daniel Come to Judgment, Yea, a</p>
        <p>Daniel ! "</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your readers would be puzzledjto know how to take Mr. E.Woods' letter in last Saturday's" Examiner,' whether it was meant seriously or as a joke. In any case it was highly amusing. I prefer to accept it in the spirit of seriousness, as I think Mr. Woods incapable of a joke. He begins by saying he has been considering the question of Protection and Free Trade and has arrived at a conclusion. What a pity he did not do this sooner and offer his services to both Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamberlain. He might by so doing have helped the one to make up his mind and have saved the other a world of trouble from making such clumsy and palpable blunders in the simplest arithmetical problems. Mr. Woods says "If he buys 1000 tons of German wire rods.'—it might have been more correct to say When he buys 1000 tons of German or American rods; it does not much matter which so longas they are cheap and foreign—" he robs the English workmen of the privilege of mining and manufacturing about 6500 tons of material in the shape of iron ore, coke, coal,billets androds." This would be accepted as faithfully repre- senting the total of both the nature and quantities of material used in the manufacture of 1000 tons of wire , rods and that English rods made wholly from English materials would be preferthle if these could be ob- tained sufficiently cheap. I was under the impression that other materials than these were necessary in large quantities for the manufacture of iron, and also that in the mixing of many quantities of iron, foreign ore has to be mixed with English,and therefore the purchase of the foreign material is absolutely necessary in some form or other, but this may be due to my ignorance and Mr. Woods will no doubt put me right I must be one o "Those wondering rustics round, who gazed, And still they gazed and still the wonder grew, "How on small head, could carry all he knew." At all events this statement of Mr. Woods' would never be questioned, because Mr. Woods has said it,and what he has said, he has said. But one or two doubts have enteredlmy mind which maybe due to crass' ignor- ance, though here again Mr. Woods will no doubt be both able and willing to set my doubts at rest. What I wish to know is, are the Germans or other foreign manufacturers so generous as to make Mr. Woods a free gift of these 1000 tons of wire rods, and if not, howare they paid for.. .</p>
        <p>ICfr.. Woods seems to be troubled with a too tender conscience. He holds it to be a grievious sin to buy foreign rods and his trouble is that he is the chief of sinners. I have no doubt his conscience is also pricked by having purchased an education,for his son "Made in Germany.',</p>
        <p>Well ! Tam sure he may count on the sympathy of all his readers, but he will pardon me if sf, as one of these, do not see exactly eye to eye with him on this matter. It seems to me, though this may be ignorance again, that it counts to his advantage and it would be a great pity to rob him of this privilege. He is a shrewd business man, hat he:would not allow any self interest to interfere with tbe operation of his sentimentality. At all events it requires a few subtle minds like his to be focussed on tlys subject to clear it of the mist in which it is enveloped to put the issues plainly before such poor ignorant mortals as yours etc., B.ASSANTO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>IMPORT OF FOREIGN STEEL RODS.</head>
        <p>THE IMPORT OF FOREIGN STEEL RODS.</p>
        <p>Replies to Mr. Edwin Woods.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am much inberested in the confessions of Mr. Edwin Woods of the Longford Wire Co. in your last Saturday's issue. _ _</p>
        <p>He has tevvakened to the fact, that by buying cheap German steel rods he is doing a great injustice to his country, and he confesses, that he ls, in this respect,the "greatest sinner in Warrington." As a tanner, I feel I ought to bow at the same penitent form because T also import a great deal of raw product which could be grown although at greater cost, in England. • While stand at the same bench as Mr. Woods I can- not feel as he does the same sense of sin. Indeed from my point of view, it seems that as buyers of anything cheap that the foreigner can offer, we tienefit the industry with which we are associated and add to the prosperity of the country. The difference in point of view of Mr Woods and myself is in the conception of the'principles whichrule the exchange of commodities, which we call trade.</p>
        <p>Are these rods which are made in Germany given to us, or are they exchanged for something that we make? May I persume Mr. Woods will agree, that if Germany =dens a present of these things,welshould be foolish in declining to accept them? I am not at all sure of Mr. Woods' attitude of mind in regard to this aspect of the question, for if he feels himself to be asinnerin buying the rods at say five shillings under cost, what an arch villainhe would think himself to be if he accepted the goods for nothing! The injustice which Mr. Woods thinks he is doing to his fellow countrymen in buying cheap iron, appears to arise from the idea, that creating work for his fellows is a desirable end in itself apart from its relation to the product of that work. If it were an end in itself the man who paid his fellows to dig holes in the ground and fill them up again, would be a benefactor to his country, the man who set fire to his houses withthe object of giving work to artizans in rebuilding would also merit his country's thanks, while the man who in- vented a machine to save labour would be his country's enemy. It is strange that after producing such writers as Adam Smith, Professor Fawcett, and John Stuart Mill, we shonld see in this country writers, who by in- ference at least, would revive the economic heresies which such men so clearly exposed. However the Ger- mans do not ,it propose to give us the rods, although they have gone so far in that direction as to sell under cost. It. remains for us to consider whether, as a country, we are gainers or losers by the purchase. Now, may I take it that we are agreed that exports whether in service or goods, pay for imports? It is difficult nowadays to know what is accepted as the teaching of economic Science; and while we do not want to waste space in establishing truths we do not want to take for granted that . which Protectionists may dispute. However let it suffice for the present to refer Mr. Woods to Mr. Balfour's Economic Notes, in which this theory of exchange is clearly taken for granted,and also to the blue book just issued at the re- quest of the Cabinet, pages 99 to 104. This section 'begins with the following words "Of the whole of the commercial and financial- transactions between any I country and the outside world, which over a period of years thoughnot necessarily Within the limits of any . single year, mast balance one another, (Ally a portion , are embodied in the commodities which pass outward and inward as exports and impo'rts." And then I the section goes on to explain how trade is balanced and how freights, &amp;c. earned, should be added to exports. The point to note is, that, instead of paying for German rods in gold (our imports of gold exceed our exports) we pay in goods. If this be true (and I wish Protectionists would tackle the point, and either accept it or show us where it is unsound) then all the iron rods, and iron everything the world sends us, must be balanced by the same value in our produce or by service rendered. The account with each particular country may not square, but our transactions with the world as the Board of Trade publication says "must balance one I another." Then Mr. Woods may console his troubled conscience, for by buying iron rods from abroad he is making an opening for an export, making an export in , some form inevitable, to pay for the iron rods he buys, and more particularly he is making. his .own evport trade easier by lessening the cost of his finished article. Mr. Woods's action is profitable to the nation, because while he buys iron which he gets under its value, he assists exports te get full value for what they send out. We would suggest a further reason why Mr. Woods should continue buying all the cheap rods he can. He tells us that in the making of 1,000 tons of rods, Ger- many will require to use:up 3,oooltons of 0re,1,500 t tons of coal to make the pig ir0n,450 tons to convert the pig iron into billets, and 550 tons to make the billets into rods, in all 3,000 tons of ore and 2,500 tons of coal. Here we</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>" The Murder is Out"</head>
        <p>" The Murder is Out"</p>
        <p>Sir,—" The murder is out." This is Mr. Chamber- lain's own phrase in which he admitted at Glasgow that the taxation of food was an essential part of his scheme. That indeed was fairly obvious from the very beginning, because Mr. Chamberlain's justification for casting his bomb-shell into our politics is his desire to federate the Empire. There have been many Unionists who would have preferred a policy that would have avoided the taxation of food, and some of the more simple-minded have hoped to the last that the great conjuror would be able so to arrange his tricks as to avoid taxing the people's food. All things, however, are not possible, even with Mr. Chamberlain, and we find him saying at Glasgow as he has said before: "if you wish to have a preference you in,ust put a tax on food."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MAIZE AND BACON TEST.</head>
        <p>THE MAIZE AND BACON TEST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain's proposals are probably familiar to most of us II this time, but it!may bejust as well to briefly explain and examine them. In order to have a tariff which will permit of special favour to our Colonies, he wants a tax of 2s. a quarter on foreign corn (except maize), and is. in the £ on foreign meat (except bacon), as well as upon eggs butter and cheese. Now it is at once to be noted that Mr. Chamberlain does not propose to tax maize or bacon, but leaves these commodities alone, and for precisely the same reason. He saya...--</p>
        <p>-"I propose to put no tax whatever on maize, partly because maize is a food of some of the 'very poorest people and partly also because it is a raw material for the farmers who feed their pigs on it. "I propose to exclude bacon, because once more, bacon is a popular food with some of the poorest of the population. It forms the staple food for many of the poorest of the population." , This exemption of maize and bacon is remarkable, because speaking of these taxes upon food, Mr. Chambe- rlain asserts that they "would be paid by the foreigner." But if the foreigner pays the tax on meat, how comes it that he does not also pay a tax on bacon? If the foreigner pays a tax on wheat, why should he not pay a tax on maize? There can only be one reason for ex- empting maize and bacon,—namely that if these two articles were taxed,the tax would be paid by these very poor people. Mr. Chamberlain says very loudly and very often that the foreigner would pay, but actions speak louder than words. The fact that he does not pro- pose to tax maize anl bacox is the best possible proof that the tax which he proposes on food would be paid not by tit. joreigner but by the consumer. The result of this proposed tax on food would be that the Treasury would get each year about six millions. It must not be imagined, however, that this is the total amount which the consumer would have to pay. If we take for in- stance 2s. per quarter duty as charged on foreign wheat, with the result that the consumer paid 2s. more per quarter, there would be a corresponding rise in price of wheat grown in the Colonies or produced in this coun- try. The buyer of the wheat will have to pay the extra 2s. a quarter, whether the wheat comes from California or from Canada or from Cam- bridgeshire, but it is only in the case of the Californian wheat that the money 'would go to the State. This is true of all these new food taxes. As to these taxes on corn, meat and dairy produce, it has been calculated in figures which have not, and (I believe) cannot be dis- puted, that if six millions is raised in duties which go to the Treasury, the consumer will altogether have to pay in higher prices, the sum of over fifteen millions:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>'HANK YOU FOR NOTHING."</head>
        <p>'HANK YOU FOR NOTHING."</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain, however, in order to "compensate" the workingman, has what he calls some "great remis- sions" of taxation to propose. He proposes to take off three-quarters of the duty on tea, half of the duty on sugar with a corresponding reduction on cocoa and coffee. This would result in a loss to the Treasury of something like seven and a half millions a year. Now there is something which at once is to be said with re- gard to this proposal—it is a case of thank volt for nothing. Altogether apart from Mr. Chamberlain's pro- posals, the country is entitled to a large readjustment of the burden of taxation, and when that readjustment takes place, and new sources of revenue are fairly dealt with, the claim of the indirect taxpayer to be con- sidered is overwhelming. By the last Budget the ineome taxpayer was relieved of ten millions a year, the indirect taxpayer of only two-and-a-half millions. It is a curious coincidence that the difference between these two amounts is the very sum of Mr. Chamber- lain's proposed remittance. The workingman therefore will be very foolishifihe consents to a tax on his food in order to get &amp;missions of taxation on tea and sugar to which in any event he has an overwhelming claim. He can afford to reject Mr. Chamberlain's bribe, since the money with which it is to be paid is owed to him on another acdo nat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>DEAR GOODS.</head>
        <p>DEAR GOODS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Chamberlain estimates that the loss to the ,ITreasury due to 'taking off these taxes on tea, sugar. &amp;c., land imposing new taxes on foreign corn, meat and dairy I produce, would be £2,800,000 a year. This he proposes to get by imposing a tax of 2s. in the £ on foreign manufactured goods, which it is estimated wouldpro- duce nine millions. If. however, the result was (as seems to be the wish) to exclude these goods, the tax would produce nothing; whilst if they still came in it is difficult to see how there would be that enormous Increase of employment Mr. Chamberlain holds out as the attraction of his scheme. But the country has as much reason for objecting to a policy of Dear Goods, as to a policy of Dear Food. If foreign manufactured articles are taxed 2s. in the £, the tax will inevitably be paid by the consumer who will also have to pay an increased price for similar articles manufactured in this country. Mr. Ritchie, who to his credit resigned office rather than take any part in tampering with the Free Trade foundations of our Fiscal system, calcu- lates that the result of Mr. Chamberlain's scheme must be to reduce the purchasing power of a sovereign to 17s MI. What will all those members of the community with fixed incomes have to say about this? Lord Rosebery may well ask the question in his speech at Sheffield—the most brilliant speech which has yet been made on this fiscal question and one which will be found deeply instructive and deeply interesting by everyone concerned in the controversy and who is not?</p>
        <p>A. SUMMARY.</p>
        <p>Let me try to summinse very briefly this examination of Mr. Chamberlain's proposals : • In order to get six millions in taxation of foreign corn, meat, and dairy produce The consumer will have to pay for• corn, meat and dairy produce (foreign, Colonial and home grown) an additional sum of fifteen and a half millions. _ _</p>
        <p>Existing taxes of seven and a half millions on tea, sugar coffee and cocoa are to betaken off. This leaves the consumer eight millions to the bad.</p>
        <p>The loss to th-e treasury is to be -M-3710 up by taxing foreign manufactured goods, the result of which must be to make the consumer pay more for these articles and for similar articles manufactured in this country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>TRICKED MINISTERS.</head>
        <p>THE TRICKED MINISTERS.</p>
        <p>I have only space to add a word or two as to the remarkable proceedings which have occurred in connection with the resignation of those ministers who declined to abandon Free Trade. Mr. Balfour's concealment of Mr. Chamberlain's resigna- tion from Mr. Ritchie and Lord George Hamilton is so amazing that it would be incredible if it were not proved beyond question. Mr. Balfciur is only too glad to get rid of Mr. Ritchie the Free Trader, so that Mr. Austen Chamberlain may be -installed at the Treasury. there to act as the telephone between Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamberlain, whilst the latter is engaged upon his peat missionary enterprise, in trying to persuade the British people that two and two make five. It is a pity that the Duke of Devonshire was for the time de- luded into believing that it was possible for him to re- main in Mr. Balfour's Cabinet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DUKE OF DEVONSHIRE ON FREE TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE DUKE OF DEVONSHIRE ON FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>Nothing could be more admirable than the lecture in which the Duke of Devonshire resigned as the result of Mr. Balfour' s speech at Sheffield.</p>
        <p>"I had hoped to have found in your speech a definite statement of adherence to the principles of Free Trade as the ordinary basis of our fiscal and commercial system, and an equally definite repudia- tion of the principle of Protection in the interest of our national industries. But in their absence, I cannot help thinking that such declarations as those which Ihave quoted cannot fail to have the effect of materially encouraging the advocates of direct Protection in the controversy which has been raised throughout the country, and of discouraging those who, like me, and, I had hoped, yourself, believe that our present system of free imports,and especi- ally of food imports, is, on the whale the most ad- vantageous to the country, although we do not contend that the principles on which it rests possess any such aufhority or sanctity as to forbid any de- parture from it—for sufficient reasons." (the italics are my own). This in my opinion states the Free Trade case with admirable clearness and precision. These of us who adhere to Free Trade do so not because we believe it is a sin to be a Protectionist, but because we are con- vinced that the material prosperity of the Mother country, of the Colonies, and of the P.mpire are all of them bound up in a maintenance of the system which during the last sixty years has brought with it such un- told blessings.—Yours, etc.,</p>
        <p>A. H. CROSFIELD. Hotel 'Metropole, London, October 15th 1903.</p>
        <p>Postmen's Federation. The members of this organization have decided to become affiliated with the Trades and Labour Council and a delegate attended the annual meeting of the Council on Tuesday evening. FOR CLASS AND CHINA of every description try 3.. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>AMATEUR OPERA IN WARRINGTON AND ELSEWHERE.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR OPERA IN WARRINGTON AND ELSEWHERE.</p>
        <p>Ayhat with Mr. Thornley-Dodge.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Musical Society concerned in the production of" The Yeomen of the Guard" have been peculiarly fortunate in securing the services of Mr. E. Thornley-Dodge as stage manager and dramatic instructor. To many Warring- ton people, Mr. Thornley Dodge is already known as a popular humorist who has performed in the Parr Hall in that capacity, but never before has he assumed the duties in Warrington of piloting a society through the dramatic intricacies of light opera. It is custom- ary to think of Yeomen of the Guard as picturesque in- dividuals in attendance at Royal banquets and dressed after the fashion of the time of Henry VII., but in the hands of Messrs. Gilbert and Sullivan the Yeomen are handled quite differently, the characters in their libretti and the music to which these are set, having gained for their collaborated work a unique position. Mr. Thornley-Dodge has already proved that he possesses all the qualities necessary for the mental out- fit of a successful stage manager, and the Warrington public will have an opportunity next week of judging of his work when the curtain rises on the stage per- formance of "The Yeomen of the Guard," in, which a number of the members of the Warrington Musical Society will demonstrate their capabilities in amateur opera for the first time. It was with the object of obtaining a shorti account of Mr. Thornley-Dodge's experiences of amateur opera in Warrington and elsewhere that a reporter of "The Examiner" sought an interview with him. Mr. Dodge is a pleasant and affable man, as befits a humorist. He has a reputation still in the making as a stage-manager and dramatic instructor. It was with no light heart that Mr. Dodge complied with the request of the interviewer. " I look upon these interviews," he bluntly said, "'much in the same light as having a tooth drawn." The reporter expressed the hope that the experience would not be quite 'so painful and commenced busi- ness at once. in reply to a preliminary question as to his opinion of the forthcoming performance of "The Yeomen of the Guard," 111r.rThornley-Dodge said, "The opera is well cast. The principals have been chosen with great discernment, and they show an aptitude for the work which augurs well for the success of the performance. They enter thoroughly into the spirit of the thing and endeavour to give a liveliness and reality to the scene's."</p>
        <p>"Then you don't think they have been too ambitious in staging The Yeomen (A.:lth° Guard ?' " asked the reporter. _</p>
        <p>Not at all," was the prompt answer. "I have had no trouble at the rehearsals and I am feeling quite happy as to the performance. Everybody seems en- thusiastic for its success. I have had no trouble in teaching anybody anything; in fact many of the com- pany don't require any teaching, they have a natural aptitude for the stage.' "And what do you think of .our amateurs from a musical standpoint ?" "The vocal material is magnificent. I have never heard 'The Yeomen of the Guard' sung better than it was at a recent rehearsal. If they sing like that next week, well, they will electrify Warrington. Given a good Jack Point, and I have every confidence in Mr. C. P. Gough in that role,l think the whole perform- ance will go through splendidly. Mr. Crossley is a conductor who thoroughly understands his business. We agree on most points and get on very well to- gether." "Then you are very well pleased with the cast ?" "Yes, taken altogether the dramatis personae is a very strong one. I would not care at the present moment to particularise but I venture to predict that there will be more. than one personal success in this</p>
        <p>opera experiment."</p>
        <p>Whai was your earliest venture with operatic management Mr. Dodge?" "The first production was H.M.S. Pinafore ' in Bury. I have been stage manager to the Bury Athemeum Operatic Society for four successive years, and that was my first production. I had been asked to produce operas in Manchester, but had declined, being doubtful of, my ability in this respect. But the work proved very successful. Last year; the same society produded`The Mikado,' and the year before`The Pirates of Penzance' under my direction. I look upon the successes attained by the Bury society with a great amount of pleasure, especially as they include my first attempt at stage management of comic opera. I am perhaps better known in Bury than in any other Lancashire tows, for in addition to my work in con- nection with the Operalic Society, I have been stage manager for the 'Bury Amateur' of which com- bination Mr. Charles Kenyon was the leading spirit. Last season this society produced 'The Marriage of Kitty' and Under the Red Robe' at the Theatre Royal. I owe a great deal both to Mr. Kenyon and the members of the Operatic Society. The only opera I have produced in Manchester was the ' Geisha ' at the Victoria Theatre, Broughton, promoted by Miss Mary Agnew. This was, perhaps, the most success- ful amateur operatic porformance given in Manchester. Crowded houses testiked each night to its success." "And what are you engagements for the present</p>
        <p>season?"</p>
        <p>"I have on hand in addition to The Yeomen of the Guard' at Warriugton, the production of The Royal Family'at St. Ames-on-the-Sea' The Gondoliers' at Bury, and lolanthe ' at Brooklands,iwhore I shall make my first bow with the society in the part of the 'Lord Chancellor.' Then I anticipate the production of Falka ' at Buxton, most probably The Mikado' or Ruddigore ' at St. Armes-on-the-Sea, and possibly San Toy' or `TheYeornen of the Guard' at Manchester. "In which of your own impersonations have you been most successful ? "</p>
        <p>"Well. I think my favourite part and also my most successful appearance, was as Jack Point in the Yeomen of the Guard.' I played this part with the Manchester Operatic Society at the Prince's Theatre some years ago. Other favourite parts have been Mr. Owen in 'Liberty Hall' and Rupert of Hentzau in 'The Prisoner of Zenda.' My highest hope—l do not know whether I shall ever realise it—is to play Mercutio in 'Romeo and Juliet.'"</p>
        <p>"Don't you find the work exhausting ? " "It is hard at times, but it is interesting, and it is ' nice to see a play go through well. 1 think I always see the humorous side of things, and at the rehearsals we always get on well, without any bother." "Could you favour me with a few personal details?" "I was born 4t Rusholme," Mr. Dodge began, "And ' my schooldays were as uneventful as the days of schoolboys usually are. I was educated by Mr. John Livesey, a well-known Manchester tutor. Even as a schoolboy, however, I had a great longing to become a professional actor ; but the 4people.at home sedul- ouslfdiscouraged the idea, and if they could have had their way I should never have taken up the profession. After school I went into business and was employed by Messrs. Parker Lord and Company, lining mann= facturers, of Middleton. I was with them twelve and a half years and they treated me kindly and consider- ately. They were aware of my bent for the stage and encouraged me in every way. I commenced with a few appearances in public as a hn florist and as engagements began to thicken, I definitely Abandoned business and became a professional four years ago. I ' have been very lucky so far and have had no occasion to regret the step I have taken. was in 1899,when Mr. Charles Kenyon, of Bury, invited me to play in 'Liberty Hall,' and as he had no stage-manager, and I knew the play pretty well he asked me to take the position."</p>
        <p>"And you accepted the pogition of .stage manager?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, -and took:: a small part in the —play. Prom that day I seemed to drift naturally into stage man- agership. I used to think when I was in business I was pretty well worked, but since I have done this I have realised a little bit more what real work is."</p>
        <p>- "In the summer, I suppose you are engaged at the fashionable spas and watering-places as a public entertainer?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, I have just completed a long list of engage- ments of that kind. I was at Southport three times, and at a week's notice I joined a London Opera Com- pany there, and took Mr. Roland Cunningham's part of Cosmo Lombard' in Toledad.' I got through to the entire satisfaction of the management. Then I have been at Blackpool, Skegness (four weeks), Ply- m.riuth, Eastbourne, Clacton-on-Sea, Tynemouth, ' Cleelhorpes and Harrogate. I had a great compliment paid me by Sir Henry Irving at the dinner at the Manchester Arts Club last year. He congratulated me ' highly on my sketch Robinson Crusoe,' and he sent me a signed photograph with an enclosure With good wishes.' rhat sketch has been a great success! It is not my favourite sketch; I have others I like better."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RESIGNATION OF ALDERMAN MONKS</head>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF ALDERMAN MONKS</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk yesterday received a letter from Alderman Monks resigning his seat on the Council. We are not in a position to publish Mr. Monks' letter in which he gives his reasons for the step he has taken, but it will be remembered that at the September meeting of the Council, he exPlainedithat the reason why he could not accept the position of a Commission- er at the inquiry was that ',changes had taken place in connection with his own business which had placed more responsibilities on his shoulders, and he had much less timo to spare than he had even so recently as a year ago. He had during the year resigned from three committees and it would be absurd for him to undertake responsibilities of the kind suggested." The retirement of Alderman Monks from the Council will, we feel sure, be generally regretted. His busi- ness aptitude made him a valuable member of the Council. For many years he was Chairman of the Sanitary Works Committee and during his Chair- manship carried out some much - needed reforms at Longford and at the intercepting depots. More recently he accepted the position of Chairman of the Tramways and Electricity Com- mittee and entered zealously into the preliminary work necessary for the introduction of electric light and the tramways into the Borough. In educa- tional matters, Mr. Monks has always taken a deep interest and has done much use- ful work in promoting technical education and more recently in inaugurating a Secondary school and assisting in carrying out the riew Education Act. His retirement from the Council causes him to sever his connection with the Educatfon Committee, as he was a representative member of the Council on that Com- mittee. It is to be hoped that he may be prevailed upon to become a co-opted member of the Education Committee and /thus continue his close connection with educational work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>JUDGE AND ADVOCATE.</head>
