        <p>Mr. Monks entered the Council as a representative of Whitecross Ward in 1890. Three years ago he .vas appointed an Alderman to fill the casual vkicancy, caused by the retirement of the late Mr. Aldermakt- Harrison from the Council.</p>
        <p>A Local Artist. Mr. Fred Podmore has on exhibition, in Mr. Jordan's shop, Bridge-street, a• successful portrait in pastels of a Warrington gentleman, We understand the artist has several impnrtant commissions on hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE FEDERATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TEMPERANCE FEDERATION.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews and Licensing Legislation.</p>
        <p>'that the temperance movement in Warrington would catch some of its old, spirit and that they would hear more about it in future. (Applause.) Mr. W. H. SIIERBURN also spoke. Had it not been for the drink power, he contended, they would have heard nothing of the fiscal question. They were told that other countries were in a better position than England simply because they had had Protection. There was only one country (Wall those mentioned that was in a better position than England. socially, and especially in regard to the working people. The United States spent in drink only onehalf per head of the popu- lation of what we spent in this country. That was one great secret of why thepeople were in abetter position. Mr. Sherburn attributed the loss of trade to the intemperance of many of the workpeople and also con- tended that drunkenness was responsible for the present housing conditions. During the evening songs were renderedby Miss K. Bowyer an Mr. J. Sheath recited. Mr. W. Jackson was the accompanist. The usual votes of thanks con- cluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
