      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>AN EXPLANATORY CATECHISM.</head>
        <p>AN EXPLANATORY CATECHISM.</p>
        <p>1. What is Gieilym Brans' Quinine Bitters?</p>
        <p>An excellent vegetable Remedy, prepared in the most skilful and scientific manner, and containing the active principles of those plants generally acknowledged to be the best remedial agents, and to possess the most heal- ing and curative properties. It is now extensively used in many countries, and everywhere acknowledged as the Best Tonic Remedy known.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>2. What does it contain?</head>
        <p>2. What does it contain?</p>
        <p>Quinine and the active principles of the following well-known herbs: Sarsaparilla, Saffron; Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, and Dandelion, scientifically pre- pared, and in such happy proportions as to form a Tonic Bitters positively unequalled.</p>
        <p>3. What is its action</p>
        <p>It assists ani. promotes Digestion, strengthens t Nerves and Muscles, purifies the Blood, enlivens the Spirits, and removes all Obstructions and Impurities in the Human Body. It gives tone to the whole system. It strengthens and fortifies those parts which have been weakened by Disease, and are consequently more liable to Colds and unhealthful influences. It has ga;ned the Highest Recommendations as the Best Family Medicine and a remedy for every kind of Weakness.</p>
        <p>What Diseases is it specially recommended fcr 7</p>
        <p>Influenza, and the after effects of an attack of Influenza, T-idigestion, Chest Affections and Diseases of the Liver in all their various forms, Neuralgia and all Nervous Disorders, Depression of Spirits, Geneial Weakness, Loss of Appetite, Sleeplessness.</p>
        <p>Must patients confine themselves within doors when using it?</p>
        <p>No; the working man need not abstain from his labour; the mother from her household duties, or the child be kept from school while using lt.</p>
        <p>6. Is there any difficulty in procuring it?</p>
        <p>There need be none, as it can be obtained of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors, or will be forwarded direct by the Proorietors, carriage free, on receipt of stamps. It is sold in bottles 2s. gaud 4.5. 6d. each. Beware of Imitations. See the name " Gwilym Evans" on Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A WARR INGTON WOM AN'S VIOLENCE-SMART SENTENCE.</p>
        <p>Annie Smith, who is well-known to the police, had some lively adventures on Sunday even:ng which re- sulted in her appearance before the magistrates at the Warrington Police Court the following mornlng. She was charged with having been drunk at the Workhouss at 10.45 p.m. on Sunday, with breaking 13 squnres of glass in the windows of the casual ward, assaulting Edmond Hewitt, the porter, and damaging two rugs at the Police Station to the amount of 7s.— The evidence showed that prisoner was admitted to the casual ward and the porter went to consult the master about her. During his absence she broke 13 panes of glass in the windows and on being given into custody deliberately kicked Hewitt. Prisoner was placed in a cell at the Police Station and Sergeant Williamson soon afterwards found that she had torn two •rugs to shreds.—The Chief Constable said prisoner only came out of gaol a week on Saturday after serving six months. This was her 36th appearance.—Prisoner,who was offen- sive in the dock, was sentenced to six months imprison- ment. As she was being removed below she shouted "I can do that like a bird."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.-_-_Before Messrs. T. pirchall (in the chair)</p>
        <p>R. T. Fairclongb, and Dr._ Foi.</p>
        <p>THROWING SAUCERW.—VairiCk Walsh, Smith-street, was charged with using abusive language in Smith- street on Fridsy night and also with assaulting his wife. —P.C. Tolley said the prisoner was creating a disturb- ance in the street and throwing saucers at his wife,— Complainant added that her husband had thrown saucers at her in the house. He was the worse for drink.— Prisoner said his wife provoked him.—He was bound over to keep the peace for six months, and fined ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>MoNT:kit-Y.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alder- men Smethurst, Councillor Thornton and Mr. S. Wright. THEFT OF TIMBER.—John Moran was charged with stealing from Arpley Rope Walk a quantity of timber of the value of 6d. the property of John Herbert Fleming.—The Chief Constable stated that at 9.25 on Saturday morning the prosecutor was in the joiners' shed and saw prisoner walking away with the timber under his arm. He asked him what he was doing and prisoner said he was taking it away for firewood. The police were informed and P.C. Millington apprehended the prisoner and found the timber at his house.—Mr. Fleming informed the Bench that ten valuable, hens and three pups had recently been stolen from his premises. Within six months property to the value of £lO had been missed.—A fine of ss. was imposed.</p>
        <p>DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT IN SANKEY-STREET.—Thomas King was charged with making use:of obscene language in Sankey-street at 6.10 on Saturday evening.—rho Chief Constablesaid he was walking along Sankey-st. at the time in question and when near the Co-operative Stores he saw three ladies standing opposite the prisoner who was sober. Prisoner walked up to them and made use of a filthy expression. W:tuess gave him into the custody of P.C. Phillip.—A fine of ss. and costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—OR the evidence of P.C. Clough, Sarah Ann Quinn was sentenced to 14 days' imprisonment for begging in Palymra-square at 620 on Saturday evening. WOULD NOT PAY THE TRAM FARE.—Joseph T. Gordon, 65, Robson-street, was summoned for refusing to pay the fare whilst travelling on the Corporation trams on the 6th inst.—Defendant did not appear and the Chief Constable remarked that Mr. Gordon was a schoolmaster in the town up to a few days ago and was ofjsuperior education.—After some discussion, the Bench decided to hear the case in defendant's absence, but the Chairman said that they would not hear any more cases under the same conditions.—Mr. Stanley Wilson deputy town clerk, conducted the prosecution and Wilson, that defendant travelled on the top of the car which left the Cemetery at 8•4.5. He was in a drunken condition and absolutely refused to pay the fare, stating that he wanted to be locked up. At the Central Station the assistance of a constable had to be obtained to get defendant out of the car.—ln the course of the evidence it was pointed out that defendant must have boarded the car when the con- ductor was changing his box at the other end, and thus escaped observation,—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed. CHIMNEY ON FIRE.—For having the chimney of her dwelling-house on fire on October 3rd, Mrs. Thomas, 20, Fennel-street, was fined is.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Dr. Fox (in the chair), Alderman Hutchinson, and Mr. J. R. Young.</p>
        <p>FIGHTING.—WiIIiam Coyne was charged' with COM- tnitting a breach of the peace by fighting at Market Gate on September 14th.—He as bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.7-Before Mr. T. Birchen_ (in the chair),</p>
        <p>Aldermen Hutchinson and Heaton.</p>
        <p>A SAD CASE.—Margaret Forrest, a young married woman living in Chester's-row, was charged with steal- ing from the pledge office of Benedict Ty rer, pawnbroker, Winwick-street, between five and six o'clock on Tuesday evening a skirt value 3s. 11d.—The Chief Constable said the case was a painful one. Prisoner went into the pledge office and on her departure the skirt was missed, and information was given to the police. The same evening prisoner took the skirt to Messrs. Jackson's pawnshop but the manager was suspicious and questioned her. Ultimately she was apprehended by P.C.'s Whittaker and Clough and at once admitted the theft. She stated that she was in very low circumstances and had been tempted to steal the skirt. The magistrates decided to record a conviction and discharge the Prisoner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Cats is Dogs</head>
        <p>Cats is Dogs</p>
        <p>In the United States, we offered I reward for finding anything wrong in the book on Fels-Naptha soap; and a woman claimed it for saying we had left out cats. She said it is as good for cats as for dogs.</p>
        <p>Cats is dogs,</p>
        <p>We answered: and dogs is in it. She didn't get the money; and cats is still dogs.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha 39 WiISOD Street LOlldo7l E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>-- STACK FIRE NEAR HALTION</p>
        <p>A hugh shed containing several stacks of hay belong- ing to Mr. T. Smith, Halton-view, s;tuated near Halton Station, was fired, it is said, by some boys at noon on Saturday. The brigade was quickly in attendance, and it WB3 not before half an hour's playing on the flames that tbey wete got under. The damage is estimated at a little under £2OO. _</p>
        <p>WIDNES BLA.CKLISTER SENT TO GOAL.—At Widnes on Monday, John Codney, an habitual drunkard, on the black list, with thirty five cony ictions, was sent to gaol for one month without the option of a fine for being drunk on Satm day e v ening. Constable Parr saw him i this condition rear the felr ground, and upon being taken into custody he became very wolent,necessitating the aid of two other constables to take him to the lockup. A RUNCORN MAN'S FIND.</p>
        <p>An employs of the Manchester Ship Canal Company named George Holland, of Holloway road, Runcorn, found in the canal near Frodsham Marsh, on Saturday,a small pickle bottle securely corked. It contained a piece of paper on which was written "To anybody who finds this. lon October 4th, 1903, drowned myself in tbn Weaver. lam tired of life. Good-bye S. W., Run- corn." The police state that two Runcorn persons are missing, but the initials " S.W." are different from both of theirs. WANTED A WINTER IN GAOL.</p>
        <p>Thomas Randalls, of no fixed abode, was brought up at Widnes on Monday upon a warrant chargedwith non- compliance with an order made upon him in February .last for family maintainance. His wife, Mary jane, said she had only received Bs. and 7s. 6d. during the period the order w.-. 1 in force, and the arrears totalled £l2.—Tke Prisoner :—T have no,th'ng to say—only that I will pay if she will come and live with me.—The Chairman: You must go to gaol for one month's hard labour.—The Prisoner: Send me to gaol for six mouths, so that I shall be there for the winter.—The Chairman: Sit down.—The Plisoner (to his wife): I will murder you when I come out! RUNCORN DROWNING FATALITIES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon James Percival, aged two years, son of a labourer living in Pennington-row, Runcorn, fell into a duckpond, and was drowned. There were only a &amp;tag inches of water in the pond. Police- constable Davies applied artificial respiration for over an hour, without effect.—Last evening Mr. Byfield, cap- tain of Runcorn Grappling Corps recovered from the Western Canal, Runcorn Docks, the body of Abram Dale, mate of the schooner Mary Fanny, who has been missing since the night of the storm last Tuesday, when he left his lodgings to go on board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY MISHAP AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>About flue o'clock on Monday night two coaches of an empty passenger train, which were being shunted at St,. Helens Station of the London and North-Western Rail- way, left the rails 'near the Raven-street signal-box. Very great inconvenience was caused, and for three hours the traffic on the main line to and from Liverpool was worked by single line. The breakdown gang from Peasley Cross, under Inspector Bonney, lifted the coaches on to the lines.</p>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDE,NT AT A ST. HELVSS GLASS WORKS. On Tuesday, at St. Helens, Mr. S. Brighopse, county coroner, held an inquiry into the death of John John- son who died as a resqlt of injuries received at the glass-bending works of Messrs. W. Gardner and Sons. Johnson was assisting some other men to remove a large empty glass case wilen by some means the case fell otter on to him, inflicting fatal injuries. The jury returned a verdict of "accidenial death." Mr. Gardner said this was the first fatal accident which they had had at the works for at least thirty-five years. The Coroner said that that was very creditable to the firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>DANGEROUS PRACTICE.</head>
        <p>A DANGEROUS PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>The carelessness of Railway Travellers in throwing articles from the windows of swiftly-moving trains is the cause of frequent injury to Platelayers and other ra;lroad men. An empty bottle thrown from a train at fifty or sixty miles an hour has the force of a gunshoi,. Newspapers are about the only thing that can be safely thrown out. A newspaper cennot hurt, and it has been known to save life. A Platelayer en the Great Central Railway read in a newspaper how Dr. Williams' pink pills bad cured a woman who suffered from chron;c indigestion in exactly the same way as his own wife, Mrs. Bartholomew, 6, Fowler-street, Wincobank, Sheffield. "I was under doctors for three years," she said; "but my disease was chronic. My appetite completely failed I could not sleep for the pains PI my fside and chest. Even milk gave me frightful pains •, my legs and feet swelled so that I could hardly walk." In consequence of a newspaper article Mrs. Bartholomew began to use Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. "After the third box," said she"l was completely cured: I have not been out of sorts since, arid have increased a stone in weight." Indigestion, bile, anemia, consumption, and rheumatism had been cured by the same means. Travellers desiring to dispose.of Empty Bottles and other articles are asked by Railway Companies to leave them under seats or inLuggage Racks in the Carriages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>S.P.G. MEETING IN THE PARR HALL:</head>
        <p>S.P.G. MEETING IN THE PARR HALL:</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the annual Warring- ton meeting in aid of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts which was held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening under the presidency of Lieutenant Colonel J. D. Buckton, V.D.—ln the course of his address the chairman pointed out that there were on the society's list 729 ordained mission- aries (including 10 bishops and 128 natives). There were also about 3,000 lay teachers,3,2oo students and 40,000 children in the mission schools. The cost of ail this seemed to have been about £150,000 last year, when the subscriptions, donations, and collections amounted to £119,837. It was evident that there was great economy, and no waste of money. Their clergy at home were unanimous in telling them that,although their charity should begin at home it ought not to end there—(applause)--that they ought to do something for their fellow men abroad according to their means, some by going abroad and working there, others by working at home, and the rest by contributing when asked to the funds required for the very existence of all such work. Could they find any such society more deserving of their support than the S.P.G.?—lnterest- ing addresses were delivered by the Rev. E. W. Hines, the Rev. Canon Sloman, and the Rev. H. Moore, who gave descriptions of work in the mission field.—The Rev. E. St. A. Duke, organizing secretary, (formerly of Warrington), in proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman, said that when he got into Winwick-street that evening and emerged from the Central Station, the delicious blend of breweries and tanneries seemed to him like home again (Laughter and applause.) The large meeting that evening showed that the people of Warrington had the interests of the S.P.G. at heart. Twenty three years ago he attended an S.P.G. meeting in Warring- ton in the old Masonic rooms in Sankey-street. There were only twenty present, and that showed that the people had awakened up considerably owing to the influence of the Junior Clergy Missiortary Association. (Applause.)—The Rev. Canon Penrhyn seconded the motion, which was carried. During the evening hymns were sung, Mr. Percy Randall presiding at the organ. The collection realized £7 13s. id.</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIA'S NEW CAPITAL.</p>
        <p>'The Federal Senate, at Melbourne on Thursday, by nineteen votes to ten rejected Tumut as the site of the Federal capital, and selected Bombala. Bombala is a township in New South Wales, in Wellesley County, 319 miles south-south-west of Sydney. It has apopula- tion of about I,soo.—Reuter.</p>
        <p>The Original Cocoa, and a Speciality,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>EPPS'S</head>
        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>being distinguished from all others by its invigorating nutritious qualities and its delicious flavour. This Cocoa, con- taining as it does all the substance of the Cocoa Nib, maintains its leading position after three-quarters of a Century as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>COCOA</head>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>the best form of Cocoa for every-day use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>NEW LOCAL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>A NEW LOCAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Foundation Stone Laid at N ewchurch by Lord Lathom.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon the Earl of Lathom laid,with full masonic honours, the foundation stone of New- church Church, Culcheth, to replace the historic pile burned down last Easter. The old church was rich in historic association. The parish of Newchurch which has an area of over 10 square miles and em- braces the townships of Culcheth, and Kenyon, dates back to the reign of Elizabeth. The church stands upon the estate of the Holcrofts of Holcroft and it is generally assumed to have been a daughter church of the ancient church of Winwick and this the name of Newchurch would appear to bear out. The registers date from 1599, and the communion plate has been used continuously for more than 216 years. This plate, happily rescued from the fire was the gift of Dr. Sherlock, rector of Winwick, to Bishop Wilson (of Sodor and Man) who at this time was curate-in-charge of Newchurch, holding this position from 1686 to 1692. Perhaps the most romantic incident connected with the ancient church was the marriage of the notorious Colonel Blood, who in the Cromwellian period leapt into fame by an audacious attempt to steal the Crown jewels from the Tower. It was hare that Colonel Blood married the beautiful Maria Holcroft, the daughter of Colonel Holcroft, who gained some fame by his fighting with the Parliamentarians during the Civil wars. The new building will be in the Norman style of architecture, with a tower 51 feet high and providing seating accommodation for 500 persons. The total cost is £7.000, including furnishing, and towards this £6,000 has been raised. IThe stone, which was laid on Tuesday afternoon, bore the following inscription :—" This foundation stone was laid with masonic ceremonies by the Right Hon. the Earl of Lathom, P.G.W., R.W. Prow. G.M. of West Lancashire, .on Tuesday, October 13th, 1903. T. E. Withington, J. Boardman, churchwardens; E. W. Whittenbury Kaye, rector." The Earl of Lathom was accompanied by members of the Provincial Grand Lodge, Western division of Lancashire, many being present from Warrington,Leigh, Earlestown and the surrounding. district. A procession was formed from a tent which had been erected in the immediate -vicinity and as the brethren paraded in their regalia and jewels, the sight was pic- turesque, and impressive in its dignity. The surpliced choir, with the Rector at their head occupied the place of honour in the procession and they were followed by clergy in the diocese, including Bishop Chavasset of Liverpool; Canon Penrhyn, rural dean • Canon Stanntng, (Leigh) ; Rev. W.H.Carr. (Leigh) ; Rev. Geo. Feather, (Glazebury) ;Rev. Thos. Ellison, Bishop's chaplin ; Rev. T. R. Matthews (Earlestown), Rev. R. Smith(Lowton), Rev. W. Bracecamp (Warrington), Rev. T. Priest (Faza- kerley), Rev. M. Rogers (Winwick), Rev. F. Chadwick (Winwick), Rev. A. W. Thompson (Pennington), Rev. J. Carson (Wargrave.) Amongst the brethren accompanying the Earl of Lathom were the Rev. H. V. Pigot (Provincial Grand Chaplain), Captain Withington, the Mayor of Leigh (Councillor W. Ho rrocks ), Mr. Lloyd (Leigh), Mr. Roger, Parr, Mr. Lister Kaye, Dr. Sephton, Councillor Rogers, Mr. T. Stringer Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, Mr. F. Howard, Mr. Craddock, Mr. Shacklady, Mr. R. H. Pearson, Mr. Hesketh, Mr. J. Critchley, Mr. Rose, and Mr. Kenyon, of Warrington. _ _</p>
        <p>The choir sang Psalm cxxii and afterwards the hymn "0 Lord of hosts "was sung. The Rector called upon Lord Lathom to lay the stone, and Mr. Ramsden, the architect, presented him with a trowel for the purpose Lord Lathom in complying with the request, made the following declaration: Be it known to you that we, being lawful masons, true and faithful unto the laws of our country, are engaged by solemn obligation to erect a handsome building to be serviceable to the brethren and the fear of God, the great architect of the universe. We have among us, concealed from the eyes of all men, secrets which may not be revealed, and which no man has discovered, but these secrets are law- ful and honourable, and not repugnant to the laws of God and man. They were entrusted in peace and honour to the masons of ancient times, and having been faith- fully transmitted to us, it is our duty to give them unimpaired to posterity; otherwise our craft or calling would not have lasted for so many centuries,nor should;we have had so many illustrious brethren in our order ready to promote our laws and further our interests. We assemble here to-day in the name of Him who is the Wisdom and the Light to lay the foundation stmle of a church, the object of which is prayer and praise and. religious instruction to the residents in this neighbour- hood, which, we pray, may prosper as it seemeth good to Him. As the first duty of masons in any under- taking is to invoke the blessing of the Great Architect of the universe on their work, I call upon you to unite with our Provincial Grand Chaplain in an address of praise._ __</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. V. PIGOT read a prayer, after which the Provincial Grand Treasurer (Brother T. W. Travers, of „Leigh), deposited the phial containing the coins, and 'the Provincial Grand Secretary (Brother W. Goodacre) placed the plate on the lower stoa. Lord Lathom spread the cement, and adjusted the upper Mone, and then strewed corn and poured wine and oil over it with the accustomed ceremonies. His Lordship then de- clared the stone " well and truly laid." BISHOP CREVASSE AND THE FREEMASONS. The Lord Bishop said that when six months ago the news spread throughout the diocese that the church of Newehurch had been burnt down he thought the feeling uppermost in their minds was one of consternation. Those of them who cared for history regarded the old church as full of interest from the historical point of view, as the place where a great saint had once ministered, and where, he thought he might add, a great rogue had once married. The burning of that church had not ceased to be a calamity, but he ventured to say it was a calamity big with mercies. It had called forth wonderful sympathy, kindness, self-sacrificeand energy. Lord Lathom, like Roderick Dhu in Scott's immortal poem, put his bugle to his lips, and lo! the Freerhasons started up, and for the second time in a few months gathered round and helped the church in a day of need. (Hear, hear.) He thanked Lord Lathom and the Freemasons, in the name of the diocese of Liverpool, with all his heart for the great help they had given them that day. Captain Withington, whom they all knew and loved, came for- ward and put down his name for the munificent sub- scription of £5OO. The Lord Lieutenant of the county (Lord Derby) also came forward, and although, he understood, he had little or no land to speak of in the neighbourhood, put his namedown for a second £5OO. The liberality of those two distinguished gentlemen had wrought wonders, and now the Rector told him that six thousand of the seven thousand pounds required had already been subscribed. (Applause.) A calamity which could draw forth such self-sacrifice such liberality, such generosity, was a blessing in disguise; nay, more, the whole country side, it seemed, had been moved. He could only hope that in years to come—when they had finished their work here below and passed to their account and their Grand Master— Ile who made the world and redeemed them with His precious blood, and to whom they would one day have to give an account, would be able to say to them wha Lord Lathom had been able to say that day: "Thet craftsmen and worshippers have wrought and worshipped well." (Applause.) • Lord LATHOM, replying to his lordship, said he was glad to think it was not the first time they had met on such an occasion, and he hoped they might have many such happy meetings in the future. (Hear, hear.) So long as Freemasons acknowledged and knew the suprem- acy of the Church, and loved the Church, all would go well with their Order. They owed a great deal in that diocese to the bishop's untiring exertions—(hear, hear) and they were very proud of him. (Ileac, hear). He (Lord Lathom) was sire that the energy displayed by the churches throughout the diocese and the building of edifices only made the possibility of money being sub- scribed to the Liverpool Cathedral much better than it ever was. (Hear, hear.) They looked forward very earnestly to seeing the Cathedral rising very soon, and he was sure his lordship would not find Freemasons backward in trying to realize that hope... (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>The BISHOP then pronounced the Beilediaion rind the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ROYAL v. FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>These teams met on the ground of the former at Stockton Heath on Saturday, the game resulting in a victory for Frodsham by four goals to nil. Powell, of the Royal, had the misfortune to get hurt just after the interval, but pluckily took up play again after-a few minutes' rest. Next Saturday the Royal encounter Ins- ley at Disley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MOOR TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>THE MOOR TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>At the adjourned hearing at Huddersfield of the charge of murdering the two gamekeepers on Buckstones Moor brought against Henry Buckley, Dr. Irving gave the result of his examination of the exhumed body of Uttley. He said the bloodstains found on the coat of the prisoner were of mammalian blood. The, defence opened on Thursday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WAR COMMISSION REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE WAR COMMISSION REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Augustine Birrell, speaking at Bristol, referred to the War Commission's report. He should-rejoice if Lord Kitchener would undertakethe patriotic duty of reform- ing the War Office. The culprits most to blame for our disasters were the Cabinet. The chief culprit had left, and was running up and down the country, not defend- ing his action, but declaring a war of hostile tariffs against the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LOCAL TRADE UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>LOCAL TRADE UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>MR. F. STOTT REVIEWS THE , YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Trades and Labour Council.</p>
        <p>An interesting and comprehensive review of trade union work in %Varrington during the past twelve months was submitted at the annual meeting of the Trades and Labour Council held in Bold-street Wesleyan Schools on Tuesday. evening. The President (Mr. J. Edwardson), was unable to attend, as he had met with an accident. Mr. Jolley presided at the outset, but later on the Vice-President (Mr. J. Podmore) occupied the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TRADE DEPRESSION</head>
        <p>TRADE DEPRESSION</p>
        <p>The annual report presented by the Secretary (Mr. F. Stott) was admirably compiled and gave a mass of interesting information. It was stated that the combined membership of the branches which had become affiliated with the Council during the year was 230, and the total membership of the branches which had withdrawn was 105, leaving an increase of 125. The proposal to build a Trades Hall in the town was next dealt with. "We believe,"said Mr. Stott,"thatja Trades Hall would bring the various branches more in touch with each other, and give an opportunity to many of them to remove their meetings away from licensed premises, some of which are unsuitable for trades union meetings. We regret to state that since our last annual meeting the trade in Warrington has not been up to the average of former years. The workers in the iron, cotton, wire-drawing. and building industries have suffered considerably through lack of employment. The dvression in trade appears to be very general at present. From the Board of Trade returns we learn that the percentage of unem- ployed in those trades making returns to the Labour Department, is much higher than at the corresponding period of last year, which indicates that the coming winter is likely to be one of great distress owing to lack of employment. We trust, however, that provi- sion will be made, to enable Town Councils to provide remunerative employment for those who require it." Mr. Stott makes a brief reference to the wire weavers' dispute which ended in the men being defeated. The main factor to bringabout the defeat, says Mr. Stott, was that many of the men who came out on strike went back to work. BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>Daring the year the report continues, the question of municipal bowling greens has been again discussed by your Council, and we are pleased to report that as a result of our repeated appeals the Town Council have at last decided to make provision for at least one bowl- ing green being provided within the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PROPAGANDA.</head>
        <p>PROPAGANDA.</p>
        <p>In referring to the recent labour demonstration Mr. Stott says :—The meeting was exceedingly successful, and we trust that, as a result of the enthusiasm dis- played by the large audience present, it will assist the local labour organizations in deciding whether it is advisable to take steps to secure a labour candidate for Warrington. LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>We are pleased, continued Mr. STorr, to again refer to the growth of the National Labour Representation Committee, to which your Council is affiliated. There is now a membership of over 900,000 and we confidently expect that the numbers will soon be increased to over one million. Since our last report three members have been returned to the House of Commons under the auspices of the committee, viz., Mr. D. J. Shackleton for Clitheroe, Mr. W. (kooks for Woolwich, and Mr. A. Henderson for Barnard Castle. We noticed with pleas- ure the firm stand for "independence" which was express- ed at the last annual conference of the committee held in Newcastle believing that to be the only safe and successful course to adopt, if we are to win the workers from their old political faith. During the year your Council has 'become affiliated withlthe Warrington La- bour Representation Committee, which is yet far from being representative of ,srganized labour in Warrington. We trust however, one of the results of the recent demonstration will be a large increase in the member- ship so that it will be possible to give practical effect to the ever increasing desire for direct labour represen- tation in our towns. One of the affiliated organizations the Warrington Independent Labour Party, has decided to contest Orford Ward at the forthcoming municipal elections, and has invited your Secretary to become their candidate. We express the desire that members of the Council will give all possible assistance to the candidature so that he may be successful at the poll and thereby pave the way for other Labour representatives. Your council was successful in securing a representa- tive on the Education Committee, Mr. Edwin Plinston having been elected as a co-opted member to represent the Trades Unionists. We are pleased tik express our entire satisfaction with the way in which he has carried out his duties as a member of that coramittee. TRADE DISPUTES.</p>
        <p>We are unable to report any improvement in the law as affecting Trade Unions and disputes. During the year a Billiwas introduced into the House of Commons by Mr. D. J. Shackleton, M.P., whichhad for its object the "Legalizing of peaceful conduct during trades disputes." We regret, however, that the measure was rejected by a small majority. A Royal Commission has since been appointed to "inquire into the law affecting Trades Unions and the right of combination." Owing to the Commission being entirely representative of the employing and middle classes' steps were immediately taken with the object of trying to influence the government to appoint at least one direct representa- tive of Trades Unionism as a member, but our efforts were unsuccessful. On the recommendation of the TradeslUnion Congress all Trade Union officials who were invited, have declined to give evidence before the Commission. We are entirely in accord with their action believing that the Commission is of too partisan a character to arrive at a fair decision, and furthermore we are of an opinion that the constitution of the Com- mission is a direct insult to the whole Trades Union movement.</p>
        <p>RETALIATION AND PREFERENTIAL TARIFFS.</p>
        <p>It is impossible in this report to deal with the ques- tion of" Retaliation" and "Preferential Tariffs,"as fully as the subject deserves. The question has been dis- cussed by your Council, and a resolution was unani- mously carried expressing disapproval of any change in our Fiscal Policy on the lines suggested by Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, M.P. While believing in Free Trade as a principle, we recognize that it alone cannot now en- sure us prosperity. We, however, are of the opinion that any alteration in the shape of a tax on food and raw materials, would not only be unwise, but highly dangerous. We have no guarantee that wages would rise, nor that the chances of employment would increase by adopting the suggested fiscal changes. Instead of placing a tax on imports we would suggest that industry should be relieved by the nationalization of the mines and tke railways, be- lieving that to be a sure remedy for the present indus- trial crisis. We regret the possibility there is of the workers having their attention drawn away from the many pressing social problems by the suggested tariff reforms. We do not hesitate to say that many of our statesmen were becoming alarmed at the growth of the Labour representation movement, and are hoping that the question of" Preferential Tariffs "will retard that growth, and again divide those workers who had broken away from the old party ties. We trust, however, that the trade,, unionist will not in this great" fiscal fight " forget the Taff Vale and other decisions, the questions of Old Age Pensions, and many other much-needed social and industrial reforms.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON moved that the Council endorse the report of the Secretary, and also thank him for the ex- haustive and able manner in which he had drawn it up. Mr. Stott had touched upon the questions dealt with briefly and concisely. To his (Mr. Plinston's) mind the all important question was that of direct labour repre- sentation. That, to his mind, was far before fiscal policy orany other juggling which might take place by members of the Cabinet or ex-Cabinet, who wished to cloak the matter and so snatch another victory as was done on the khaki question. (Hear, hear.) They could not afford to let that matter drop, and the only remedy for their grievances was direct labour representation. If they kept that question constantly before them and worked to the utmost to obtain representatives, they could depend upon it that the proposed change in the fiscal policy would be done for." The labour members would not support Mr. Chamberlain's scheme. They knew what it meant—that it was a policy that was going to turn topsy turvy the whole system of Free Trade by which they had profited for years. In spite of what might be said to the contrary England was the richest country under the sun. In countries where preferential tariffs existed the workers were not as well paid or fed, and he thought they would fight—and fight successfully— against any proposals which would bring about such a state of things. Mr. BUCKLEY seconded.</p>
        <p>TherumnmAN observed that there was nothing par- ticularly" revolutionary" about Ithe report: (Laugh- ter.) They were told in the papers last week by a gentlemanwhom he had not the honour of knowing, a Mr. Osborne, that possibly the reason why the Trades Council did not grow as the Secretary would like it was because of the revolutionary debates. His (Mr. Pod- more's) experience of the Council was that they had had a very practical, common sense year, and he thought the Secretary's report was proof of that. (Hear. kear.) He objected to the Council being, in the slightest degree, misrepresented by anybody. (Hear, hear.) The motion was heartily carried. A BALANCE IN HAND.</p>
        <p>Mr. Malone, treasurer, had prepared the balance sheet which showed that the income for the year amounted to £Bl 10s., and after paying expenses there was a balance in hand of £2 6s. Id.</p>
        <p>The balance sheet was adopted and the Treasurer and Auditors (Messrs.Rathbone and Fargher) were cordially thanked.</p>
        <p>OFFICIALS ELECTED. Voting by ballot for the various officials resulted as</p>
        <p>follows :—President, Mr. J. Podmore ; vice-president, Mr. E. Plinston ; secretary, Mr. F. Stott (unopposed); treasurer, Mr. E. Malone (unopposed) ; delegate to Trades Council Federation, Mr. Stott; executive com- mittee, Messrs. 0. Buckley, E. Plinston, J. Kendrick, T. Plumb, and A. Jolley. On the motion of Mr. PLINSTON, seconded by Mr. EATON, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the reti.ing President (Mr. J. Edwardson) for his services. Mr. PODMORE returned thanks for his election, re- marking that he had been connected with the Council for nine years. He believed they were in for a hard year. There was no doubt the way was opening for labour to take a step forward and great responsibilities would devolve upon those who took the lead in the various societies. He hoped they in Warrington would take advantage of the opportunities afforded by the wave of labour enthusiasm which was passing over the country,and -Chet they would play their part in a manful and straightforward way. (Applause). The ordinary business was then proceeded with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>OPEN DOOR.</head>
        <p>THE OPEN DOOR.</p>
        <p>A doctor recently received a written request for his professional services, worded thus: "My wife's motber is at death's door. Please come at once, and see if you can pull her through." Now there are two sides to every door, as there are to every question, and it will be observed that the man with the sick mother-in-law neglected to state to which side of the door he wished the doctor to pull his suffer- ing relative. But we will be charitable, and suppose that he desired to save the patient, though he expressed himself so awkwardly. When Mrs. Cooper, of 17, York-road, The Hyde, Hen- don, was ill about five years ago there was no question as to the anxiety of her friends to save her. Her second child was only a few weeks old, and her life was very essential to her husband and family. It was just then that she became subject to fainting fits, and betrayed great nervousness. The root of the trouble was indi- gestion—the complaint which only Mother Seigel's Syrup will cure. "I was sometimes bent double with pain," said Mrs. Cooper on 24th March, 1903, "and was afraid to eat, knowing what the consequence would be. Besides, I had no appetite. Violent headaches,with floating specks before my eyes, assisted to make my life unendurable. Numbness of the hands and feet, and palpitation of the heart, were other bad signs. A doctor who attended me said mine was a case of severe indigestion and nervous debility. He appeared to understand my case, and sent me a great deal of medicine; but the only re- sult of hisattendance was—bills. Instead of improving, I grew worse. At night I could get no proper sleep, being disturbed by dreams and in constant dread, as of some impending evil. It was last Christmas, when I was very ill, that my mother suggested that Seigel's Syrup might benefit me, having proved it to be a splendid thing in other cases. I accordingly tried it, and was greatly astonished at the result. Before I had quite taken one bottle, I felt better. The pain left me, and I improved rapidly. It gave me refreshing sleep, followed by an appetite for breakfast. Soon the headaches and dizziness ceased to trouble me, while my strength increased daily. It is four months since I began to take Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and now (thanks to it) I am as well as i ever I was nmy life." Of late there has been much contention about the policy of the "open door"; but there can never be con- tention as to the value of what cured Mrs. Cooper. That was proved 35 years ago, and has been proved every day since.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Business of the next Meeting.</p>
        <p>'A meeting of the Education Committee will be held in the Town Hall, Warrington, at 6 o'clock on Monday eveningnext when the following among other business will be transacted :</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE'S RECOMMENDATIONS. The Finance Committee hasiresolved that Mrs. Sarah Blenkinsop be appointed keeper of the Ladies' School of Industry, at a salary at the rate of £52 per annum, the engagement dating from the 12th October, 1903. A letter from the managers of St. Peter's school, in- timating that they are unable to agree to the terms laid down by the committee in their scheme for the cleaning of schools, itc., and forwarding an account for payments made to the school keeper since the Ist April last, was submitted, and the Director was re- quested to intimate, in reply, that the committee were not prepared to take any action in the matter until the managers had accepted their scheme. The Director having asked for instructions as to the supply of fuel and cleaning materials to those schools whose managers had not yet accepted the committee's scheme, it was decided that such materials be not supplied to schools until the managers thereof have complied with the re- quirements of the scheme. Resolved, that an advertisement Se issued inviting tenders for the supply of coal (Yard and Pemberton), firewood, and sawdust to the Committee's schools, to be delivered into the schools' stores. _ .</p>
        <p>It was decided that a supply of Coke to the schools be obtained from the Corporation Gas department. Resolved, that the tenders of Messrs. G. Artingstall and Co. Ltd , and Mr. H. N. Houghton, for the supply of cleaning materials to the various schools be accepted. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE. NEW TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>The above Committee recommends that permission be given for the following appointments :—Hamilton street, N, two certificated assistant mistresses (in place of Miss Chatterton and Miss Burton, resigned). St. Mary's (R. C.), Latchford, two certificated ass:stant mistresses (one in place of Miss Turner, resigned). St Peter's (John Street) Churck, one certificated assistant mistress. Warrington Parochial, two certificated ass:s- taut mistresses.</p>
        <p>That consent be given to the appointment of the following teachers, viz :—St. Benedict's (R.C.) Mr. George Thorpe, £BO. Warrington Parochial, Mrs. Anna M. Bell, £BO. Wycliffe British Miss Ella Broadbent,2Bo That the following appointments of assistant teachers for the evening schools be made, at a salary, in each case, at the rate of ss. per evening, together with a bonus of Sc. 6d. for every student in their classes making 75 per ceht, of the possible number of attend- ances, viz. :—Fairtleld N, Miss E. M. Hudson, Miss J. Harding, Miss C. Taylor, Miss M. E. Taylor; Hamilton Street, N, Mr. R. Manifold, Mr. J. Armstrong, Mr. L. A. Take; Heathside N, Mr. G. Newburn, Mr. J. Strettle, Mr. T. Edden, Mr. A. Malcolm, Mr. F. J. Emson ; Warrington British, Miss N. Bayley, Mr. W. H. Cragg, Mr. W. Dagnall, Mr. J. Pinnlngton, Mr. A. Knowles, Miss E. Pickavance ; Warrington Parochial, Mr. H Cook, Mr. G. Lewis; Wycliffe British, Miss S. Jones, Miss A. Harrison.</p>
        <p>Resolved, that in the case of those who are Art. 50 and Art. 68 teachers, the appointments be made on the understanding that they will not be continued after the present session, unless their qualifications are improved in such a way as to meet with the approval of the committee.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Board of Education, consenting to the proposal that the educational year of all the schools in the Borough should, in future, terminate on the 31st July, was read and noted. It was reported that Mr. William Scatson had been appointed as a foundation manager of the Warrington Parochial school in place of Mr. W. Dooley, resigned. A letter from the Managers of St. Mary's (R.C.) school, asking for permission for violin class to be held by Head Master immediately after school hours, was submitted, and it was resolved that the Finance Com- mittee be requested, in making arrangements with the Managers as to the charges for the use of schools at times other than those during which they are used as public elementary schools, to grant the free use of the rooms to the head teachers for purposes of an educa- tional character and connected with school work. A letter from the Director of Education for Lanca- shire, forwarding a copy of resolutions passed by the Lancashire Education Committee with reeard to the attendance of pupil teachers from the Administrative Countyarea at Pupil Teachers' Contres in the County Boroughs, was submitted and noted. Resolved, that, with a view to the adoption of a scale of salaries for head teachers, the Director be requested to prepare andsubmit to the Committee a report as to the organisation of the whole of the elementary schools in the borough. A report that the existing cookery rooms at Hamilton street and Fairfield schools are now used for ordinary, school purposes, and would only be available for instruc- tion in cookery if a number of children were removed from the schools, was submitted, and it was resolved, that with a view to the provision of accommodation for instruction in cookery enquiries be made as to the suitability of some premises in the neighbourhood of Orford-lane.</p>
        <p>Resolved, that the society for University Extension in Liverpool and district be asked to appoint the Rev. H. A. Lester to give a course of lectures on the theory of teaching and school management to acting teachers at a fee of seventy-two guineas, together with a charge of two guineas per hundred in respect of the correction of exercises.</p>
        <p>Resolved, that the salary of Miss Annie E. Edwards certificated assistant mistress in the Infants' department of the Latchford N. school, be increased, in accordance with the scale of salaries, from £75 to £BO per annum as from the 15th June. 1903.</p>
        <p>Resolved that the Secondary school be opened every morning by the recital of the Lord's Prayer and the reading of a portion of the Bible by the teachers. Con- sideration of the desirability of singing a hymn as part of the opening of the school was deferred for further consideration.- A communication from the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope and Temperance Union, applying for per- mission for one of the lecturers of the Union to give, without charge, scientific object lessons on alcohol to the scholars in the upper standards of the elementary schools, having been submitted, it was resolved that permission be given for the lectures to be delivered in the schools as hitherto.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The question of the need for additional school accom- modation in the borough, having been discussed, it was</p>
        <p>n view of the terms of section 8 of the Education Act, i 1902, resolved that it be a recommendation to the I Education Committee that the Board of Education be asked whether the provision of temporary school accommodation is to be regarded as equivalent to the provision of new public elementary schools for the pur- poses of that section, and whether it would be neces sary for the three months' 'notice required therein o , the proposal to provide such temporary school accom- modation, to be given. Instructions were also given for a return to be pre- pared and submitted to the Committee of the number of ch;ldren residing outside the borough attendingschools within the borough, and of the children residing within the borough attending schools outside thereof; and for enquiries to be made as to any suitable premises available/for the purpose of providing the required tsmpo-ary school accommodation. The School Attendance Committee recommends that, in view of the increased school accommodation required in the Latchford district, the, Managers of the Latch- ford National. school be asked to consent to the conver- sion of the mixed department into a Girls' school. That, provided the Managers of the Latchford National school consent to such conversion of the Mixed depart- ment into a Girls' schoo!, it be a recommendation to the Educationrommittee to provide a school to accommodate 500 children (300 infants and 200 boys) ; and that it be a recommendation to the Sites and Buildings Committee that the plans for the proposed new school should be prepared so that, if necessary, an extension could be made of the Boys' department to increase the accommo- dation to :300. _</p>
        <p>Resolved also that it be a recommendation to the Education Committee to provide a new school to accom- modate 300 infants in the immediate neighbourhood of the existing St. George's Misfion school in Brick-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH. '</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH. '</p>
        <p>THE MULBERRY TREF, INN.—At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday the licence of the Mulberry Tree Inn, Stockton Heath, was transferred from Priscilla Anne Newall to Mr, Joseph Shillcock.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE AND STOCiTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.—Thera was a crowded attendance at the first public meeting of the session which was held in Hill Cliffe schoolroom on Monday evening. The Rev. J. S. Hughes presided, and was supported on the plat- form by Mr. W. H. Sherburn' Mr. A. H. Dunn, and 'Mr. G. Whitfield (secretary). Agood programme had been provided, and was thoroughly enjoyed. The members of the Ellesmere-street Mission Band of Hope contribu- ted a bell drill and an action song which were well given. Miss Davenport recited Me and Jim," for which she was heartily applauded,and Miss Elsie Spruce, of Warrington, a promising young vocalist sang very sweetly "Killarney." Master Stanley Jones sang "We want 3 ten thousand boys," the children joining in the chorus. Recitations were contributed by Mary Earle,Lily Powell, Edith Cole, May Isherwood, and Lillian Hall, and Master J. Whitfield and G. A. Whitfield. Mr. W. Gould contributed a Lancashire recitation, and Mr. J, Worrall provoked roars of laughter by his stump speech "Dark Clouds." Mr. Sherburn delivered a short address and spoke hopefully of the Band of Hope work and also appealed to the parents to encourage the children to attend the meetings, and become useful members. A vote of thanks was accorded to the friends who had contributed to the programme, and Mr. Hamblett res- ponded on behalf of the Ellesmere-street friends. Mr. Spruce, Master Whitfield, and Miss Taylor acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EFFECTS OF COLDS AND CHILLS.</head>
        <p>EFFECTS OF COLDS AND CHILLS.</p>
        <p>Bile Beans a Certain Remedy.</p>
        <p>At this period-of the year thousands of people suffer from colds and chills. Once the system is run down, colds and chills creep in, and frequently make the ail- ment more serious and more lasting. A case in point is that of Mr. Wm. Mosley,27, Montgomery Terrace-road, Sheffield. From his youth upwards, until 1898, Mr. Mosley had never known what a serious illness was. In that year,however,he got a severe coldand neglected it. The result was that he had a severe attack of in- fluenza. This left him weak, with little or no appetite, and utterly deprived of all that vigour which goes for so much in one's every day work. "These will wear off " said the doctor, but unhappily they did just the re- verse. His appetite went away .altogether, a feeling of depression possessed him, a desire to shirk every duty and just to lie doing nothing, caring for nothing, and with littlelconcern whether or not'anybody cared for him. A feeling of giddiness made it difficult for him to keep a constant knowledge of his bearings. He was so weak that he could net walk unsupported. "If I had not got my stick with me," he says, "I had to cling to railings or anything to hold me up. Some people who saw me staggering along, actually thought I had been indulging too fre3ly, and spoke to me about it afterwards. Pills, blood mixtures, doctors' prescrip- tions, and lhome-made recipes I had tried by the dozen almost, and they had all failed. So you cannot be surprised that I had completely lost all hope of ever recovering." His wife however, was more hopeful. She had heard of the wonderful cure which Charles Forde's bile beans hadleffected in a similar case. On her recommendation bile beans were tried. As a result, to adopt Mr. Mosley's own words, "my health and strength soon began to return, and gradually my appetite came back. I ceased to have the sickly weakly sensations. and to cut a long story short, having taken the beans for a comparatively short period, I felt as well, strong, and fit for work as ever in my life. I can tell you they have done so much for me that never again shall I be without a box in the house."</p>
        <p>Charles Forde's bile beans cure biliousness, piles, indigestion, liver complaints, anwmia, female ailment., and the host of ailments having a common origin in impurity of blood, general congestion of the system, and loss of vital force. Of all chemists at Is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WARRINGTON PLASTERERS' CLAIM</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PLASTERERS' CLAIM</p>
        <p>Interesting Case.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, yesterday, before Mr. H. Roberts and other magistrates, J. B. Sladin, 21, Pierpoint-street, and Isaac Wadsworth, 43, Price-street, brought a claim against J. B. Johnson and Company, 108, Crown-street, Liverpool, to recover 16s. 10d. wages and expenses alleged to be due to them. The wages were reckoned at the rate of WA. per hour, and the expenses at 6d. per day.—Mr. Steel appeared for the plaintiffs. and Mr. Willett represented the defendants Mr. Steel stated that defendants , were plas- terets in the employ of the firm. On Thursday, Sep- tember 17th, they finished some work at Earlestown, and Mr. Hanratty, defendants' representative, told plaintiff and another man named Brooks that their services would not be required any longer. He afterwards changed his mind in regard to the plant- tiffs and asked them to do certain work at Wellfield-st., Warrington. This was done, and Mr. Hanratty told the plaintiffs to attend to the getting up of the tools, scaffolding, &amp;c. to certain work at Hill Cliffe. On the following Monday this was done by the plaintiffs and a labourer and they were told to be on the job on Tues- day morning. At six o'clock they went up there and when Mr. Hanratty arrived he told them that a motor waggon was bringing on the scaffolding, &amp;c. and he ex- pected it to arrive at any moment. This was evi- dently a dodge on the part of Mr. Hanratty to get rid of the men, because he said !the motor had broken down but he expected it would now be repaired. He told the men to go one way to meet the waggon and he would go the other. Their worships would understand the absurdity of it when he (Mr. Steel) told them that Mr. Hanratty sent the plaintiffs down the very steep incline which led past Hill Cliffe graveyard and over Red Lane bridge. No one would expect a motor waggon to bring a load of scaffolding up that roadway. In accordance with Mr. Hanrattyrs instructions they went to the job on Wednesday morn- ing and were told that the waggon had not arrived. Mr. Hanratty said it might come at any moment and asked them not to go away but to start work as soon as the scaffolding came. Plaintiffs waited there all Tuesday and Wednesday on the explicit instructions of Mr. Hanratty. They were not able to start work on Wednesday and they had to go away and look for other work. They sent in a time sheet, claiming wages at the rate of 90. an hour for 20 hours, and expenses at 6d per day. The claim was made under the rules approved by the workmen and masters for the Warrington district.—Plaintiffs gave evidence and denied that Mr. Hanratty told them that no expenses or walking time would be allowed when they went to the new job. The defence was that the plaintiffs were told on Friday that the work was finished. A big job was com- ing onat Hill Cliffe but the firm did not know when it would be ready. The men had no other work to go to and Mr. Hanratty told. them that the company had written to him informing him that in the meantime no expenses or walking time would be paid. No employ- ment existed from the Friday until the job was ready for the men to commence work.</p>
        <p>The foreman Hanratty was called and denied that he saw the men en the Tuesday. The magistrates decided that plaintiffs had made out their case and awarded the amount claimed with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MR. SHACIMETON M.P., NOT A DELEGATE.</head>
        <p>MR. SHACIMETON M.P., NOT A DELEGATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. J. Shackleton, member for Clitheroe, addressed a meeting at Barrowfcrd, near Nelson. He devoted the major portion of his remarks to replying to strong Catholic criticism in relation to his attitude on the Education Bill, and denied that he had allied himself with either of the political parties in Parliament. He contended that on all questions, apart from labour he had a right to use his own private judgment, and if he had to vote as per resolutions received from different bodies in the divison his position would be reduced to that of a mere delegate, and representation reduced to a farce.</p>
        <p>National</p>
        <p>Physical</p>
        <p>Decadence</p>
