        <p>find these the best. American</p>
        <p>Oats are much inferior.</p>
        <p>R. ROBINSON &amp; SONS,</p>
        <p>ANNAN. N. B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TRINITARIAN BIBLE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>TRINITARIAN BIBLE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Interesting Lecture at Academy-street</p>
        <p>Chapel.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with this Society was /lel! in Academy-street Chapel on Thursday night. JO: some time now since a public meeting was held c;,.; behalf of the Warrington Auxiliary which has been existence for many years, the late Sir Gilbert Greeald having been the president. A large audience asserabl to hear Dr. Bullinger, of London, give an address oath; history, and work of the Society.—Mr. Henry Eyes, ° Appleton, presided. Dr. BULLINGER first explained how it was there calv! to be more than one Bible Society, and why this ono was called "Trinitarian." He shewed that it vre° because in the early days of the British and Foreigu, Bible Society they invited and obtained the help Socinians and Arians, and gave the ministers the right •ttee- to "attend and vote" at all meetings of the comml-- InlB3l (27 years after its formation) "some of its be% and noblest workers" having a strong objection to tilt alliance, moved a resolution at its annual meeting May 24th in Exeter Hall, to the effect "that the Nve,o"- ' Christians ' in the Ninth Law should not be taken include those who denied the Divinity and the Atoll' meat of Christ." This resolution was rejected, e'lled in consequence of this another society was for.T.A. on December 7th of that year on "Scripttu'' principles "and they called it "Trinitarian" os a pr_cr, test and a witness against allowing Socinians to havZ governing membership on a Bible Society. But tbo. was not the only evil. Unfortunately there Vf: another "Bible" which taught "another gospel." °lO ..ri. many centuries the Church of Rome kept the from the people. When the, Reformers began to ran translation of it, Rome met them by setting up inquisition and lighting the fires of the stake, and Wiled these means failed to stamp out the Bible,Rome tureS translator, and made her own version, not from To,: Hebrew and Greek originals, but from the Papa/ Lott: Vulgate which had then been recently "authorized "„Pf, the Council of Trent. Their Bible were called "antidote "to the " poison " of the faithful vex:: ; made by our Protestant Reformers in variouslangAgi;acesh and they were full of corruptions which made Go eat) idolatry (by tampering with the second Commae"iher and Penance and Mariolatry and all the, -Asti errors of the Church of Rome. When the was started it was soon asked to adopt and Vulgate versions' but their earl 3 committees, to founders of the Society refused again and arliwaa have anything to do with them and declared it 11 ground "from which they were excluded." But few years this was all changed, and in the early twentl.d these very coersions were adopted and were circtiler". to the present day, being advertised in their forei;bv; I catalogues as "for Catholics." There was ample reaSof I therefore for the formation and continued existelle,euto the Trinitarian Bible Society. Dr. Millings, " summarized,</p>
        <p>THE PRINCIPLES OF THE SOCIETY. I.—This Society is distinctively Protestant circti- 'proter. lating only those versions which are made by •ts tants ; eschewing all other versions made by Roman.lic which are corrupted in order to teach Roman Caul° - error. and 2.—lt expressly excludes all who deny the Deity 4.. At Ch</p>
        <p>itonement o ffist from any share in its govel. 4.0 and refuses to allow any such to "attend and vel" or take any part in its proceedings. i 5 3.-41 t believes that the work of a Bible Soclet3le NOT "to circulate the best translations that poi) will take," but the best that can be obtained. 4.—it adopts and acts on the principle of Free tribution, not being misled by the fallacy that " Pe,°l3 value what they pay for," • but led by the fact people pay pay for what they value. al Dr. BIILLINGER spokeat length as to one branch of the Society:—viz. its work in Britany ; givingaT i is tails of that n preference to giving an outlined 'a worldwide operations. Brittany was, he said, nearest foreign missionary field, and but for 110 Trinitarian Bible Society would never have had a /0" to this day. Tv. Much interest . was manifested throughout Bullinger's address, which was listened to with gr' attention. is gr' The Secretary of the Warrington Auxiliary .0 Edward Twiss, of 18 Grove-street, and the Treasureftei Mr. Albert Wright, 4 Enville-street. They will happy to give information and receive contribution.g.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <p>"FUEL AND ?ITS ECONNOMIC 4 APPLICATION." Paper by Mr. Ruddock, 111 r. F. G. Ruddock, F. S. C. the new president of the Warrington Literary and Philosophical Society, lectured at the first meeting of the session on Monday evening, his subject being "Fuel and its economic aPPlication." The lecture was illustrated by lantern elides, shown on the screen by Mr. Herbert Parkinson.—Mr. Ruddock pointed out that in the large majority of works only three per cent. of the Power the fuel contains was being utilized. Our rannufacturers were not in line with the German and the American, and the question forced itself "What is the reason? Were we going to try to throw the re- sponsibility of a stagnant trade on to the less educated Portion of the community? Or was it because we Were unable to take up the somewhat complicated processes of chemical engineers ? " Scientific principles underlay the whole of their manu- factures. There was no single factory, however Inechanical the work in it might appear, which had no need of science and above all chemical science. It Was applied chemistry alone that could point the way. Recent developments in fuel economy had all been the work of chemists, but our? manufacturers had not appreciated, save in rare instances, their labour. Applied science was exacting, and marched with un- erring footsteps. In the first half of the last century they obtained a great start over other countries, and the impetus of that start was still felt, but with ever lessening effect; under the stress of recent years the Pendulum was swinging slower and slower. It was -visible to all eyes to see. Voice after voice was raised with the warning cry, and now with the great inheritance we had received were we going to confess ourselves beaten, and that ,ether countries are too strong? Were we to 130 driven to the wall? A thousand times no. tut one path only was before us. We had no choice ZnowleclT3 must be met with knowledge, skill with Science was the common property of all coun- tries, only let us seize her outstretched hands, and our industries under her sure guidance would once more flourish as before, and show the world that science, wedded to native grit, would do more than recover the ground lost, and would extend our triumphs in the znidst of the fiercest competition.—An interesting dis- cussion followed, Alderman Bennett remarking that he Should like to have seen the Sanitary Committee of Which he was a humble member, present. If there was.a class of individuals who ought to listen to a, cluestion of this kind it was the class who systema- tically neglected their duties in regard to the impor- tant question of the consumption of their own smoke Re should also like to have seen the stokers here. Re believed they were largely responsible, owing to their want of knowledge, for the dense clouds of black 813104 which were emitted, and which with more care and knowledge might he prevented to a great extent by their assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>WORKHOUSE "HANDY MAN."</head>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE "HANDY MAN."</p>
        <p>Trades Council and the Appointment.</p>
        <p>The much-discussed appointment of a " handy man" at the Workhouse was again alluded to at the meeting Of the Trades and Labour Council held in Bold-street Wesleyan Schools on Tuesday evening. The Secretary (Mr. P. Stott) said he addressed the following letter to Mr. J. C. Sutton, Clerk to the Board of Guardians : "I am instructe4by special resolution of our Council to write you protesting against the appointment. of a handy man,' which appointment was made by the Board of Guardians at their last meeting. We emphati- cally protest against any single individual being ap- pointed to undertake the .dnties of painter, plumber, glazier, bath attendant, Sic., and, as one of the members of the Board expressed. he would have to do all sorts of odd jobs, and be responsible for the general upkeep of the whole establishment. We strongly object to this method of saving money, which can only be done at the expense of Warrington and other artizans. If the Board is so very anxious to save the ratepayers' money, we would suggest that ways and means should be found other than providing 'cheap labour' at the expense of skilled workmen, which in our opinion is a glaring example of knob-sticking.' We trust the Board will reconsider their decision, and make Provision for skilled workmen being employed to do the work at not less than the standard rate of wages." He received a reply from Mr. Sutton stating that the letter was laid before the Visiting l Committee and he was instructed to acknowledge it. Hearing nothing,furtherhe (Mr Stott) again wrote to the clerk asking if his corn- Munication had been placed before the Guardians,and if so,what decision had been arrived at. Mr. Sutton replied Stating that he submitted the several letters to the Board, and it was ordered that the letters should lie on the table. Mr. YORKE said they would have to get a proper representative on the Board before they could do any- thing. Mr. FARGHER said it had been explained to him that it was necessary to employ a man to do odd jobs. If a window was broken, he had been asked, did he think the Guardians would send for a man from town to put a taw one in, when they knew that the "handy man " had not enough work to fill in his time? The CHAIRMA.N (Mr. Podmore) said there might be tome reason in that but it did not justify the Guardians 44 treating the secretary's letter with contempt. If the Board had any reasons to give they should have been. Prepared to have given them. He thought that for the Present they might let the matter drop, and count it as °4e of the sins ,of the present Board of Guardians. Mr. STOTT remarked' that it had been stated that the kan would do plumbing and painting and therefore e/Pense would be saved. The matter then dropped.</p>
      </div>
