      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NEW CONGREGATIONAL SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW CONGREGATIONAL SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>The election of the Rev. J. A. Mitchell to the post of secretary to the Congregational Union in succession to the Rev. W. Wood, possesses interest to Manchester and to South Lancashire by reason of the rev, gentleman's former association with the city and district. An Edinburgh man by birth, Mr. Mitchell came south to Halifax. In the seventies he spent five year, at New College, and having graduated at London University he cameto Manchester in 1877 as assistant minister to the late Dr. Finlayson at Rusholme, whose initiation into ministerial guidance he has said was of inestimable value. Fifteen years were next spent in Nottingham, and then Mr. Mitchell came back to Manchester to act for four years as pastor at the church at St Helens over which the late Dr. Macfadyen at one time pre- sided. Then he returned to Nottingham as successor to Dr. Paton in the Principalship of the Institute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday October 17, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 17, 1903.</p>
        <p>are not receiving treatment so favourable as their fellows, and among all whose trade with foreign nations ! is injured. I Really there are no other methods yet suggested of closer union that require notice. An Imperial Court of Appaal is spoken of. We have that already in the ! Privy Council, which contains all the highest and ablest Judges in the land, as well as many of the best Colonial Judges. It is true that the Board is sometimes inade- quately manned. Let it be better manned. But, in truth, the notion that by dressing up judges in grand robes and seating them in a gorgeous court-house any- thing is done towa,rds consolidating the Empire is too far fetched to need criticism. So of cheap postage, rapid lines of steamers, and suchlikeTproposals—excel- lent in all ways, but of quite infinitesimal value in this , connect2on. In effect, no legislative contrivance lor constitutional change, great or small, and NO TINKERING WITH TARIFFS, can, as things are now, I).ring us near to our kinsmen across the seas. What may happen hereafter, when the colonial system we have now been trying for less than a couple of generations has been worked for a long space of,time;when difficulties have been smoothed away one by one as they arise, and when convenient customs have entwined themselves as in England round the hard pillars of the law—which advance may then be possible unman can foretell. But in the meantime the wiser friends both of Great Britain and the Colonies will per- ceive that the bonds of Empire are not 'material, ' resting upon a taxing Act or a paper Constitution however cunningly devised, but spiritual. Race, language, literature, history, common traditions, in an atmosphere of complete freedom, are infinitely power- ful factors for the keeping together of communities,no matter how many thousands of miles may separate them. If these things cannot do it nothing can. Every fine action by the Mother Country will strengthen the tie ; forbearance towards the weak, pity and succour to the oppressed,justice to everyone. No Colony will lightly forsake a parent nation whose honour and renown shine brightly before the world. If we fall short of this we shall be shorn of the esteem of our Colonies, and union will not long survive esteem. R. T. REID.</p>
        <p>[This article forms one of a series which we have arranged with leading members of the Eighty Club to contribute to our columns. Book copyright strictly reserved. The next article will be contributed by the Right Hon. James Bryce, M.P.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PROTECTIONIST CAMPAIGN</head>
        <p>THE PROTECTIONIST CAMPAIGN</p>
        <p>SURRENDER OF MR. BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>DESERTION OF THE DUKE.</p>
        <p>Death from Natural Causes.</p>
        <p>MR. JOSEPH CHAMBERLAIN, by his Protec- tionist campaign, is carrying destruction into the ranks of his supporters, without coming within measurable distance of attacking his opponents. A few months ago Mr. Chamber- lain was the virtual head of a powerful and united party. In May last he suddenly opened his Protectionist campaign, a campaign which was to demand all the attention of his opponents and frustrate their best efforts to overthrow the Government. How utterly different has been the history of the last few months from that which Mr. Chamberlain vainly predicted! Instead of belabouring his opponents, Mr. Chamberlain has bewildered and,. at last, estranged his friends. • The official Opposition, instead of being engaged in a life-and-death struggle, has mildly looked on while Mr. Chamberlain has been fighting a rearguard action with his revolted and most powerful allies. The Protectionist issue has wrought havoc in the eamp of the Unionists. Day by day the revolt against the Chamberlain proposals has grown in strength and volume, until the strongest half of the Cabinet has resigned; the most influential and able section of the party is leagued in opposition to its leaders. Protec- tion is undermining the constitution of the Unionist party, which, capable of resisting attacks from without, seems likely to perish from internal diseases. The evil of Chamber- lainism will prove more fatal with Unionism than with Liberalism. As a result of the present complication, the Unionist party seems likely to perish from natural causes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>The. Sheffield Speech.</head>
        <p>The. Sheffield Speech.</p>
        <p>So long as Mr. Balfour kept silence, had an open mind and no settled convictions, it was possible to keep his party together. But when he had to take sides' for or against Free Trade, the end was not far off. The result was never really in doubt. His weak and unsettled character was, from the first, domi- nated by the one determined man in the Cabinet. The open mind was but an excuse to gain time, a plea for time decently to abandon principles hitherto held. The measure of Mr. Balfour's fall must be taken from his previous utterances. In May last he had no unsettled convictions about British trade, and he preached a very wholesome sermon to those foolish people who think that because the trade of other nations is prosper- ing therefore ours must be declining. Said he: I am one of those who profoundly distrust the current creed . . . that the prosperity of one nation is the adversity of another, and who thinks he best serves the prosperity of his own nation who attempts to depress the industrial prosperity or snatch a share of the common work of industry from some other nation.</p>
        <p>There he gave us the pure gospel of Free Trade. But at Sheffield on October 2nd his view had changed. He then said that</p>
        <p>Foreign countries want to improve:their trade at our expense.</p>
        <p>He went on more explicitly to declare his entire opposition to Free Trade, saying:</p>
        <p>Do you desire to reverse the fiscal traditions, to alter fundamentally the fiscal tradition which has prevailed during the last two generations ? Yes, I do.</p>
        <p>With this complete renunciation of Free Trade ringing in our ears, let us ask ourselves, "WRY WAS IT MADE ? " Here is a statesman who for years has been a leader of one of the great parties in the State; who presumably has studied the fiscal question in all its aspects; who in May last vigorously defended Free Trade and withdrew a small tax on bread; but Who, in the following October, completely changes his opinion and policy! What reason is there for this? The trade of the country was never better. The success of Free Trade was never more conspicuous. The condition of the people though far" from what it ought to be, is better than it has ever been. Why, then, this sudden change? There is neither reason nor excuse from an economical point of view. The only excuse is that the Premier's feminine type of mind (all apologies to womanhood) has been captured and com- pletely subjugated by the unbending will of the late Colonial Secretary. If one needed evidence of this, it is enough to remember the half-pathetic, half-contemptible, attempt to appear, at Sheffield, to be leadinerather than being led. Then he said:</p>
        <p>A man who, however unworthy, is called upon to lead a party, must lead it, and so long as I am in that position I mean to_lead it.</p>
        <p>Those are the words of one who has sur- rendered, not of one who is master of his own destinies.</p>
        <p>A Fatal Blow.</p>
        <p>THE resignation of the Duke of Devonshire, following on those of Mr. Ritchie, Lord George Hamilton, Lord Balfour, and the Hon. Arthur Elliot, is the greatest blow the Chamberlain-Balfour scheme of Protection has received. The severity of this latest blow may be judged by the shrill and vindictive reply it produced from Mr. Balfour. But the chief significance of the Duke's resignation is the seal which it puts upon the compact between Mr. Balfour and Mr. Chamberlain, to work together towards the same end—com- plete Protection. The resignation of the Duke removes the last impediment to the complete subjection of the Primp Minister to Mr. Chamberlain. Unionists who have been sup- porting Mr. Balfour in the belief that he would moderate Mr. Chamberlain's plans now realise that, according to the Duke of Devonshire, all hope of saving Mr. Balfour is gone. His conversion from Free Trade to Retaliation has been rapid. His full accept- ance and advocacy of Chamberlainism will probably be still more so. Free Traders have now to fight Mr. Chamberlain.. They must force their opponents to declare for or against Protection pure and simple. There is no half-way house between Protection and Free Trade. It will be simple enough to show the hollowness of the Chamberlain scheme. We shall have pleasure in doing this next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>FEARNHEAD CLUB.—The general meeting of this club was held at the Farmers' Arms Hotel. on Thursday even- ing. The President, Mr.W. J. Shaw, who presided, was supported by the Vice-President, Mr. Henry Hoult, and there were above 30 other members present. The treasurer presented his balance sheet which disclosed a satisfactory balance. The prizes gained during the season were distributed. The President spoke of the admirable servicestrendered to the club by the secretary, Mr. T. Boyd, to whom much of the success of the club was due, and, on behalf of himself and fellow members, he asked him to accept a token of their appreciation in the shape of a handsome tea and coffee service. Mr. Boyd suitably responded. The principal officers were then elected for next season, viz. :—President, Mr. W. J. Shaw: vice-president, Mr. Henry Hoult ; treasurer, Mr. Joseph Roberts; and secretary, Mr. T. Boyd. The business portion of the proceedings having been completed, the remainder of the evening was spent in harmony, contributed to by Messrs. Joseph Roberts (piano), and E. Greenwood and g rda n pho Hughesne ) ,H. la eschHaosfwmhom contributed songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
