 be only one Non-trading candidate for Orford as rington on the whole, and not at the benefit of a fewWar- for the other Seats and that we may be able to return at that better arrangements might be made with the Ship any rata Canal Companythan are suggested at the present. The Mersey in my opinion can be navigated by suitable barges as well as ever. As I understand the new scheme, about £lOO,OOO, probably a great deal more (as litigation would sure to be very heavy through certain author- ities opposing it with might and main), would be spent in making a new river diversion with lock and weir,and a wall constructed across the present river opposite Monks, Hall and Co.'s works, thus holding up the water to a certain height. This in experts' opinion; may have detrimental•effect on the channel below Warring- ton, as the natural flow of the water would thus be impeded, and nature would require the penalty to be paid. Now, from experience, I find it possible to have from five to six feet of water at all times in the present dredged channel with not very much difficulty, but the additional two to three feet is a very difficult and castly business. Barges are working in 'other places which can get about with good cargoes on 6 feet draught, but eves larger boats drawing, say 10 feet could then work 'with ease and safety on the spring tides, and, as only for about four days out of 14, does the tide put up less than two feet, that means only 4 days when there would be less than 8 feet of-water. Now, in lieu of a concession of reduction in "depth to say 5 feet, cannot Warringtoik negotiate with great advantage now for a new Dock to be made, taking in the old river at Wilderspool towards • which £lOO,OOO would go a long way. Here we have at Sour very door, with even an entrance made, one of the afinest waterways in the world, in fact the sea flows right ,up to our feet. lam fully persuaded that Warrington, with its splendid position, situated as it is, very near to --the coal fields, embracing within itself an ever expand- ing commerce, surrounded by populous and growing dis- -tricts, would :grow in importance and prosperity by leaps,and bounds. As there is ample room to make such. -at dock on the present Arpley fields without any feces- nary purchase of property, it is a question whether it would not be to the advantage of the old town to herself provide' the dock, appropriating to her- .aelf the revenue from dock and town dues &amp;c., making the most favourable arrangements possible -fir to tolls to be charged on the camel. As a smallirate- pgyer, I know rates are fairly high, but you cannot launoh into great enterprises without spaying the cost. and seeing the flip it would give to labour in town duriag and after construction, bringing in moreover a large amount of new enterprise, increasing the value of property, along- with all the advantages of a full-fledged port I recommend the consideration of this to our local parliament mid townspeople. Many years ago, old Warringtonians Will remember, Warrington built and launched the Australian clippers" Tayleur" and' Lady Palmera" besides other fine ships, which were among the best of their day. Why should not 'Warrington again take their place along with Barrow, Birkenhead, and ()thee towns in this important industry, font which Abe is :peculiarly well situated? If wehad to construct a canal, or like Preston, Barrow, and other towns, spend :Enlarge fortune in deepening and maintaining a water way, it would be another matter, but passing our doors sire the ships of all nations, traversing the finest inland water-way in the world, and I am fully sure it will be the making of our town of many industries if these name ships are enabled to convey merchandise to and from the port of Warrington-.—Yours &amp;c, JOS. MONKS, Junr. . t-</p>
        <p>TWO L ARO OR REPRESENTA T IV 11S.</p>
        <p>among the'ten to be elected as-it-r-e's'at. of the coming vacancies. (Applause.) If we adhere to our original programme I have no fear. Last November we had against us the nominal leaders of the two political parties, we 'had against us our Member of 'Parliament and the prospective Liberal candidate as I know from correspondence I had with them. The Conservative and Liberal leaders were all opposed to us, and particu- larly desired that I at any ratenhould be defeated, but as you know, we got along pretty well despite them, aud their influence, and it will ever be to me a precious memory that the rank and file of the Conservative partyand of the Liberal party let perish their party dis- tinctionc in their determination last November to unite in striking a crushing blow at Tammany in War- rington. '(Applause.) I have referred to</p>
        <p>THE INQUIRY. I regret that inn letter which I had no responsibility for, of which 'indeed I knew nothing, there was a sen- tence which might be construed as suggesting t!Aat one or two of the Council Commissioners really doubted the seriousness of Mr. Pemberton's illness. Although I am assured, and believe, that nothing offensive was intended, I think the words which have caused offence were unfortunately chosen, and I wish they had not been ,used. As regards the dead-lock,r will leave Mr.Norbury .N•Villiams to deal with that. I only wish to say that the object of the Non-trading members of the original Joint Committee (of which I was one) was to bring out all the facts of the last seven years at the least possible expense to the ratepayers. We would have no lawyer as a Commissioner, we would pay no Commis- sioner, and the only expense we anticipated was that involved in the reporting of the proceed- ings. We had in mind the reporting of all the oral examination of witnesses, and this we thought could not cost more than a very nominal sum at the outside. When we found that the Council Commissioners were deter- mined that there should be reports of the preliminary investigations, that is, the talk of the Commissioners 'while the Corporation books were being perused, and that the cost must run into thousands of Pounds, we determined tiat we would not be parties to such an un- necessary waste as we believe of the ratepayers moneys. (Applause.) I think Mr. Norbury Williams will satisfy you that we should have been unworthy your confidence had we acquiesced in the decision of Messrs. Birchall, Greening andJolley. (Applause.) One word more as to the letter of these gentlemen to the Mayor ;—I refer to the reference to</p>
        <p>"LEGAL PROCEEDINGS." The use of these words can have but one object in my opinion, i.e.—to intimidate witnesses. We had asimilar experience in the Council Chamber when the inquiry resolutions were passed. The non-traders werepointedly threatened, indeed I was personally threatened by Coun- cillor F. R. Roberts. He said "he warned Councillor Doddsnot to vote for the passing of the resolutions as he(Mr. F. R.Roberts)was advised that his doing so would probably involve him in legal proceedings." Now, gentlemen, if there has been nothing wrong why all these threats on the part of Councillors and Commis- sioners—l leave you to say. (Applause.) I will now briefly refer to some of</p>
        <p>The •Elections.—Mr. Stott's Candidature Six,—lt was ,with ifeelings of sorrow and amazement that I listened as one of a vast audience in the Parr Hall on Thursday night to the name of Mr. E. Plinston, submittedas a probable:candidate for Orford Ward in opposition to Mr. Fred Stott. Mr. Stott is and has been for the last three months before the electors of that ward as the candidate in the labour interest. it may interest many of your readers to know that Mr. Plinston is Chairman oT the Labour Representation Committwt of which committee Mr. Stott is a member. Mr. Stott is, I believe, being nominated by the Independent Labour Party, of which he is a member and which pirty I believe is affili- ated with the Labour Representation Committee. A resolution endorsing the candidature of Mr:: Stott was passed by that coinntittee. Mr. Plinston "was present end gave his support to that resolution. Later on in his capacity of chairman at the labour demon- stration in the Parr Hall Mr. Pliuston made a strong appeal to the Trade. Unionists of Orford Ward to sup- port Mr. Stott on November Ist. Knowing this.amaze- =exit scarcely expresses my feeling when I heard at the Non-Trading meeting on rhursday night the name of Mr. Plinston submitted as a probable candidate for Orford Ward in opposition to Mr. Stott. Whatever is the meaning of it all? Has Mr. Plinston thrown over his colleagues of the Labour RepFesentatiou Committee? If so I should like to know his reasons for doina so Perhaps he is ambitious and would like to shine as a Town Councillor, and has attached himself to the Non- Trading party as the one most likely to help him to the realization of his ambition. Even then I cannot under- stand why he has allowed htniself to be nominated for Orford ward. His utterances in the Trades Council and from the platform at the Labour demonstration in the Parr Hall are not consistent with his position as candi- dato for Orford Ward, and I among hundreds of others interested in the Labour movement in Warrington will look forward with interest to an explanation of his coxtduDto—YDurs &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>ONE INTERESTED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>WORK WE HAVE DONE</head>
        <p>THE WORK WE HAVE DONE</p>
        <p>during the current municipal year.—L—T have said we were few, but we never forgot that outside the Council Chamber we were backed by you, the great body of the people of Warrington, and we never forgot that the really small and insignificant section were the Mayor and Aldermen and a gradually dwindling number of the Councillors, who only represented themselves, that is the 25 or 30 men theycount up to; veryfine men in.their way, but Ihave observed they don't care about facing elections. Under all the circumstances, I consider our success bas been marvellous. Within a few days of our election we stopped the slip-shod financial mettgods in which we found payments were being authorized by:the Finance Committee, and orders on the bankers of the Corporation signed before the accounts had been passed for payment by the Committees responsible for the outlay; we killed ft harebrairt scheme for lavish additions to the Technical Schools involving a total expenditure of over .£ll,OOO, and out of which, according to Mr. Alderman Monks, about £2,000 would have been spent on a single staircase—(applause) ; we prepared at a considerable expenditure of time what, as we believed 4nd believe, was an unanswerable case against an ill- advised scheme of the Water Committee, representing an outlay at Daresbury of £70,000. (Applause.) Owing ato the 'refusal of the Borqugh Treasurer to endorse the Water Committee's figures (he found out what we had previously discovered) the scheme, although passed by the Water Committee and inserted in the Council agenda,. was hurrielly abandoned. We exposed the most irregular way in which £5O a year was being improperly paid to the Librarian; we upsetan attempt to add .£lOO a year to the salary ofthe Director of Education before he had got fairly into harness. I attach no blame either to Mr. Madeleyor Mr. Murray ran regard to these unpleasant matters, and I would again point out that my real com- plaint in respect of the last-named business was that we advertised for and secured a £3ooa year man, whereas had we meant to immediately pay 1:100 a year we should have so advertised and got the best man possible for the larger amount of salary. Let me say that lam sure we have secured in Mr. Moore Murray an admirable officer and one likely to prove himself well worth whatever financial encouragement we can afford him. Then we did our utmost to prevent the engage-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday October 17 1903</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday October 17 1903</p>
        <p>Men of too many Seccmdary school teachers until some approximate idea could be formed as to the number of students who would require to be taught. I put it to Mr. Jolley in the absence of Alderman Monks at a monthly meeting of the Education Committee whether it would not be possible for the proposed head teacher to teach chemistryand physics until it was seen whether , the special teachers were needed. Mr. Jolley promised that my suggestion should be kept in mind; it may have been, but all I k low is that these two special teachers and all the teachers advertised for were appoint&amp;l, although to-day there are only being taught 253 students all told, as against the 1,300 odd that Alderman Monks led the Council, some months ago, to believe were awaiting accorihnoilation. But I let that pass, and I remind you of the good work done by Mr. Lord and Mr. Pemberton in regard to the Slack con- tracts, the Arpley-road matter, and the proposed arrangement with Mr. R. E. Garnett in anticipation of the making of</p>
        <p>sure that without. Mr. Norbury Williams they would not have had t one tithe the success they had had(Applause). MR. NqRBURY WILLTANIS AND THE INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>THE DIFFERENCES AMONGST THE COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>Mr.NOR.BURY WILLIAMS, who had a hearty reception, said that was the fourteenth great meeting that he had attended in Warri..gton. They never dreamed when m.eating number one was held that they were going to havelsuch a campaign as this had turned out to. be. Vir. Williams proceeded to refer to some of the classic fields in history, such as Harpers Ferry in the United States where the abolition of slavery was commenced; Waterloo where Napoleon's power was broken ; and Runnymede, where the Barons extracted from King John his signature to the great Magna Charta. In the world of municipal government, • and particularly in the branch of municipal reform he ven- tured to state that when history came to be written in reference to that subject, there was no town in this kingdom as to which it would be said that there was any better, braver, nobler and more living town than the town of Warrington. (Applause.) They had,had a great struggle here. Ithadibeen the cockpit of municipal reform for two years and the eyes of England were on Warrington. There were reforms going on in every Corporation. Warrington was not the only Cor- poration that had gone far from the straight line, but this was the town where there had been more activity than in any other tawn. This was the town that ' had made more advance in this brief space of time than any other borough iii the kingdom and therefore to Warrington as far as he was concerned, would be awarded the crown that was justly due to them. Having first seen her defects, and seen how far she had allowed her local government to go wrong, she had with great promptitude and exceed- ing swiftness set herself right in the eyes of the whole, country. (Applause.) ..He must commence his observa- tions that night with an expression of his great regret at the absence of his friend Mr. Pemberton. He deplored Mr. Pemberton's state of health. He had admired the way in which Mr. Pemberton had helped in this struggle from the very beginning. When days were dark and friends were few John Pemberton stood by the cause, and though he was not present in that second great campaign that was about to start, Mr. Pemberton was with them still in the indomitable spirit which no illness or sickness of any sort could take from him. (Applause.) They had been told that they were enemies of this town, enemies because they point- ed out the defects in its local government, enemies because they made, perhaps, some strong observations with reference to the public conduct of their public men. They were there to-night to say that they were not the enemies of this town, but they had been WARRINGTON'S BEST FRIENDS.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) They were going to remain to the end of this classic chapter, the friends of Warrington. (Ap- plause.) Everybody said, except a handful of nobodies in Warrington, that they had done grandly, and he would ask them not to listen to this handful of croakers. (Applause.) The people of Warrington had done well and were on the right track; and if they were going to help, as the Non-Traders trusted they would, in the second stage of this great campaign they would effect such a permanent and complete Change in their local government as would satisfy the most exacting burgess in Warrington. (Applause.) He noticed they had cleaned their Parr Hall and he hoped they would cleanse out the town in the samelway. Referring to the Chairman's observations respecting Mr. Parr, Mr. Williams said Mr. Parr had made some mistakes during his Mayoralty. Every man was liable to make some , mistakes and Mr. Parr was only a man after all. , A. VOICE: He is a gentleman. (Hear, hear.) Mr. WILLIA.MS : Yes. If a man is gentle, he is a gentleman, whether he runs a factory, or a bank or whether he serves in the fifth-grade of an ironworks. (Applause.) Do not let us have these hateful divisions, (Hear, hear.) The true gentleman is the man who knows what true valour is; kmdness to his neighbour, doing duty to his friends and even to his enemies. (Ap- plause4Continuing, Mr. Williams said when he saw Mr. Parr speaking of those present in the London" Times" as a small but demonstrative section of the community he wondered whether he had taken a clear view as to who they were and what their principles were. (Hear, hear.) The main portion of his address, continued Mr. Williams, was to deal with</p>
        <p>going to dictate this expense of public money ?” (Applause). In 1890 Mr. Jolley gotlinto the Council unopposed. That was in the halcyon days when what was known as the compact was in force—when there 'were no fights, and anybody could go in who had got the seal upon him of some particular political party. Under that wretched compact, which they had smashed into a thousand fragments, Mr. Jolley made his entrance into the Council—a triumphant entry. (Laughter.) Three years later he came out, but the compact had dis- appeared, the old crutches had been taken away from these ancient people. They Lad to depend upon them- selves, and their own capacities, powers and suitability as [public representatives. Try and think of Mr. Jolley across the table, said the speaker, dictating terms, and then think of these figures with the people of Warring- ton behind them :—Craik, 629 ; Bolton, 309; Jolley,lsB. (Laughter.) What a fall was there! And this was the gentleman who was going to make the earth to tremble, to.whom they were supposed to be going to bow and agree to this expenditure. In face of that fact they could not do so. He found further that Mr. Jolley was in his time</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Nle97 STREET</head>
        <p>THE Nle97 STREET</p>
        <p>from Sankey-street to Rylands-street, a street which,so near Bridge-street, is not required, and which if carried through in the unsatisfactory way now intended would involve the ratepayers in an appalling amount of un- necessary expenditure for the benefit of a single rate- payer. (Hear, hear.) This foolish scheme in one form was defeated some weeks ago by 17 to 11 votes, but at the last meeting of the Council a slightly different scheme was carried, but only by the Mayor voting twice, in my opinion a rather unfortunate action on his part in view of the fact that only 22 voted altogether and that a majority of those who supported the scheme were not directly elected members of the Council, but mostly Aldermen representing only themselves and the discredited political compact. Mr. Loyd has given notice for the recission of the motion carried in so very unsatisfactory a way, and you may rely upon it we shall do our utmost to kill this sus- picious proposal. (Applause). Imust not omit to men tion our efforts to secure fair treatment for the work- ing employbs of the Corporation. After considerable trouble we have, within the last few days had justice done to two tram guards, who for some time past had been 'unable to get their grievances considered, in accordance with the Standing Orders. We have also secured by the persistent effort of Mr. Pemberton, the recognition of theright of every member of any Com- mittee of the Council 'to have full access to every docu- ment and every tender which comes before such Com- mittee. So much, ladies and gentlemen, for what we have been able to accomplish witnin the last eleven months. Before I close (and I have detained you too long) I must mention</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>RIVER DIVERSION SCHEME.</head>
        <p>THE RIVER DIVERSION SCHEME.</p>
        <p>A new proposal under which we, while our own in- debtedness on loan is approximately 14 millions are to borrow .£BO,OOO and lend it to the Ship Canal Company (hear hear). The scheme might prove a verygood thing for the Ship Canal Company. I think it. would, and it might prove a good thing for the traders and manufac- turers who are interested, but I dont at present see where the advantages to the great body of the ordinary ratepayers come in. In view of its origin and the par- ties who are so zealously pushing it, I think we shall do well to jealously watch developments and be ready to end or mend the business as occasion may require. iApplause) In conclusion, T and my colleagues have endeavoured to faithfully discharge the trust you imposed uponus last November; we are prepared to go on as we have begun, and it will be for you to strengthen our hands and to encourage our hearts by sending us, a fortnight next Monday, thel reinforcements we sorely need. We have no selfish ends inview, we are doing your work at sacrifices we• are willing to make; we are sure we may rely upon you to stand by your own cause, and to take the trouble to vote for it. Not to vote is to act the part of the unfaithful servant who hid his talents in the earth; to vote corruptly is felony: If you vote for a man for no other reason than that he is a friend or a neighbour and it would not be pleasant to disoblige him, or because the rival candidate omitted to invite you to a dinner party or forgot to tell his Wife that your daughters were to be asked to a dance,— this is a clear violation of public duty. If you vote for a man because he subscribed to an institution in which you are interested or because he helped you to secure some office, if you refuse to vote for him because he gives you no orders for coal or meat—this is positive corruption. You will have to account for your vote at the judgment seat of God; I am sure you will so use it as to satisfy your own consciences, as to promote good citizenship, to secure equal justice for ever3!•inhabitant, and to establish and to protect purity and righteousnes in the administration of our public affairs. Mr. JAMES POVEY, the Non-trading candidate for Fairfield Ward, said he was very much surprised at being called upon to speak. From the first meeting of the Non-Traders he made up his mind that he was on their side. He was still on that side. (Hear, hear.) In the first place he congratulated the ratepayers on the changedcondition of things within the last two years. Prior to two years ago they had not the privilege of voting for the men they wished to represent them. That condition of things was now changed and they had the opportunity of voting for those who would reprpsent the interests of the ratepayers and not their own. (Ap- plause.) As they knew he (Mr. Povey) was one of the candidates at the forthcoming election and if they honoured him with their confidence he felt sure he would not betray the trust reposed in him. (Hear, hear.) He was in full sympathy with the objects of the Non-Trading Association, and,if elected, he would endeavour to promote and extend the I work which the association had commenced. They had a good captain and a good cause, and they would win. ,(Applause.) He had often been surprised and amused at the things Mr. Norbury Williams had found out and exposed in connection with the Town Council,but there was one little matter which had not been alluded to. About 1 three years ago he remembered reading of a discussion in connection with the Warrington Water scheme. He (Mr. Povey) had been connected with municipal matters elsewhere, and was a member of the St. Helens Town Council for some years before he came to live in War- rington. About 16 years ago the Town Council of St. Helens contracted with the Collins Green Colliery Company to pump their water and take it from them for 20 years and pay them £.1,000 a year for it. A VOICE: Keep to Warrington. Never mind St Helens.</p>
        <p>Mr. POVEY (proceeding) said that after a few years the water was found to be practically useless. A VOCE: "Keep on with Warrington." Mr. POVEY : I am coming to that. (Hear, hear.) Proceeding, he said the water obtained from this colliery was found to be useless and had to be discarded, and the St. Helens Corporation had still to pay for water that was now running down a ditch. About three years ago he remembered reading in the Warrington papers of "a suggestion that the Corporation should take water from a neighbouring colliery." The gentleman who suggested it, was interested in the same colliery company, and he (Mr. Povey) congratulated the ratepayers upon being fortunate enough in escaping any such arrangement. Mr. S. B. STRINGER, the candidate for Latchford ward, said he believed it had been a Providential inter- vention that Mr. Dodds:had been raised up for this particular purpose. He (Mr. Stringer) had almost lost hope for Warrington. Things had become so crooked and bad that he had lost almost all hope and all heart. He had had his eyes opened. There were two little kingdoms in the town and they were ruled by two little kings (laughter). They had such influence and power that they were taking the power from the people to elect their own representatives. When arranging these matters in their little ring they sank their political differences and ruled the entire roost. (Laughter.) He was thankful to Providence that they had smashed these kingdoms up. (Hear, hear.) In coming forward as a candidate he was aware of the fact that he had been knocked down on three previous occa- sions—(laughter)—but he did not feel as though all the breath had gone. Here he was again (Hear, hear and laughter.) He was in entire sympathy with the movement, and whether in the Council or not, he should feel it to be an imperative duty to support the movement until the political parties were entirely smashed and the best men sent te represent the rate- payers, irrespective of party politics. He was in favour of an independent man going to Parliament. (Applause.) If he had the election of magistrates he would take good care that no man went on the bench who was carried home by a policeman over night, and next morning fined his unfortunate brother ss. and costs for doing the same thing. (Laughter and applause). The CHAIRMAN announced that he had received a letter from Mr. W. E. Thornton, son of Councillor Thornton, who was unable to be present. Mr. W. E. Thornton was heart and soul with them, and they looked to him by and by to become a good member of the Town Councir. They had with them that evening, Mr. Ash- croft, their worthy representative—or would be shortly —for St. Austin's Ward ;—(applause)—Mr. Ashley, who was going to fight Mr. John Maginn in St John's Ward —(applause)—and Mr. William Benson who had threat- ened Mr. .John Evans in Hawley Ward. Mr. Evans would have been the next Alderman, but he dare not come to the scratch and face Mr. Benson. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>RESOLUTION OF CONFIDENCE.</head>
        <p>A RESOLUTION OF CONFIDENCE.</p>
        <p>I Mr. A. Hut.= moved: "That this meeting records I its entire approval of the work of the non-trading meni- hers of the Town Council during the past municipal year,and pledges itself to support approved non-trading candidates only at' the forthcoming election." Mr. Hulme pointed out that one result of the educational work carried on by the Non:Trading party was that some members of the Town Council now realised that in future they must act in the public interest an 4 not in their ONVII. (Hear, hear.) As a result of this they had decided voluntarily not to offer themselves for re-elec- tion next November. (Laughter.) One could not help but admire that oldsection of the Town Council which still remained, for possessing the quality of never realising when they were beaten. (Laughter.) They had evidently not had enough and he would advise the ratepayers to let them have it for all they were worth. (Laughter.) That was the only way to do it and it could be,done by voting solidly for the non-trading candidates at the 'forthcoming election. (Applattse.) Mr. S. KiNYON briefly seconded the resolution and urged his heareFs to support the non-trading candidates in the interests of the town and the welfare of mankind. The resolution was carried Unanimously. The CHAIRMAN acknowledged the passin,, of the resolution on behalf of the non-trading members of the Council. They intended, he said, to stand by the .elec tors if the electors would stand by them, and work for the reduction of expenditure as far as possible and watch the ratepayers' interests. He had now to ask thei old friend Mr. NorburyWilliams, who had not had a vote of thanks, to speak. Mr. Williams said he did not care to have a vote of thanks but he (the chairman)vras</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>INQUIRY,</head>
        <p>THE INQUIRY,</p>
        <p>labout which so much had been said. Theyknew as well as he did that for nearly two years they had been pressing in season, and out of season, for the holding of the inquiry. They knew how frequently and in what various ways the Town Council, out of harmony, he affirmed, with the great body of burgesses had from time to time defeated resolutions which had been pro- posed in favour of the holding of the inquiry. The Council had been their opponents in the matter, and it was only through constant and repeated pressure that they were able a short time ago to secure the consent of the Council to the opening of the inquiry This inquiry which would have been and he hoped might still be historical, was duly set on foot. After a number of sittings held between the three representatives of the Corporktion and three representatives of the Non. Trading Committee,a basis was arrived at under which the Commissioners were instructed to make an inquiry Mr. Williams claimed for the three non-trading com- missioners that they represented the people of Warring- ton (Applause). Continuing he said the report had gone through the Press not only in, Warrington but in towns far away,that therommissioners ha4iot been able to agree, and very recently, his three fellow oCminis- sioners who represented the Council had sent to the Council a communication as to which he wanted to say something. There was an understanding arrived at, that for various reasons the proceedings should not be public. Some of them were prepared for open proceed- ings, but on the other hand it was felt that there had been for years such a spirit of intimidation in Warring- ton that many men would be prevented from coming before the commission if the inquiry had been entirely open. For that reason alone they agreed that the Com- mission should sit in private. They agreed, however, that a shorthand report of the proceedings should be taken by such persons as the Commissioners might appoint. That appeared to be a very simple clause which nobody could have any trouble about but, strange to say, on those dozen or fifteen words the Commis- sioners had broken in half and for the time being had parted. He was one of the three who, at the beginning, drew up a code of rules for the guidance of the Com- missioners, so he thought he had some idea at any rate of what was in their minds. The two sections of the Commissioners were now unable to agree as to what was meant by "the proceedings." He had in his time been connected with a number of.in- quirks which had become well-known. The iligginbottom inquiry in'Manchester wasj as wellknown as tolits de- tails in Australia and America as it was iniLancashire.He knew something of how the proceedings were conducted and what was done in reference to shorthand notes. That night he had brought down the shorthand proceedings of an inquiry which was held in Manchester with reference to the affairs of the cleansing committee of the City Cou- ncil. The preparations for that inquiry were going on for months before the inquiry was held. Mr. Norbury Williams related how one witness came to his house at six o'clock one Saturday night to make a statement in the matter, and it took until six o'clock the following morning before all the information was given. That was only one of a great number of witnesses and it was all in the course of theipreparation for the inquiry. That was exactly what they had split on in Warrington. They said--and he thought with some force—that it would probably take</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>SEVERAL MONTHS OF PRELIMINARY INQUIRY</head>
        <p>SEVERAL MONTHS OF PRELIMINARY INQUIRY</p>
        <p>of searching of books and documents, before they would be able to enter upon the proceedings proper, call the witnesses, and have a shorthand writer present to take down what the witnesses said. (Applause.) Mr. Williams produced copies of the proceedings of the Manchester inquiry referred to. He pointed out that on the last day alone the shorthand notes occupied 172 pages. The inquiry took ten days, and during that period the shorthand notes extended over 1157 foolscap pages. The cost of the notes was £205 9s. 11d., the cost of type- writing £73 Iss. Id. making a total of ,E 279 ss. That was equal to about £2B a day. The Non-Trading Com- missioners did not think it was reasonable and right that they should agree that the searching of books, in- voices, &amp;c., which would occupy several months, should be fully reported at that tremendous cost. (Applause.) They protestedagainst it and should protest against it. The expense at Warrington would be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>MORE THAN £2B A. DAY</head>
        <p>MORE THAN £2B A. DAY</p>
        <p>ibecause the report was to be printed and twenty-five copies made. Not until the inquiry proper began, were they prepared to agree to that large expenditure. In nine months the cost would be about £3,600. He did ' not know how long the actual inquiry would go on, but such a figure as he had named would be something approaching to a scandal and he would not be a party to it as representing the men of Warrington. (Applause.) At the Higginbottom inquiry the preliminaries took six weeks—night and day—and the inquiry occupied two days. He considered that he had had experience in these matters and that his friends on the other side of the table had not had experience. The Non-Trading Commissioners considered that they represented the people and the other Commissioners repre- sented precisely nobody. (Laughter). Referring to ; the Council representatives, the speaker said that Mr. Birchen was an excellent friend ,of his. He made his acquaintances at the ;inquiry and hoped to keep him as a very good friend. Mr. Birchall told them candidly that he had been neutral since the beginning of the fight. He (Mr. I Williams) did not like theneutral folks. He was down on the neutrals. (Laughter,) "I would thou wert cold or hot; but because thou art hike-warm I will spew i thee out of my mouth." (Laughter.) When Mr. Birch- all said "I have been neutral" from the beginning and am neutral now; I only want •fair inquiry," he (the speaker) said "That is the pity of it—that all you magis- trates have been neutral. Neutral meant sitting on one side and waiting to see what was going to turn up. The I Soldiers who sat in their tents till the cry of victory came were no good at all. They *did not want toy soldiers of that kind, andhe did not want neutral J.P.'s in Warrington when a great matter of right and wrong • was before the people. (Applause). Speaking of Mr. A. J.Jolley, Mr. Williamssaid that when sitting at a table opposite the Council Commissioners dayafter day one could not help measuring them up in a public sense (Laughter.) One could not help asking what was the force of public opinion behind these three men. When they came and dictated practically and said ''This expense must be" it was only natural that the three men on the other side of the table should say " And who are you who are</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>MAYOR'S AUDITOR</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S AUDITOR</p>
        <p>for the borough. From a letter which Mr. Dodds had received that day, he found that for auditing the accounts for the year ending 31st March, 1901, £9O was paid by cheque. Three receipts were attached to that document—each for .00—signed by three gentlemen,l and one of them, dated June 28th, was A. J. Jolley— the first Mayor's auditor in Great Britain that he had I ever heard of receiving pay, (Applause). For the audit-1 ing of the accounts for the year ending 31st March, 1902, there was another cheque for £9O. As before, three receipts were given, and one, dated June 19th, 1902, was by A. J, Jolley. That was certified to-day by James Bolton elective auditor for the borough of Warrington. (Applause.) Mr. Jolley was a gentleman whom the electorate had rejected, and who, while he was in power.took pay for the office of Mayor's auditor —a thing to him (Mr. Williams) unheard of, and as to the legality of which, he wasnot atall convinced. Mr. L. Greening, the third Commissioner, was elected to the Council in 1899 for Orford ward and on retiring in 1902 did not come forward again. Why was it that these men did not come forward last year? Why was it they•were not coming forward;this year?—because there was nobody behind them. It was a serious matter to have to stop the inquiry. 14 was an inquiry which they had set their hearts upon and which was going to do infinite good to Warrington in the clearing of its good name. There- fore it was a serious responsibility to stop the proceed- ings even for a single month; but they thought they had done it with good cause. Another reason why they did it was this :—"ln view of the possibility of legal proceedings as the otitcome of the inquiry" said the Council Commissioners "it is imperative in. the interests of justice and for the protection of individuals that an independent report by a co.upetent shorthand writer should be taken of the proceedings of the Commis- sioners." He should have kept quiet on the subject, but for that wretched passage. He resented that passage. He would not have his colleagues telling him that legal Iproceedings were going to happen in reference to the inquiry. It was a threat that he I hated. (Applause.) Mr. Williams then alluded to the letter which he received from Councillor F. R. Roberts two years ago stating that what he (Mr. Williams) had said was an unmitigated lie, and if not withdrawn he might hear more about it. (Laughter.) He resented thesethreats. The agitation was not carried on for the purpose of attacking individuals, but was wholly and solely concerned with public actions. Continuing, the speaker expressed the hope that the inquiry would be resumed in the-near future. He raw no reason why it should not. If the Council were wise it would be re- sumed—whether with present Commissioners or with others it was not for him to say. He hoped that after November2nd when the Council was once more METAMORPROSISED ALMOST BEYOND RECOGNITION, some clear instructions would be givenas to the inquiry and they would be allowed to proceed with it on the commonsense lines he had indicated—which were that shorthand reports should be taken of all actual proceed- ings when witnesses gave evidence, and in the three or four months of preliminary gettings ready they would have no I shorthand report and no great expenditure at all. (Applause.) Mr. Norbury Williams referred to the last November election when he said, they made an im- pression such as had never before been made in Warring- ton. He asked his hearers to enter with enthusiasm into the forthcoming elections, and said he was given to understand that, out of the nine seats, in four or five cases no one was going to dare to stand up against their men (Applause). They were going to put all the steam and energy into the remaining wards and he asked all present so help them in the fight. He knew of in- conveniences, annoyances, insults,and boycottings which had been suffered by many people on their side for what they had done on behalf of the people of Warrington Some day he might have to give the names of those who had deserved well of Warrington. There were many soldiers who had been severely wounded in the fight, and there would be a great many more, perhaps, before the fight was over, but it would be one of the grandest memories that any of those men could have in life, that they had stood by the cause that needed assistance—that they had opposed the wrongs that needed resistance, and had striven according to their powers,and at great personal sacrifice, to serve the'inen and women of Warrington. CounCillor Dodds had told them something of what had been done by the mere handful of men who went into the Council last November—a list of lachievements which he ventured to say very few bands so small had ever achieved. They had had no personal ends to serve; they were not converting themselves into companies and getting orders from the Council. That was an easy thing to do in the old days, but it was jolly hard to do it now. (Laughter). They had been straight and true and they were waiting in their comparative solitude in the Council Chamber till another battalion of eight or ten was sent in to help them. Mr. Norbury Williams then proceeded to give the names of the candidates in the various wards. Coun- cillor Lord would again stand for Town Hall Ward, and no one had -vet ventured to oppose him. In St. John's Ward ouncillor F. R. Roberts was going to withdraw. He might threaten and bully and tell them Olaf he was going to send writs and inspire them with fear, but when the Ist of November came he stood down. (Laughter.) He was one of the wise men who had leaant wisdom by experience. (Laughter.) Inthat Ward Mr. Ashley was going to fight</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN MAGINN.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) He did not know what his friends would do in connection with that election but he would like them to put on the walls this passage from a speech delivered, by Mr. Maginn on July 28th: —"Did they mean to say that when a man was a Town Councillor he was to debar himself from the ordinary trade he was entitled to? He was quite entitled to an equal chance of the trade if his tender was lower than that of anyone else." That might very well have been said by Mr. Henry Roberts. A greater heresy than that was never preached and the sentences he had read were, in his judgment, enough to keep Mr. Maginn out of public life in Warrington for the remainder of his life. (Applause.) If they were going to give their suffrages ta Mr. Maginn after he had preached that doctrine they were going to spoil all they had done and put a blot on the .page of the present election. Referring to Mr. Magmn's big picnic—a monster "blow up "Mr. Williams termed it—the speaker said he did not suggest for an instant that Mr. Maginn promoted it with a view to any candidature, but he did say that now Mr. Maginn had done that and had ingratiated himself, by the outlay of £2BO, in the affections of many of the men women and children in the borough, he ought to stand down and not be a candidate. In St. Austin's Ward Mr. William Roberts, like his brother, was going with a long and sad farewell, and they were'putting up in the place rendered vacant by his retirement Mr. T. H. Ashcroft. They had a good friend coming before them for re-election in Whitecross Ward—Mr. Thomas Barton, Non-Trader. There had been no opposition to him so far. In Bewsey Ward their candidate would be Councillor William Pierpoint—(applause)—who had always been favourable to them since the agitation began—(applause)—and who would probably.be the next Mayor of Warrington. (Applause.) There was no opposition to him. In Howley Ward there would be another of those farewells. (Laughter.) As one of the poets said, "Parting is such sweet sorrow." (Laughter.) They were going to say good-byeto Mr. John Evans, and in his place he (the speaker) hoped 1 there would reign Mr. V Villinm Ben- son. (Applause.) In Fairfield Ward Councillor T. J. Hulse, was, he understood, going to fight again. The Non-Traders had selected Mr. Povey and he asked them to vote for him. (Applause.) In Latchford Ward the present member was Councillor Sampson—who was not a strong man. (Laughter.) He was one of the modern Sampson's who was devoid of strength. The Non- Traders were supporting Mr. Stringer—(applause)—and if the men of Latchferd were to prefer Sartipson to Stringer, Latchford would deserve to be wiped off the map. (Laughter.) In regard to</p>
        <p>_ _ ORFORD WARD, they would gather from the papers a week hence what the Non-Traders had decided. At present there was a little doubt. Councillor Peacock was the retiring re- presentative and he was going to stand again. They were going to bring a bird of another colour for that ward, but it was not absolutely settled whether it would be their friend Mr. Plinston or Mr. Stott. (Applause.) They wanted,to endeavour to secure a proper arrangement They did not want to lose any seats, and they were the friends of every party—Labour, Liberal or Conservative There was going to be no boycotting of any party by the Association. Even though all the Non-Trading candidates were returned they would possibly not have quite a majority in the Council. They hoped that next year—they were certain in fact— they would have such a number of representatives in the Council that they would transform the aldermanic bench. They wanted the ratepayers to strengthen their hands to such an extent that theyiwouldibe able to!practi cally dictate the policy of the Town Council. (Applause.) There was to be no more domination of political parties in municipal affairs. Mr. Williams again referred to the state of affairs in thel Corporation when the Non- Traders entered it, and said an element of reform had been imported, and they were going to add to it in November. lie hoped after the next election to be able to say that they had secured for Warrington such a change in the complexion of the Council as redounded to their everlasting credit and removed from the town the stigma that had so long been 'cast upon It. (Loud applause.) Cheers for Mr. Norbury Williams concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>LATEST ELECTION DETAILS. List of Candidates.</head>
        <p>LATEST ELECTION DETAILS. List of Candidates.</p>
        <p>Saturday next will be the last day for receiving nominations of candidates for the elections on Novem- ber 2nd. Considerable interest is already being manifested in the forthcoming contests, the Non- Trading Committee having announced the names of the gentlemen selected up to date to stand for various wards on their behalf. Councillor John Evans has informed the Conservatives that he does not intend to seek re-election for Howley Ward: The Conservative Committee of Fairfield Ward have asked Councillor Hulse, the retiring representative, to stand again. Mr Hulse has been confined to the house by a serious illness, but on inquiry last night we were pleased to learn that he is slowly improving. It is, however questionable whether he will be able to accede to the? committee's request, as it will probably be some con- siderable time before he regains his usual health. There are two labour candidates in Orford Ward where two seats fall vacant. Mr. Fred Stott is the nominee of the Independent Labour Party and Mr. E. Plinston announced to the Trades and Labour council meeting on Tuesday evening that he had been selected by the Tramway and Vehicle workers to come before the electors of the same ward. The Non-trading com- mittee have, not yet decided upon a definite course of action in the matter. Councillor A. Peacock, one of the retiring representatives, is entering the fEeld at the request of the Liberal Ward Committee. Mr. John Maginn has decided to contest St. John's Ward again. He was adopted as candidate at a meeting of the Workingmen's committee, which is stated to have been a fair representation of both political parties,on Monday evening. Councillor Sampson will seek re- election for Latchford ward. We understand that yesterday Mr. Arthur Browne definitely declined to' stand as the Liberal candidate for St. Austin's or Howley Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>POSITION IN ORFORD WARD.</head>
        <p>THE POSITION IN ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Protest Against Mr. Plinston's Candidature.</p>
        <p>As stated above Mr. E. Plinston intends to make an effort to capture one of the seats in Orford Ward, where Mr. F. Stott has been prosecuting his campaign for a considerable period. We are informed • that the following resolution has been carried by Mr. Stott's Election Committee :—" That as an affiliated body of the Labour Representation Committee, we emphatically protest against 'the proposed candidature of Mr. E. Plinston for the Orford Ward at this juncture." The Committee give as reasons that Mr. E. Plinston's can- didature has not been discussed, nor endorsed by the Labour Representation Committee). That although Chairman of that Committee he has not asked for the opinion of his colleagues, nor taken them into his con- fidence; that he has allowed his name to be publicly mentioned at the Non-Trading meeting in Parr Hall as a probable candidate, knowing that Mr. Stott, whose candidature has been endorsed by the Labour Repre- sentation Committee, the Trades and Labour Council and many Trade Union branches, was already in the field.</p>
        <p>Resolutions in support of Mr. Stott's candidature have been passed by the local branches of the Amal- gamated Society of Engineers, the Amalgamated Union of Enginemen, the General Union of Braziers and Sheet Metal Workers, and the Wheelwrights and Smiths' Society.</p>
        <p>THE CANDIDATES. Wards. Candidates. TOWN HALL Councillor Lord, WHITECROSS Councillor Barton, BEWSEY Councillor W. Pierpoint, }Councillor Peacock, ORFORD Mr. F. Stott, Mr. E. Plinston.</p>
        <p>Sr. JOHN'S 1 Mr. John Ashley, J Mr. John Maginn. ICouncillor Hulse, FAIRFIELD J Mr. J. Povey. HOWLEY Mr. W. Benson. Sr. AUSTIN'S Mr. T. H. Ashcroft. 1_ Councillor Sampson. LATCHFORD Mr. S. B. Stringer. Last Night's Ward Meetings.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Liberal Committee of Town Hall Ward held last evening it was decided to invite Mr. J. Rymer Young, J.P., to contest the ward in oppositionto Councillor Lord.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee for Fairfield Ward was held in Fairfield Schools, but no definite action was decided upon. A well attended meeting of Liberals of Howley Ward was held in Ellesmere-street Mission last evening under the presidency of Mr. W. Clegg. After discussion it was decided on the proposition of Mr. Vose seconded by Mr. Mather "That this meeting of Howley Ward Liberals, considering the non-political manner in which Councillor John Evans has discharged his duties in the Council and the very valuable services he has rendered to the town decides not to oppose him at the forth- coming election."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warrington put up a very poor display last Satur- day against the burly, men of Hunslet, and in conse- quence- had to be. content with sharing the spoil. - The gain therefore was all on the side of the visitors, who last season were well trounced 16-7, and ought to have been sent back defeated on Saturday. Success has perhaps made Warrington Over-confident, and it is certain that, after winning at Broughton, they did not anticipate sacrificing a point at home against the Hunslet team. As things turned out they were some- what fortunate to stave off defeat by Goldthorpe and his merry men, who came very near scoring twice in the second half. Once, the younger Goldthorpe was less than a yard below the bar with a place•kick, from beyond half-way, and on another occasion the forwards rushed the ball over, and the referee fortunately decided that Warrington touched down first.</p>
        <p>There was a fair ',ate of about 4,000, including an enthusiastic few from Hunslet,- accompanied by their " gude wives." These ladies take a deep interest in, the fortunes of the Hundet brigade, and when Jackson scored a soft try as the result of Forster's mull; in the first five minutes, they smiled with a sweetness that mitigated the severeness of the blow to the local occupants of the stand. There was a ding- dong tussle after this but Warrington could not get rid of their awkward foes until they had increased the score by a penalty goal from half-way, placed by " Ahr Albert's long-legged younger brother, Walter. This second reverse acted like a °(-road upon the locals and they simply swarmed over the Hunslet rear-guard. Isherwood and Fish made, a brilliant effort which failed right on the line: Success was not long de- layed, however, for Fish landed a Rinalty goal very neatly,and two minutes later he dashed in at the corner most brilliantly from an opening provided by the resourceful "Danny." Several people behind me yelled—yes, they positively yelled, though ordinarily most staid and sober citizens—and I hope they felt better for the exercise. Personally, I thought my ear-drum had .split, but the apprehension was, luckily, ill-founded. Some folks need a mega- phone to make themselves heard, but there were one or two behind me on Saturday who would have made wireless telegraphy blush.. They possess the true 'Wilderspool whisper—which explains everything.</p>
        <p>The second half was commenced with the sides on equal terms, and the expectation was general that Warrington would rise, to the occasion and display themselves to advantage. Vain expectation ! Was rington seemed to have shot their bolt in that wild dah Just before the interval, and they could hardly raise a gallop afterwards. They just about held their own and that was all. Never once did they flatter their supporters with the hope of a score, Whereas Hunslet very nearly did the trick by sheer power in the packs. It was a dismal second half, full to overflowing with poor punting on one side and good though deadly dull kicking on the other. Isherwood and Fish scarcely touched the ballr though the latter did get possession twice from Hunslet punts, which he calmly returned instead of taking the chance of running for the line or making play of some kind. He was decidedly lazy at this stage of the proceedings, and there is nothing the crowd resents more. " Jack" will be loosing his popularity if he does not bestir himself more in the interest* of his side.</p>
        <p>Isherwood was about the best man oh the home side, and had he been given more work in the second half something tangible might have resulted. Th halves were very poor however, and though collare repeatedly by the Hunslet , for wards they persisted i passing when the men behind had no earthly chanc</p>
        <p>• if ICES Warrington.—Printed and Published by .Toffs' \NIL- &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing VST°l.kgr Bew-3y-street, and published by them at the saany place,and also at The Examiner Offices,Victoria-ro ‘VIDN'ES ; 32, George-street, ST. H.ELF.NS; Marke-t. street, EA.RLESTOWN; Church-street RusconS, Ocr 17TH. 1903.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <p>I never saw a. couple show less resource, and after', their promising display at Broughton the work of Davies And Mahoney was very disappointing. Edmunds would have been much more useful, for be does use his head, and he would have seen the neces- sity for some of those ishort kicks which disconcert three-quarter line lying so close up as were the Hunslet quartette. They smothered all attempts at passing but the Warrington halves persisted in chucking the ball about to no useful purpose. It was Warrington's off-day, for neither forwards nor backs gave more than a glimpse of form.</p>
        <p>Hunslet are a strong, though not a clever side. Ost teams will find them difficult to beat. They are one of the best kicking teams in the League and good boot-work is worth a lot on the defence. It is not very interesting however, and the call of time was welcome enough on Saturday. Praise must be given to the youthful visiting custodian, Place, who played a most resourceful and steady game. Jackson at hall back was the next best man, and he frequently made headway with.strong dashes, while the forwards proved themselves an exceedingly fine lot.</p>
        <p>Had Warrington won, they would have ris.en to thir.d' place, but they are not doing badly with fourth posi- tion. Salford still lead and accounted comfortably for Swinton after a keen game. The surprise of the day was the rout of the Runcornians at home by Hull Kingston Rovers, who are going great guns at present. It is a long time since the " Linnets " and their sup- porters received such a shock. In addition they lost Jolley, who had several ribs broken, and will cer- tainly not appear again yet awhile. Nobody who knows him, however, would dare to suggest that Jiol Jolley's football days are over. He is too plucky and too used to injury to give in while he has a kick left. Tam sure he has the sympathy of the whole League as well as that of his immediate friends.</p>
        <p>The league table up to date is as follows : For. Against. • P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. E Ptø Salford 6... 6... 0... 0...15 22 96... 2 3-13...12. Hull K't'n R 6... 5... 1... 0... 5 10— 40... 3 4-18...10 Hunslet 6... 4... 1... 1... 8 9 43... 5 6-28... 9 Win r'rington 6... 4... 1... 1... 7 9 41... 7 5-29... 9 Ofdham 6. 4. 2 0. 5 11— 43... 3 3-15... g Bradford Runcorn Halifax 6... 4... 2... 0... 4 4 20... 3 3-15... En,3 Leeds 6... 4... 2... 0... 5 7 31... 4 6-26... Br'ghton R 6... 3... 3... 0... 4 11— 41...11 8-46... 6 Hull Leigh 6... 2... 3... 1... 5 6 28... 5 7-31... 0, Swinton Batley - Keighley G... 0... 4... 2... 7 2 20...11 18-76... Widnes 6. 0. 5. 1. 5 3 19...11 14-64.- Wigan -1„ Huddersfield 6... 0... 6... 0... 1 4 14... 8 11-19.-</p>
        <p>The County trial game at Warrington proved an teresting event, particularly as the twelve-aside game was played, and there were about 3,000 spectators present. To the surprise o# everybody the Probables proved immeasurably superior to the Possibles and in the first 30 minutes of play put on no fewer than 28 points, both forwards and backs working with a cohesion which there was no resisting. Such an el" hibition of fast passing, running and scoring 1133 rarely been seen in Rugby football, but it was too one-sided to give a proper indication either of• the merits of the players, or the possibilities of the twelve-aside game. At half-time there was a re-arrangement of the teams, the forwards changing sides, and this levelled matters somewhat, though the same men still proved their superiority. Lomas played: a. remarkable game, his quick dashes through the de" fence being quite unique. So effective was his plaY, that Fish and Hogg, on the Wings had the easiest el journeys to the line, and Wilson at centre was hardlY required. The Salford crack fairly kept Fish bus:4' and the two together demoralised the other side com- pletely. Fish himself accumulated 17 points in the shape of four goals and three tries_, and while he was trying he was seen at his best. He was not a trier throughout, however, in which respect he was inferior to Lomas who did the work of two men. It was quite- evident that the Possibles had no chance of supplant- ing any of the other side from the start, and the Pro - babies were picked en bloc as the County team.</p>
        <p>Fish is thus the only Warrington representative ,- though Dickenson is on the reserve as a wing, a posi- tion he is quite unsuited for. On Monday he was practically idle all day, and finally had to be assisted. off with a nasty gash in his head. Boardman plaYed a hard game-, and along with Buckler, deserves tbe reserve forward position.</p>
        <p>The match proved one thing, and that increased openness of the twelve-aside game and 'diet. possibilities of scoring. Under such conditions guar backs will have endless opportunities and I am c°ll- vinced the twelve-aside game would be a o•od-seout to Warrington. The play is of the sort likely to tenlP pectators, and should the experience of the CoulltY. . games prove satisfactory .I expect to see the rule 9 altered' for next season. Was .thei</p>
        <p>The annual battle between .Widnes and Warriug" ton takes place to-day at Widnes, and the usual griln' struggle may be relied upon. The odds are certainly on. Warrington's side, but the men will have to play ° far different game from that of last Saturday t° annex the full number of points—and those are &amp;Jr' Warrington have picked exactly the same team, though Dickenson is, perhaps, a doubtful starter.</p>
        <p>Harris has been placed on the "A" team IN' Lle trial spin, and Edmunds will be ready next week.</p>
        <p>!Angling. The Warrington District United Anglers' Protec- tion Society, announce the sum to be handed over, as. the result of the Charity Fishitv, match, as 1,26 a most gratifying amount. ..17 new club has -Wel started in the town and will be known as the "Goo' Intent,"with Mr. J. Glover as honorary secretary. Thel join the local association as a matter of course. I" the result of local efforts some useful stocking bail been done to the Bridgewater Canal, and more shortly be done on the same .lines. The consolation match of the Latchford Angling Society on SaturdaY last was a big disappointment, only two. rods catch" ' ing fish. The successful competitors were Mr. rr• Worthington and Mr Thornton. A similar result reported of the . committee match of the Working Men's Angling Association at Acton Bridge, for o.° of about 30 rods only one caught a fish. Warringt°llt, rods at various popular resorts all agree that sP°r • was poor last week-end.</p>
