        <p>------ -- MUNICIPAL ELECTION. 1903. rrO THE ELECTORS OF FAIRFIELD WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, I have been requested to become a candidate to re- present your Ward in the Town Council at the next Municipal Election—and after serious and thoughtful consideration I have consented and now beg respect- fpilly to offer my services and solicit your support and vote. I have bad experience in Municipal work, having been a member of the tcwn council of St. Helens and served on the most important committees and only resigned that position a year after I came to reside in Warrington. lam not a member of any Political organization, nor am I a member of lour local Non-Trading associa- tion, but I am in full sympathy with them in their effort to purge the Town Council of all that is legally or morally wrong and would support them in their policy of Economy and Reform. In moving about your Ward I notize several matters waiting attention Most of your streets are insufficiently lighted, and many of your roads are in such a condition as to cause much annoyance to those people 'who have to use them. andi if selected would lose no time in bringing tha matter to the notice of the responsible committee. I have no axe to grind, and no personal ends to serve, but would deem it an honour to serve you and do all that lies in my power to promote the welfare of my native town. It you honour me with your confidence I will en- deavour to do all I can to serve your interests to the best of my ability. I remain Your Obedient Servant, JAMES POVEY. 11. Springfield-street, Warrington.</p>
