        <p>PASSIVE RESISTANCE. - - - CASES AT LYMM. " Strong Even from a Solicitor." Pour atssive resisters were summoned at Lymm, on Wednesday, for non-payment of balances of their poor rate. The defendants were the Rev. Wilson Eccles, of Swinton; Henry Champ, of Reddish; William Taylor Moss, of Newfield, Lymm ; and George Lawrence Welford of Lymm. The magistrates on the Bench were Messrs. T. P. Norris (chairman), Dr. Bmbazon,the Hon. J. E. Cross,and Admiral Jones Parry. The court was crowded. Mr. Welford, who is a well-known Manchester solici- tor, asked that his case should be taken first, saying he had somelegal objection to take, and if they found him right he was quite sure they wouldnot proceed with the other cases. The Chairman replied that they had simply to administer the law, and, wanted neither speeches nor arguments. Mr. Welford said that if his position was correct they would be compelled to refuse proceeding with the other eases, and the request was granted. The Overseer having admitted that Mr. Welford had paid the whole of the rate with the exception of the part owing to the County Council for education pur- poses, Mr. Welford claimed that this portion was not really for the relief of the poor, as stated on the demand 1 note. The Clerk: The rate has been allowed by the justices Mr. Welford: But I am going into the validity of the rate. The Clerk: Oh, but you can't. 1 Mr. Welford: Yes, I can. I have a right to obj ect and to ask the Bench to state a case if necessary,andthat is perhaps what I shall have to do. In support of his contention, Mr. Welford quoted a case between a Building Society v. The West Ham Churchwardens. The Clerk replied by quoting another case, but Mr. Welford still submitted that the rate was wrongly levied, and that the summons was therefore wrong. It was not a frivolous obj ection, but a legal one, and the practice followed for years was illegal. It was a wicked thing to charge him with not paying the "poor rate," when he had paid every penny of it. Education was paid for now out of public money, but it was a question whether public money should be con- trolled by individuals and not by public authority. To charge him with not having paid his poor rate was a scandal and a shame. It was like obtaining money by false pretences. The Clerk: That is a bit too strong, even from a solicitor. Mr. Welford: A solicitor understands these things, and I say it, and say it emphatically. The Clerk advised the magistrates that they had not the power to inquire into the validity of the rate. Mr. Yates, the County Stipendiary, had ruled in the same 'Tray. and he advised them to support him. The Clerk observed that the duty of the Bench was purely administrative. It was for them to issue a dis- tress warrant, and Mr. Welford could not go into the question whether the rate was valid or not. The Chairman: I think we are quite unanimous on that point. Mr. Welferd said he must accept that ruling, though sorry the Bench took that view of the matter. Beyond what he had said, he objected to the rate on conscienti- ous grounds. A distress warrant was issued. The case of the Rev. Wilson Eccles was next taken, but that gentleman having no defence other than a conscientious objection to the Education Act, the usual order was made, as also in the cases of Mr. Champ and Mc. Moss. Mr. Welford wanted to know why sum- monses had not been issued against two lady resisters, but was informed that their rates had been paid—s statement at which he expressed surprise, though he had to accept it as disposing of the matter so far as the Court was concerned.</p>
