        <p>UNIVERSITY EXTENSION LECTURES AT WARRINGTON. Dante. Mr. Philip H. Wicksteed, M.A., delivered the second of a course of six lectures on " Dante " in the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Wednesday evening. The Lecturer confined himself more particularly to Dante's aims and studies, with special reference to "The Convivio." At the previous lecture, reference was made to some of the factors of Dante's environ- ment, the romantic literature of the period being speci- ally dealt with. The Lecturer now proceeded to speak of the nature and character of the philosophical, scientific and literary studies of Dante. At the outset he called attention to the wider conception o love. The dominating idea of love in the medivral world, he said, was not self-sacrifice, but self- surrender to something higher than themselves. They could trace three dominating forms of this medialval love in Dante's works. In the first place they had his love for Beatrice, secondly his love for wisdom and thirdly his love for God. In the medimval mind there was a close connection between the love of woman, the love of learning and the love of God. Dante had a passion for learning and he sang songs of love to "my lady philosophy." The lecturer proceeded to demonstrate that Dante's studies covered the whole range of the knowledge of his time. He began with the usual studies of grammar (Latin),logic and rhetoric, and passed on to arithmetic geometry (including euclid),[ music and astronomy (including astrology.) Beyond that there was the study of physics and metaphysics, moral philosophy and theology together with the whole range of literature open to him. From Dante's writings it was clear that he had made a close study of Aristotle. In his desire to minister to others, Dante became one of the first University Exten- tioners. (Laughter.) He desired to give to those who had not an opportunity to study, the fruits of his learn- ing, and with that object in view he set to work to write "Convivio." It was to be an encyclopedia of learning and as planned, was to comprise fifteen books. Dante only lived to partially execute the work, and four books only were completed. It was interesting to see the ingenuity with which Dante contrived to introduce all kinds of matter into the book. In the first book, there was a defence of the Italian language adopted for love of it, in the second allegory and history were treated, in the third, philosophy was supreme' and in the fourth, ' true gentlehood was discussed. The lecturer concluded by stating that in the next lecture they would take a rapid account of the political atmosphere with which Dante was surrounded. (Applaube.) THE GOVERNMENT'S SHAME. Dr. Horton, chairman of the Congregational Union in his address to!:the Assembly at Bournemouth on Tuesday said the Government which perpetrated the enormity of the Education Act was overwhelmed with division and confusion. The Government had not sounded the depths of its shame, for it had yet to hear the indignant protest of the Church of England itself as the Church discovered that the Government had brought it into odium with the nation, such as it had not experienced since the days of Archbishop Laud. There was a remarkable scene at the mid-day service for business men on Tuesday at the Central Hall, Man- chester, when the sermon, preached by the Rev. J. G. Greenhough, of Leicester, was interspersed with loud cheering and clapping of hands. LANCASHIRE HIT AT BOTH ENDS. Speaking at a meeting of the Bury Women's Liberal Association on Tuesday night, Mr. George Toulmin, M.P., said that by the proposed fiscal changes, Lanca- shirewould be hit at both ends. Lancashire very largely Lived on imported food, and the Lancashire cotton trade would not get the advantage which was promised. Mr. Chamberlain said the colonies would not arrange for tariffs in the future in order to start industries in competition with the mother country. He did not be- lieve that ; he did not believe t